he carried over 1. 3+1 is 4, plus 1 is 5. So that's 5 here, no carryover.\n\nFinally, the billions place: The first number has a 3, and the second has nothing, so just 3. Adding any carryover? No, since there was none from the previous step. So that's 3 in the billions place.\n\nPutting all the digits together from right to left, the sum should be.. let me check again step by step to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nWait, maybe I should write it out vertically to visualize better:.\n\n      351,943,250.\n    + 170,139,784.\n    ----------------.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 0 +4 =4.\nTens:5+8=13 (carry 1).\nHundreds:2+7+1=10 (carry 1).\nThousands:3+9+1=13 (carry 1).\nTen thousands:4+3+1=8 (no carry).\nHundred thousands:9+1=10 (carry 1).\nMillions:1+0+1=2 (no carry).\nTen millions:5+7=12 (carry 1).\nHundred millions:3+1+1=5.\nBillions:3.\n\nWait, hold on. Let me verify each step again carefully. Maybe I missed a place.\n\nBreaking down each digit position with their place values:.\n\nLet me align both numbers properly:.\n\nFirst number: 3 5 1 9 4 3 2 5 0.\n\nSecond number: 1 7 0 1 3 9 7 8 4.\n\nWait, actually, let's count the digits. 351,943,250 has 9 digits: 3 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nThe second number, 170,139,784 is also 9 digits: 1 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 4 (ones).\n\nWait, maybe I miscounted. Let me write each digit's place:.\n\nFirst number: 3 (billions place? Wait, 351,943,250. Let's parse the commas. In some notations, commas separate every three digits. So 351,943,250 is 3,519,432,50? Wait, no. Wait, actually, 351,943,250 would be 3 hundred fifty-one million.. Wait, actually, commas in numbers can vary by region, but assuming standard US notation where each comma separates thousands, millions, etc. So 351,943,250 is 351 million, 943 thousand, 250. So the digits are:.\n\nBreaking down 351,943,250:.\n\n3 (hundred millions place), 5 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nSimilarly, 170,139,784 is 1 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 4 (ones).\n\nSo when adding:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones):.\n\n0 +4 =4.\n\nTens place:5 +8=13 (write 3, carryover 1).\n\nHundreds:2 +7 +1=10 (write 0, carryover 1).\n\nThousands:3 +9 +1=13 (write 3, carryover 1).\n\nTen thousands:4 +3 +1=8 (write 8, no carryover).\n\nHundred thousands:9 +1=10 (write 0, carryover 1).\n\nMillions place:1 (from first number) +0 (second number's millions place is 0) +1 (carryover) =2 (write 2, no carryover).\n\nTen millions place:5 (first) +7 (second) =12 (write 2, carryover 1).\n\nHundred millions place:3 (first) +1 (second) +1 (carryover) =5 (write 5, no carryover).\n\nBillions place: Only the first number has 3 (since it's 351 million, so the 3 is in the hundred millions, so actually, wait, the first digit is 3 in the hundred millions, so the total digits are up to hundred millions. Wait, so actually, the first number is 351,943,250 which is 3 (hundred millions), so the highest place is hundred millions, so there is no billions digit here. The second number is 170,139,784 which is 1 (hundred millions). So adding those, the hundred millions place is 3 +1 + any carryover. Wait, but in the previous step, the hundred millions place was 3+1+1 (carryover from ten millions). Wait, let's re-express the addition step by step:.\n\nLet me write them aligned:.\n\n          3 5 1 9 4 3 2 5 0.\n        + 1 7 0 1 3 9 7 8 4.\n        -----------------------.\n\nStarting from the right (ones):.\n\n0 +4 =4 → ones digit:4.\n\nTens:5+8=13 → 3, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:2 +7 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nThousands:3 +9 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:4 +3 +1=8 →8, no carryover.\n\nHundred thousands:9 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nMillions:1 (from first number) +0 (second's millions digit is 0) +1 (carryover) →2, no carryover.\n\nTen millions:5 (first's ten millions) +7 (second's ten millions) =12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:3 (first's hundred millions) +1 (second's hundred millions) +1 (carryover) →5 →5.\n\nSo putting it all together, the sum is:.\n\nStarting from the highest digit:.\n\nHundred millions:5.\n\nTen millions:2.\n\nMillions:2.\n\nHundred thousands:0.\n\nTen thousands:8.\n\nThousands:3.\n\nHundreds:0.\n\nTens:3.\n\nOnes:4.\n\nWait, but let's arrange all digits properly:.\n\nThe digits from highest to lowest (billions place? Wait, but both numbers are up to hundred millions. So the highest is hundred millions place, so the total sum would be 5 (hundred millions), then ten millions 2, millions 2, etc.\n\nWait, let me write the digits in order:.\n\nThe sum's digits are:.\n\nHundred millions place:5.\n\nTen millions place:2.\n\nMillions place:2.\n\nHundred thousands place:0.\n\nTen thousands place:8.\n\nThousands place:3.\n\nHundreds place:0.\n\nTens place:3.\n\nOnes place:4</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
