e numbers properly aligned:.\n\n      280,983,056.\n    +418,768,450.\n    -------------.\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 6 +0 =6.\n\nTens:5 +5=10 (carry 1).\n\nHundreds:0 +4 +1=5.\n\nThousands:3 +8=11 (carry 1).\n\nTen thousands:8 +6 +1=15 (carry 1).\n\nHundred thousands:9 +7 +1=17 (carry 1).\n\nMillions:0 +8 +1=9.\n\nTen millions:8 +6=14 (carry 1).\n\nHundred millions:2 +4 +1=7.\n\nWait, the ten millions place: the digits from the first number is 8 (since 280,983,056: the digits are 2,8,0 in the first three, then comma, so 9,8,3,0,56. Wait maybe I need to check the digit positions again. Let me parse each number digit by digit.\n\nWait, maybe breaking down the numbers:.\n\n280,983,056: .\n\nDigits from right to left (positions 1 to 9):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):6.\n\n2:5 (tens).\n\n3:0 (hundreds).\n\n4:3 (thousands).\n\n5:8 (ten thousands).\n\n6:9 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:0 (millions).\n\n8:8 (ten millions).\n\n9:2 (hundred millions).\n\nSimilarly, 418,768,450:.\n\nPositions 1:0 (ones).\n\n2:5 (tens).\n\n3:4 (hundreds).\n\n4:8 (thousands).\n\n5:6 (ten thousands).\n\n6:7 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:1 (millions).\n\n8:4 (ten millions).\n\n9:4 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the commas. Let me write each number with commas properly:.\n\n280,983,056 is 2 8 0 , 9 8 3 , 0 5 6. So splitting into digits:.\n\nHundred millions:2.\n\nTen millions:8.\n\nMillions:0.\n\nHundreds of thousands:9.\n\nTen thousands:8.\n\nThousands:3.\n\nHundreds:0.\n\nTens:5.\n\nOnes:6.\n\nWait, actually, in the standard format, commas are placed every three digits. So 280,983,056:.\n\nFirst comma after three digits from the right: 280,983,056 is 280,983,056. Wait, the first comma separates millions, so:.\n\n280 (hundred millions, ten millions, millions), then 983 (hundred thousands, ten thousands, thousands), then 056 (hundreds, tens, ones). So yes, the digits are:.\n\nHundred millions: 2.\n\nTen millions:8.\n\nMillions:0.\n\nHundred thousands:9.\n\nTen thousands:8.\n\nThousands:3.\n\nHundreds:0.\n\nTens:5.\n\nOnes:6.\n\nSimilarly, 418,768,450 is 418,768,450:.\n\nHundred millions digit:4.\n\nTen millions:1.\n\nMillions:8.\n\nHundred thousands:7.\n\nTen thousands:6.\n\nThousands:8.\n\nHundreds:4.\n\nTens:5.\n\nOnes:0.\n\nWait, let me check that again. 418,768,450:.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\n4 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 8 (millions),.\n\nthen comma, 7 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands),.\n\nthen comma, 4 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 0 (ones). Yes.\n\nSo adding digit by digit:.\n\nStarting from the ones place (rightmost):.\n\nOnes: 6 +0 =6.\n\nTens:5 +5=10 (carryover 1).\n\nHundreds:0 +4 +1 (carry) =5.\n\nThousands:3 +8=11 (carryover 1).\n\nTen thousands:8 +6 +1 (carry)=15 (carryover1).\n\nHundred thousands:9 +7 +1=17 (carryover1).\n\nMillions:0 +8 +1 (carry?) Wait, millions place?.\n\nWait, the millions digit in first number is 0 (since millions place is the third from the left in the first part: 280, so the millions digit is 0). The second number's millions digit is 8 (from 418 in the hundred millions, ten millions, millions). So 0 +8 =8 plus any carryover?.\n\nWait, let's track the positions step by step:.\n\nLet me list each digit position for both numbers:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):.\n\nNumber1:6.\n\nNumber2:0.\n\nSum:6+0=6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):.\n\n5 +5=10 → write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):.\n\n0 +4 +1 (carry)=5 → write 5, no carryover.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\n3 (from first number) +8 (from second) =11 → write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\n8 +6 +1 (carry) =15 → write 5, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\n9 +7 +1 (carry) =17 → write7, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\n0 (from first number's millions digit) +8 (second's millions digit) +1 (carryover) =9 → write9, no carryover.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\nFirst number:8 (ten millions digit) + second number's ten millions digit is 1 (since 418, so ten millions is 1). Wait, wait, the second 