ions)\n\n9:7 (hundred millions)\n\n10:0 (billions, since it's only 7 hundred million, so the billions digit is 0?)\n\nWait, wait, 713,552,229:\n\nThe first comma is after three digits from the right: 713,552,229 → 713,552,229. Wait actually, in standard notation, commas are placed every three digits starting from the right. So 713,552,229 is:\n\n713,552,229 → 713 (millions), 552 (thousands), 229.\n\nSo breaking down:\n\nBillions place: 0 (since it's less than a billion)\n\nHundred millions:7 (since 700,000,000)\n\nTen millions:1 (10,000,000)\n\nMillions:3 (3,000,000)\n\nHundred thousands:5 (500,000)\n\nTen thousands:5 (50,000)\n\nThousands:2 (2,000)\n\nHundreds:2 (200)\n\nTens:2 (20)\n\nOnes:9\n\nSo in digit positions (from right, 1 to 10):\n\n1:9 (ones)\n\n2:2 (tens)\n\n3:2 (hundreds)\n\n4:2 (thousands)\n\n5:5 (ten thousands)\n\n6:5 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:3 (millions)\n\n8:1 (ten millions)\n\n9:7 (hundred millions)\n\n10:0 (billions)\n\nWait, so the second number's billions digit (position 10) is 0.\n\nSo when adding, the first number has 8 in the billions place (position10), second has 0, so adding those gives 8+0 + any carryover.\n\nWait, let me redo the digit-by-digit addition carefully.\n\nStarting from digit1 (ones):\n\nDigit1: 9 +9=18 → 8, carryover1\n\nDigit2 (tens):9 +2=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):9+2=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1\n\nDigit4 (thousands):8+2=10 +1=11 →1, carryover1\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):0 +5=5 +1=6 →6, carryover0\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):8 +5=13 →3, carryover1\n\nDigit7 (millions):3 +3=6 +1=7 →7, carryover0\n\nDigit8 (ten millions):8 +1=9 →9, carryover0\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):3 +7=10 →0, carryover1\n\nDigit10 (billions):8 +0=8 +1 (carryover)=9 →9, carryover0?\n\nWait, but if we have a carryover from digit9 (hundred millions), which was 3+7=10, so we write 0 and carryover1 to the next digit (billions). Then, adding the billions digits: first number has 8, second 0, plus carryover1 →8+0+1=9. So digit10 is 9, no furth