g into digits (left to right):.\n\nDigit 1 (hundred millions place): 6.\n\nDigit 2 (ten millions):3.\n\nDigit3 (millions):7.\n\nDigit4 (hundred thousands):2.\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):6.\n\nDigit6 (thousands):9.\n\nDigit7 (hundreds):7.\n\nDigit8 (tens):7.\n\nDigit9 (ones):7.\n\nWait, no. Wait the number is 637,269,777. Let me parse each digit step by step:.\n\nThe number is 6 3 7 , 2 6 9 , 7 7 7.\n\nSo commas after every three digits starting from the right. So:.\n\nFirst part: 637 (million).\n\nSecond part: 269 (thousand).\n\nThird part: 777 (units).\n\nSo the digits from left to right:.\n\nDigit positions (from left):.\n\n1: 6 (hundred millions).\n\n2:3 (ten millions).\n\n3:7 (millions).\n\n4:2 (hundred thousands).\n\n5:6 (ten thousands).\n\n6:9 (thousands).\n\n7:7 (hundreds).\n\n8:7 (tens).\n\n9:7 (ones).\n\nSecond number:501,286,598.\n\nDigits:.\n\n5 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions),1 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands),8 (ten thousands),6 (thousands),5 (hundreds),9 (tens),8 (ones).\n\nSo when adding, each digit place must be aligned:.\n\nLet me write them vertically aligned by place:.\n\nHundred millions:6 and5.\n\nTen millions:3 and0.\n\nMillions:7 and1.\n\nHundred thousands:2 and2.\n\nTen thousands:6 and8.\n\nThousands:9 and6.\n\nHundreds:7 and5.\n\nTens:7 and9.\n\nOnes:7 and8.\n\nSo adding each column from right to left (starting with ones):.\n\nLet me list each column's digits:.\n\n1. Ones:7 +8 =15 →5, carryover1.\n\n2. Tens:7 +9 =16 +1=17 →7, carryover1.\n\n3. Hundreds:7 +5=12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nWait, wait, in hundreds place: first number's hundreds digit is 7, second is5 (from 501,286,598: the hundreds digit is 5). So 7+5=12? Wait no, 7+5 is 12? Wait 7+5 is 12? Wait 7+5 is 12? Wait, 7+5 is 12? Wait no, 7+5 is 12? No, 7+5 is 12? Wait 7+5=12? Wait 7+5 is 12? Wait no, 7+5 is 12? Wait 7+5 is 12? Wait 7+5 is 12? Wait, 7+5 is 12? Wait 7+5 is 12? Wait that's correct. 7+5 is 12. Then plus the carryover1 makes 13. So write 3, carryover1.\n\nWait, but the hundreds place: first number's hundreds digit is 7 (from 777 in the first number) and second number's hundreds digit is 5 (from 598). So 7 +5 =12, plus 1 carried over is 13. So yes, 3 with carryover1.\n\nContinuing:.\n\n4. Thousands place: first number's thousands digit is9, second is6. 9+6=15, plus 1=16 →6, carryover1.\n\n5. Ten thousands place: first's 6 and second's8. 6+8=14, plus1=15 →5, carryover1.\n\n6. Hundred thousands place: first's2 and second's2. 2+2=4, plus1=5 →5, carryover0.\n\n7. Millions place: first's7 and second's1. 7+1=8 →8, no carryover.\n\n8. Ten millions place: first's3 and second's0. 3+0=3 →3, no carryover.\n\n9. Hundred millions place: first's6 and second's5. 6+5=11 →11. Since this is the highest place, we just write 11 here.\n\nPutting all together from right to left, the digits are:.\n\nOnes:5.\n\nTens:7.\n\nHundreds:3.\n\nThousands:6.\n\nTen thousands:5.\n\nHundred thousands:5.\n\nMillions:8.\n\nTen millions:3.\n\nHundred millions:11.\n\nWait, but the hundred millions place sum is 11, so that's actually 1 (billion) and 1 (hundred million). So the total digits would be:.\n\nStarting from the highest, the 11 in hundred millions would make it 1 billion and 1 hundred million, so we have to carry over 1 to a new billions place.\n\nWait, so actually, the hundred millions place digits sum to 6+5=11. So when adding, the hundreds of millions place's sum is 11, so we have to write down 1 in the hundred millions place and carry over 1 to the billions place. But since there was no billions digit before, this creates a new digit at the beginning.\n\nTherefore, the total sum would be:.\n\nBillions place:1.\n\nHundred millions:1.\n\nTen millions:3.\n\nMillions:8.\n\nHundred thousands:5.\n\nTen thousands:5.\n\nThousands:6.\n\nHundreds:3.\n\nTens:7.\n\nOnes:5.\n\nSo putting it all together:.\n\n1 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands),5 (ten thousands),6 (thousands),3 (hundreds),7 (tens),5 (ones)</think>\nTher