\n\nPosition4:7,\n\nPosition3:0,\n\nPosition2:1,\n\nPosition1:8.\n\nThus the number is 927,297,018.\n\nWait, but in the alternate method, I had 927,297,188. So why the difference?\n\nAh, perhaps I made a mistake in the alternate method's breakdown.\n\nLet me recalculate the alternate method properly.\n\nFirst number:369,074,769.\n\nSecond number:558,222,419.\n\nBreaking them into parts:\n\nFirst number:\n\n369,000,000 + 74,769.\n\nSecond number:\n\n558,000,000 + 222,419.\n\nAdding the millions:369,000,000 +558,000,000 = 927,000,000.\n\nAdding the smaller parts:74,769 +222,419.\n\nCalculating 74,769 +222,419:\n\n74,769 +200,000 = 274,769.\n\n274,769 +22,419 = 297,188.\n\nSo total sum is 927,000,000 +297,188 = 927,297,188.\n\nHmm, so there is a discrepancy between the two methods. The digit-by-digit gives 927,297,018 versus the other method gives 927,297,188. The difference is in the hundreds, tens, and ones places.\n\nWait, let me check the digit-by-digit again for the hundreds, tens, and ones places.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\nFirst number's hundreds digit:7.\n\nSecond number's hundreds digit:2.\n\n7 +2 =9, plus any carryover?\n\nWait in position3, after position2 had a carryover of1.\n\nWait let me go step by step again for the first few positions.\n\nPosition1 (ones):9+9=18 \u21928, carryover1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):6 (first) +4 (second) =10 +1 (carryover) \u219211 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):7 (first) +2 (second)=9 + the carryover1 \u219210. So the hundreds digit is0, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4 (first) +2 (second)=6 + carryover1 \u21927, carryover0.\n\nSo position4 is7.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7 +2 =9.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0+2=2.\n\nPosition7 (millions):9+8=17 \u21927 carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):6+5=11 +1 \u219212 \u21922 carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):3+5=8 +1 \u21929.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nPosition9:9,\n\nPosition8:2,\n\nPosition7:7,\n\nPosition6:2,\n\nPosition5:9,\n\nPosition4:7,\n\nPosition3:0,\n\nPosition2:1,\n\nPosition1:8.\n\nTherefore, the number is:\n\n9 (hundred millions),\n\n2 (ten millions),\n\n7 (millions),\n\n2 (hundred thousands),\n\n9 (ten thousands),\n\n7 (thousands),\n\n0 (hundreds),\n\n1 (tens),\n\n8 (ones).\n\nSo written as 927,297,018.\n\nBut the alternate method says 927,297,188. The difference is in the last three digits: 018 vs 188.\n\nAh! Wait, in the alternate method, the smaller parts were 74,769 +222,419.\n\nWait let's calculate that again carefully:\n\n74,769 +222,419:\n\nLet's add the thousands:\n\n74,769 is 74 thousand 769,\n\n222,419 is 222 thousand 419.\n\nAdding them:\n\n74,769 + 222,419:\n\nStart with the thousands:\n\n74,000 + 222,000 = 296,000.\n\nThen the remaining parts:769 +419 = 1,188.\n\nSo total is 296,000 +1,188 =297,188. So that's correct.\n\nSo when adding to the millions part (927,000,000), it's 927,297,188.\n\nBut the digit-by-digit gives 927,297,018. So there's a discrepancy of 170.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the digit-by-digit calculation in the hundreds place?\n\nWait let me check again the digit-by-digit for the hundreds place:\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\nFirst number's hundreds digit is7,\n\nSecond number's hundreds digit is2,\n\nPlus carryover from position2, which was 1 (from the tens place addition).\n\nSo 7 +2 +1 =10, so digit0 with carryover1.\n\nThen position4 (thousands):\n\nFirst number's thousands digit is4,\n\nSecond's is2,\n\nPlus carryover1 \u21924+2+1=7, no carryover.\n\nSo thousands digit is7.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\nFirst's ten thousands digit is7,\n\nSecond's is2,\n\nSo 7+2=9, no carryover.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n0+2=2.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n9+8=17 \u21927 carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n6+5 +1=12 \u21922 carryover1.\n\nPosition9:3+5+1=9.\n\nSo the digits are as before, leading to 927,297,018.\n\nWait, but according to the alternate method, it should be 927,297,188. There's a difference in the last three digits: 018 vs 188.\n\nAh! Wait a minute, perhaps I messed up the digit positions in the digit-by-digit approach. Let me check again the positions.\n\nWait, when writing the digits from position1 (ones) to position9 (hundred millions), the digits are as follows:\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):9,\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):2,\n\nPosition7 (millions):7,\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):2,\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):9,\n\nPosition4 (thousands):7,\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):0,\n\nPosition2 (tens):1,\n\nPosition1 (ones):8.\n\nSo the number is:\n\n9 (hundred millions),\n\n2 (ten millions),\n\n7 (millions),\n\n2 (hundred thousands),\n\n9 (ten thousands),\n\n7 (thousands),\n\n0 (hundreds),\n\n1 (tens),\n\n8 (ones).\n\nSo when written out, that's:\n\n927,297,018.\n\nWait, but the alternate method gives 927,297,188. The difference is in the last three digits: 018 vs 188. So why the discrepancy?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the alternate method's breakdown of the numbers.\n\nWait let me recheck the numbers:\n\nFirst number is 369,074,769.\n\nBreaking it down:\n\n369,074,769:\n\n- 369 million,\n\n- 074 thousand,\n\n- 769.\n\nWait, actually, 074,769 is 74,769.\n\nSecond number:558,222,419:\n\n-558 million,\n\n-222 thousand,\n\n-419.\n\nSo adding 74,769 +222,419:\n\n74,769 +222,419:\n\nLet me do it step by step:\n\n74,769\n\n+222,419\n\n= ?\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:9+9=18 \u21928, carryover1.\n\nTens:6+1=7 +1=8 \u21928, carryover0.\n\nHundreds:7+4=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nThousands:4+2=6 +1=7 \u21927, carryover0.\n\nTen thousands:7+2=9.\n\nHundred thousands:0+2=2.\n\nMillions:0+0 (since it's 222,419 has only 222 thousands and no millions beyond that? Wait no, actually:\n\nWait 222,419 is 222 thousand, so in the hundred thousands place, it's 2 (since 222,419 is 2 hundred thousands, 22 thousand? Wait, no:\n\nWait, 222,419 is 2 hundred thousands, 22 thousands, 419. So:\n\nBreaking down 222,419:\n\n2 (hundred thousands),\n\n2 (ten thousands),\n\n2 (thousands),\n\n4 (hundreds),\n\n1 (tens),\n\n