),5 (10M),8 (M),2 (100k),2 (10k),2 (k),2 (h),4 (t),9 (o).\n\nNow, adding column by column from right to left:\n\nOnes column (position1):\n\n9 +9 =18 \u21928, carryover1.\n\nTens column (position2):\n\n6 (first) +4 (second)=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nHundreds column (position3):\n\n7 (first) +2 (second)=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nThousands column (position4):\n\n4 (first) +2 (second)=6 +1=7 \u21927, carryover0.\n\nTen thousands column (position5):\n\n7 (first) +2 (second)=9 \u21929, carryover0.\n\nHundred thousands column (position6):\n\n0 (first) +2 (second)=2 \u21922, carryover0.\n\nMillions column (position7):\n\n9 (first) +8 (second)=17 \u21927, carryover1.\n\nTen millions column (position8):\n\n6 (first) +5 (second)=11 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions column (position9):\n\n3 (first) +5 (second)=8 +1=9 \u21929, carryover0.\n\nThus the digits from left to right are:\n\n9 (hundred millions),\n\n2 (ten millions),\n\n7 (millions),\n\n2 (hundred thousands),\n\n9 (ten thousands),\n\n7 (thousands),\n\n0 (hundreds),\n\n1 (tens),\n\n8 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 927,297,018. But the alternate method gives 927,297,188.\n\nWait a second! In the thousands place, the digit-by-digit says 7, but in the alternate method, the thousands place is part of the 297,188. Let's see:\n\nThe alternate method's 297,188: breaking down:\n\n297,188:\n\n2 (hundred thousands),\n\n9 (ten thousands),\n\n7 (thousands),\n\n1 (hundreds),\n\n8 (tens),\n\n8 (ones).\n\nWait, so the thousands digit is7, which matches the digit-by-digit result. But the hundreds digit is1 vs 0 in the digit-by-digit. That's the discrepancy. The digit-by-digit has 0 in the hundreds place, but the alternate method's addition has 1 in hundreds and 8 in tens and ones.\n\nWait, let's look at the alternate method's addition of 74,769 and222,419 again:\n\n74,769:\n\n7 (ten thousands),\n\n4 (thousands),\n\n7 (hundreds),\n\n6 (tens),\n\n9 (ones).\n\n222,419:\n\n2 (hundred thousands),\n\n2 (ten thousands),