dard place values:.\n\nActually, standard place values from right:.\nPosition 1 (units): 0.\nPosition 2 (tens):8.\nPosition3 (hundreds):4.\nPosition4 (thousands):3.\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7.\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):7.\nPosition7 (millions):8.\nPosition8 (ten millions):7.\nPosition9 (hundred millions):3.\n\nWait, maybe I'm getting confused. Let me write the number 387,773,480 as digits:.\n\nDigits from right to left (positions 1 to 9):.\n\nPosition 1 (units): 0.\nPosition 2 (tens):8.\nPosition3 (hundreds):4.\nPosition4 (thousands):3.\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7.\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):7.\nPosition7 (millions):7.\nPosition8 (ten millions):8.\nPosition9 (hundred millions):3.\n\nWait, that might not be right. Let me count:.\n\n387,773,480:.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nStarting from the left, the first digit is 3, then 8,7,7,7,3,4,8,0. Wait, commas are placed every three digits. So 387,773,480 is 3 8 7 , 7 7 3 , 4 8 0. So:.\n\nDigits:.\n\nBillions place: 3 (since it's a 9-digit number, the first digit is hundred millions place? Wait, 387,773,480 is 3 hundred million (since 100,000,000 x3=300,000,000), plus 87 million. So the digits are:.\n\n3 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions),7 (millions),7 (hundred thousands),7 (ten thousands),3 (thousands),4 (hundreds),8 (tens),0 (units).\n\nSo written out:.\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 1):.\n\nPosition 1: 0 (units).\nPosition 2:8 (tens).\nPosition3:4 (hundreds).\nPosition4:3 (thousands).\nPosition5:7 (ten thousands).\nPosition6:7 (hundred thousands).\nPosition7:7 (millions).\nPosition8:8 (ten millions).\nPosition9:3 (hundred millions).\n\nSimilarly, the second number is 915,094,797.\n\nBreaking that down:.\n\nDigits: 9 (hundred millions),1 (ten millions),5 (millions),0 (hundred thousands),9 (ten thousands),4 (thousands),7 (hundreds),9 (tens),7 (units).\n\nSo positions:.\n\nPosition1 (units):7.\nPosition2 (tens):9.\nPosition3 (hundreds):7.\nPosition4 (thousands):4.\nPosition5 (ten thousands):9.\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0.\nPosition7 (millions):5.\nPosition8 (ten millions):1.\nPosition9 (hundred millions):9.\n\nOkay, now let's add each position step by step, starting from position1 to position9, keeping track of carry-over.\n\nStarting with Position1 (units):.\n\nFirst number:0, second number:7. Sum:0+7=7. Write down 7. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition2 (tens):.\n\nFirst:8, second:9. 8+9=17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):.\n\nFirst:4, second:7. 4+7=11. Plus carryover 1 → 12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\nFirst:3, second:4. 3+4=7. Plus carryover 1 →8. Write 8, carryover 0.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\nFirst:7, second:9. 7+9=16. Write 6, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\nFirst:7, second:0. 7+0=7. Plus carryover1 →8. Write8, carryover0.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\nFirst:7, second:5. 7+5=12. Write2, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\nFirst:8, second:1. 8+1=9. Plus carryover1 →10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\nFirst:3, second:9. 3+9=12. Plus carryover1 →13. Write3, carryover1. But since this is the highest position, the carryover becomes a new digit at the beginning.\n\nWait, so the total sum would have an extra digit here. Let me see:.\n\nWait, Position9 (hundred millions) sum: 3+9+1 (carryover from Position8) =13. So we write down 3 and carryover 1. Since there are no more digits beyond Position9, that carryover 1 becomes a new digit (billions place). So the digits from the addition are:.\n\nStarting from Position1 to Position9 plus the extra:.\n\nBillions place:1 (from the carryover).\n\nThen Position9:3 (hundred millions).\n\nPosition8:0 (ten millions).\n\nPosition7:2 (millions).\n\nPosition6:8 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition5:6 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition4:8 (thousands).\n\nPosition3:2 (hundreds).\n\nPosition2:7 (tens).\n\nPosition1:7 (units)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
