eir respective positions). 7+9=16 \u21926, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\nFirst:7, second:0. 7+0=7 +1 (carryover)=8 \u2192 correct.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\nFirst:7, second:5. 7+5=12 \u21922 carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst:8, second:1. 8+1=9 +1 (carryover)=10 \u21920 carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst:3, second:9. 3+9=12 +1 (carryover)=13 \u21923 carryover1.\n\nThen the carryover1 adds a new digit at the billions place. So the total is 1,302,868,277.\n\nHmm, let me add the numbers another way to confirm. Maybe adding them as whole numbers:\n\n387,773,480 + 915,094,797.\n\nLet me add the millions first:\n\n387,773,480 is 387,773,480.\n\n915,094,797 is 915,094,797.\n\nAdding them:\n\nStart with 387,773,480 + 900,000,000 = 1,287,773,480.\n\nThen add the remaining 15,094,797.\n\n1,287,773,480 +15,094,797.\n\nAdding 15,000,000 gives 1,302,773,480.\n\nThen add 94,797: 1,302,773,480 +94,797 = ?\n\n1,302,773,480 + 90,000 = 1,302,863,480.\n\nThen +4,797: 1,302,863,480 +4,797 = 1,302,868,277.\n\nYes, that matches the previous result. So the final answer is 1,302,868,277.\n\nI think that's correct. So the reasoning steps would follow digit by digit addition with the carries as I outlined earlier. The key is to ensure each digit place is added correctly and carryovers are properly handled, especially in the higher places where an extra digit might be introduced.\n</think>\n\n",
