messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 649424786 and 792873169?\n# Reasoning:", "<think>\nOkay, let's see. I need to add 649,424,786 and 792,873,169. Hmm, adding large numbers can be tricky, but I'll take it step by step. First, I should write them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly by their place values. Let me visualize that.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The first number ends with 6, and the second ends with 9. Adding those gives 6 + 9 = 15. So I write down 5 and carry over 1 to the tens place.\n\nNext, the tens place. The first number has 8 and the second has 6. Adding those plus the carryover: 8 + 6 = 14, plus 1 makes 15. Again, write 5 and carry over 1.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place. The digits are 7 and 1. 7 + 1 = 8, plus the carried 1 equals 9. So that's 9 in the hundreds place, no carryover here.\n\nThousands place: 4 and 3. 4 + 3 = 7. No carryover, so just 7 there.\n\nTen-thousands place: 2 and 7. 2 + 7 = 9. Straightforward, so 9.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 4 and 8. 4 + 8 = 12. Wait, so write 2 and carry over 1.\n\nMillions place: The first number has 9 and the second has 2. 9 + 2 = 11, plus the carried 1 makes 12. So write 2 and carry over 1 again.\n\nTen-millions place: 4 and 9. 4 + 9 = 13, plus the 1 carried over is 14. So 4 here and carry over 1.\n\nHundred-millions place: 6 and 7. 6 + 7 = 13, plus the 1 gives 14. So write 4 and carry over 1.\n\nBillions place: There's only the carried over 1, so that becomes the next digit. .\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right, the digits we got were 5 (ones), 5 (tens), 9 (hundreds), 7 (thousands), 9 (ten-thousands), 2 (hundred-thousands), 2 (millions), 4 (ten-millions), 4 (hundred-millions), and the 1 in the billions place. .\n\nWait, let me check that again. Maybe I messed up a place. Let me recount the positions step by step properly. Maybe writing it out vertically would help, but since I can't, I'll have to be careful.\n\nLet me list each digit position by position:.\n\nFirst number: 6 4 9 4 2 4 7 8 6.\n\nSecond number:7 9 2 8 7 3 1 6 9.\n\nWait, actually, wait, the numbers are 649,424,786 and 792,873,169. Let me break them into digits properly.\n\nBreaking down each number:.\n\nFirst number: 6 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nWait, actually, maybe it's better to write each number's digits from right to left with their positions:.\n\nLet me list the digits starting from the ones place as position 1:.\n\nNumber1: 6 (position1), 8 (2), 7 (3), 4 (4), 2 (5), 4 (6), 9 (7), 4 (8), 6 (9).\n\nWait, no, that's not right. Let me actually write out the number 649,424,786 properly:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 6.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 8.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):7.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):2.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):4.\n\nPosition7 (millions):9.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):4.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):6.\n\nSimilarly, the second number: 792,873,169.\n\nPosition1 (ones):9.\n\nPosition2 (tens):6.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):8.\n\nPosition7 (millions):2.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):9.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):7.\n\nPosition10 (billions): there's a 7 in the hundred millions place, so the second number is 9 digits as well, so both numbers have 9 digits, so when added, the maximum would be 10 digits.\n\nWait, actually, 792,873,169 is 9 digits: 7 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 2 (millions), etc. So both numbers are 9 digits. So when adding, we can proceed digit by digit from the right, which is position1 to position9, and carry over as needed.\n\nLet me start over with the addition step by step, digit by digit, keeping track of the carryover.\n\nStarting with position1 (ones):.\n\nNumber1:6, Number2:9. 6 +9 =15. Write down 5, carryover 1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):.\n\nNumber1:8, Number2:6. 8+6=14. Plus carryover1=15. Write 5, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):.\n\nNumber1:7, Number2:1. 7+1=8. +1=9. Write9, carryover0.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\nNumber1:4, Number2:3. 4+3=7. +0=7. Write7, carryover0.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\nNumber1:2, Number2:7. 2+7=9. Write9, carryover0.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\nNumber1:4, Number2:8. 4+8=12. Write2, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\nNumber1:9, Number2:2. 9+2=11. +1=12. Write2, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\nNumber1:4, Number2:9. 4+9=13. +1=14. Write4, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\nNumber1:6, Number2:7. 6+7=13. +1=14. Write4, carryover1.\n\nNow, after all positions, there's a carryover of 1 remaining. So we add a new digit to the left (billions place) which is 1.\n\nPutting all the digits together from last to first (since we started from the right):.\n\nThe digits we wrote, starting from position1 (ones) to position9 (hundred millions), plus the final carryover:.\n\nThe digits from right to left (positions 1 to 9) are:.\n\nPosition1:5.\n\nPosition2:5.\n\nPosition3:9.\n\nPosition4:7.\n\nPosition5:9.\n\nPosition6:2.\n\nPosition7:2.\n\nPosition8:4.\n\nPosition9:4.\n\nPlus the carryover 1 as the next digit (position10: billions).\n\nSo when written in order from left to right (billions down to ones):.\n\n1 (billions), 4 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nWait, hold on, maybe I need to arrange the digits properly. Let's see:.\n\nThe final digits after each position:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (position1) to the left:.\n\nThe digits are:.\n\nPosition1 (ones):5.\n\nPosition2 (tens):5.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):7.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):9.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):2.\n\nPosition7 (millions):2.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):4.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):4.\n\nPlus the carryover 1 at the billions place (position10).\n\nSo the number is:.\n\n1 (billions), then the hundred millions digit is 4, ten millions 4, millions 2, hundred thousands 2, ten thousands 9, thousands7, hundreds9, tens5, ones5.\n\nWait, arranging them properly:.\n\nBillions place:1.\n\nHundred millions:4.\n\nTen millions:4.\n\nMillions:2.\n\nHundred thousands:2.\n\nTen thousands:9.\n\nThousands:7.\n\nHundreds:9.\n\nTens:5.\n\nOnes:5</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
