s:6 +7=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nMillions:6 +0=6 +1=7 \u21927, carryover0.\n\nTen millions:4 (first) +8 (second)=12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:2 +1=3 +1=4 \u21924, carryover0.\n\nNow compiling the results from ones to hundred millions:\n\nOnes:0  \nTens:5  \nHundreds:4  \nThousands:4  \nTen thousands:9  \nHundred thousands:3  \nMillions:7  \nTen millions:2  \nHundred millions:4  \n\nSo the sum is:\n\nHundred millions:4, Ten millions:2, Millions:7, Hundred thousands:3, Ten thousands:9, Thousands:4, Hundreds:4, Tens:5, Ones:0.\n\nPutting it together: 4 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands),4 (thousands),4 (hundreds),5 (tens),0 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 427,394,450.\n\nAh! So earlier mistake was in the first method when I considered the digits from the first number's hundred millions as 2 and second's as1, but when adding the ten millions place, I had 4 (first) +8 (second)=12, so that gives 2 in ten millions and carryover1 to hundred millions. So the hundred millions becomes 2+1 +1 (carryover)? Wait let's recheck:\n\nWait, the Ten millions place addition:\n\nFirst number's ten millions is4, second's is8: 4+8=12 \u2192 write 2, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions place: first's is2, second's is1 \u21922+1=3 plus carryover1 \u21924. So yes.\n\nTherefore the correct sum is 427,394,450.\n\nSo where was the first method's error?\n\nIn the first digit-by-digit approach, I incorrectly assigned the digits. Let's see:\n\nIn the first method, I had:\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions): 2+1 +1 (carryover from position8) \u21924.\n\nBut in the correct breakdown:\n\nThe ten millions place (position8 in the digit numbering from right) was:\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is4 (since from left, ten millions is the second digit, so from the right it's the 8th digit). The second number's ten millions is8 (from left it's the second digit, from right it's 8th digit? Wait, perhaps my confusion was in numbering the positio