,704 +27,746 =427,394,450.\n\nAh! So the second method gives 427,394,450. So the first method must be wrong.\n\nWhere did I go wrong in the first method?\n\nLet me retrace the first digit-by-digit addition.\n\nStarting with the numbers as:\n\nFirst number: 2 4 6 6 6 6 7 0 4 (hundred millions to ones)\n\nSecond number:1 8 0 7 2 7 7 4 6 (same order)\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the digit positions.\n\nWait let's write them out digit by digit in the same order:\n\nFirst number (246,666,704):\n\nDigits from left (hundred millions) to right (ones):\n\n2 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions),6 (millions),6 (hundred thousands),6 (ten thousands),6 (thousands),7 (hundreds),0 (tens),4 (ones).\n\nWait, hold on, that can't be right. Wait the number is 246,666,704.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n2 (hundred millions place), 4 (ten millions), 6 (millions), comma, then 6 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), comma, 7 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 4 (ones).\n\nYes, so the digits from left to right:\n\n2,4,6,6,6,6,7,0,4.\n\nSecond number:180,727,746.\n\nDigits from left (hundred millions) to right (ones):\n\n1 (hundred millions),8 (ten millions),0 (millions),7 (hundred thousands),2 (ten thousands),7 (thousands),7 (hundreds),4 (tens),6 (ones).\n\nSo digits:1,8,0,7,2,7,7,4,6.\n\nSo when adding digit by digit from left to right, but actually we need to add from right to left (units first).\n\nWait, in the first method I did digit positions starting from right (ones) as position1, but perhaps I messed up the digit values.\n\nLet me write both numbers digit by digit from right to left (position1 to position9):\n\nFirst number (246666704):\n\nPosition1 (ones):4  \nPosition2 (tens):0  \nPosition3 (hundreds):7  \nPosition4 (thousands):6  \nPosition5 (ten thousands):6  \nPosition6 (hundred thousands):6  \nPosition7 (millions):4  \nPosition8 (ten millions):6  \nPosition9 (hundred millions):2  \n\nSecond number (180727746):\n\nPosition1 (ones):6  \nPosition2 (tens):4  \nPosition3 (hundred