et me write the numbers with commas to see the places:\n\nFirst number: 856,564,531 → 8 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 1 (ones).\n\nSecond number:643,645,481 →6 (hundred millions),4 (ten millions),3 (millions),6 (hundred thousands),4 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),4 (hundreds),8 (tens),1 (ones).\n\nWait, perhaps my breakdown earlier was wrong. Let me write them without commas to see:\n\nFirst number: 8 5 6 5 6 4 5 3 1\n\nSecond number:6 4 3 6 4 5 4 8 1\n\nWait, but actually, when written as 856,564,531, the digits are:\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 1):\n\n1: 1 (ones)\n\n2:3 (tens)\n\n3:5 (hundreds)\n\n4:4 (thousands)\n\n5:6 (ten thousands)\n\n6:5 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:6 (millions)\n\n8:5 (ten millions)\n\n9:8 (hundred millions)\n\nSimilarly, the second number 643,645,481:\n\n1:1 (ones)\n\n2:8 (tens)\n\n3:4 (hundreds)\n\n4:5 (thousands)\n\n5:4 (ten thousands)\n\n6:6 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:3 (millions)\n\n8:4 (ten millions)\n\n9:6 (hundred millions)\n\nWait, so aligning them digit by digit from the right:\n\nDigit 1:1 +1=2\n\nDigit 2:3+8=11 (1, carryover1)\n\nDigit3:5+4=9 +1=10 (0, carryover1)\n\nDigit4:4+5=9 +1=10 (0, carryover1)\n\nDigit5:6 (from first) +4 (second) =10 +1=11 (1, carryover1)\n\nDigit6:5 (first) +6 (second)=11 +1=12 (2, carryover1)\n\nDigit7:6 (first) +3 (second)=9 +1=10 (0, carryover1)\n\nDigit8:5 (first) +4 (second)=9 +1=10 (0, carryover1)\n\nDigit9:8 (first) +6 (second)=14 +1=15 (5, carryover1)\n\nThen the carryover 1 becomes digit10:1.\n\nSo putting it all together from right to left:\n\nDigit1:2\n\nDigit2:1\n\nDigit3:0\n\nDigit4:0\n\nDigit5:1\n\nDigit6:2\n\nDigit7:0\n\nDigit8:0\n\nDigit9:5\n\nDigit10:1\n\nSo the number is 1,500,209,012.\n\nWait, but according to the second method, adding the millions parts gave 1,500,210,012. Which is conflicting. So where is the mistake?\n\nAh! Let me recalculate the second method:\n\nOriginal 