ed millions) +73M (ten millions + millions?) Wait, no. Let me correct the breakdown.\n\nThe second number is 973,329,535.\n\nBreaking it down:\n\n- 900,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n- 70,000,000 (ten millions)\n\n- 3,000,000 (millions)\n\n- 300,000 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 20,000 (ten thousands)\n\n-9,000 (thousands)\n\n-500 (hundreds)\n\n-30 (tens)\n\n-5 (ones)\n\nSo the ten millions part is 70M, millions is3M. So total for the second number's ten millions and millions is 73M.\n\nThe first number's ten millions is20M, and millions is0M. So adding the ten millions and millions parts:\n\n20M (first's ten millions) +73M (second's ten millions + millions) → but actually, the ten millions are separate from millions. So the total for the ten millions place is 2 (first) +7 (second) =9 ten millions? Wait no, the breakdown should be:\n\nThe first number's ten millions digit is2 → 20,000,000,\n\nSecond's ten millions digit is7 →70,000,000,\n\nso 20M +70M =90M,\n\nplus the first's millions digit is0 →0M,\n\nsecond's millions digit is3 →3M,\n\nso total millions and ten millions parts:90M +3M =93M,\n\nAdding to the hundred millions:\n\n600M +900M =1,500M,\n\nplus 93M → total 1,593M,\n\nthen adding the remaining parts (hundred thousands down):\n\n684,370 +329,535 =1,013,905,\n\nso total is 1,593,000,000 +1,013,905 =1,594,013,905.\n\nBut according to digit-wise addition, it's 1,558,013,905. There's a discrepancy of 36 million. Where is the mistake?\n\nWait, let me re-express the two numbers:\n\nFirst number:620,684,370\n\nSecond number:973,329,535\n\nLet me add them as:\n\n620,684,370\n\n+973,329,535\n\n= ?\n\nAdding the two:\n\nStart with 620,684,370 + 900,000,000 = 1,520,684,370\n\nThen add 73,329,535:\n\n1,520,684,370 +73,329,535\n\n=1,520,684,370 +70,000,000 =1,590,684,370\n\nThen +3,329,535 → 1,590,684,370 +3,329,535 =1,594,013,905.\n\nThis matches the breakdown method. So why does the digit-wise addition give a different result?\n\nWait, in the digit-wise addition, I must have messed up the places.\n\nLet me try digit-wise again carefully:\n\nNumbers:\n\nFirst:620,684,370 → digits from right:\n\nPosition1:0\n\nPosition2:7 (tens)\n\nPosition3:3 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:4 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:8 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:6 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:0 (millions)\n\nPosition8:2 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:6 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond:973,329,535 → digits from right:\n\nPosition1:5\n\nPosition2:3\n\nPosition3:5\n\nPosition4:9\n\nPosition5:2\n\nPosition6:3\n\nPosition7:7 (millions)\n\nPosition8:3 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:9 (hundred millions)\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nPosition1 (ones):0+5=5\n\nPosition2 (tens):7+3=10 →0, carry1\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):3+5=8 +1=9\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4+9=13 →3, carry1\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):8+2=10 +1=11 →1, carry1\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):6+3=9 +1=10 →0, carry1\n\nPosition7 (millions):0+7=7 +1=8 →8, carry0\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):2+3=5 →5, carry0\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):6+9=15 →5, carry1\n\nBillions place:0+0+1=1\n\nThus the result is 1 (billions),5 (hundred millions),5 (ten millions),8 (millions),0 (hundred thousands),1 (ten thousands),3 (thousands),9 (hundreds),0 (tens),5 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 1,558,013,905.\n\nBut when adding via the breakdown, I get 1,594,013,905.\n\nWait, there's a contradiction here. I must have made a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let me use another method: compute the sum using smaller chunks.\n\nLet me add 620,684,370 + 973,329,535 step by step:\n\nFirst, add 620,684,370 + 900,000,000 = 1,520,684,370\n\nNow add the remaining 73,329,535 to this:\n\n1,520,684,370 +73,329,535.\n\nBreakdown:\n\n1,520,684,370 +70,000,000 =1,590,684,370\n\nThen add 3,329,535 to that:\n\n1,590,684,370 +3,000,000 =1,593,684,370\n\nAdd 329,535:\n\n1,593,684,370 +300,000 =1,593,984,370\n\nThen +29,535:\n\n1,593,984,370 +29,535 =1,594,013,905.\n\nSo this confirms the breakdown method's result. Therefore, the digit-wise addition must have an error.\n\nWait, where is the error in the digit-wise method?\n\nLooking back at the digit-wise addition:\n\nPosition7 (millions):0 (from first number) +7 (from second's millions digit) + carryover1 (from position6's addition).\n\nWait position6 was hundred thousands:\n\nPosition6:6 (first's hundred thousands) +3 (second's hundred thousands) =9, plus carryover1 from position5 (which was 11, so carryover1) → total 10. So position6 result is0 with carryover1.\n\nThen position7 (millions):\n\nFirst number's millions digit is0,\n\nSecond number's millions digit is3 (wait, no! Wait the second number's millions digit is3?\n\nWait second number breakdown: the millions digit is the 7th digit from the right. Let me recheck:\n\nSecond number:973,329,535.\n\nDigits from right (position1):\n\nPosition1:5,\n\nPosition2:3,\n\nPosition3:5,\n\nPosition4:9,\n\nPosition5:2,\n\nPosition6:3,\n\nPosition7:7 (millions?),\n\nPosition8:3 (ten millions),\n\nPosition9:9 (hundred millions).\n\nYes, so the millions digit (position7) is7. Wait, so in the millions place, the second number has7, not3. Wait, I think this is the mistake!\n\nIn the breakdown earlier, I thought the second number's millions digit was3, but actually, the millions digit is the 7th digit from the right, which is the '3' in 973,329,535? Wait, let's parse the second number again:\n\nSecond number:973,329,535.\n\nBreaking into place values:\n\n- 9 (hundred millions),\n\n-7 (ten millions),\n\n-3 (millions),\n\n-3 (hundred thousands),\n\n-2 (ten thousands),\n\n-9 (thousands),\n\n-5 (hundreds),\n\n-3 (tens),\n\n-5 (ones).\n\nWait no, let's parse digit by digit from left to right:\n\nThe number is 973,329,535.\n\nBreaking down:\n\nDigit1 (billions place? No, since it's 9 digits, so the first digit is hundred millions):\n\nPosition1 (hundred millions):9,\n\nPosition2 (ten millions):7,\n\nPosition3 (millions):3,\n\nPosition4 (hundred thousands):3,\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):2,\n\nPosition6 (thousands):9,\n\nPosition7 (hundreds):5,