carried over 1 makes 11. So that's 1 in the hundred millions place and carryover 1.\n\nBillions place: The first number has nothing here (since it's an 9-digit number starting with 8), so just the carried over 1. Wait, actually, the first number is 862,618,747 which is 9 digits, so the hundred millions place is 8 (the first digit), and the next digit would be the ten millions (6), millions (2), etc. Wait, maybe I got the places wrong earlier. Let me recount to make sure.\n\nWait, perhaps I should write them out with commas to check:.\n\n862,618,747 breaks down as:.\n\n- Billions: 8 (since 800 million is part of the first number).\nWait, actually, 862,618,747 is 8 hundred million, 62 million, etc. Let me see:.\n\nBreaking down 862,618,747:.\n\nDigits from left: 8 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nWait, maybe I miscounted the places earlier. Let me start over properly.\n\nLet me align both numbers vertically:.\n\n          8 6 2 6 1 8 7 4 7.\n        + 2 7 3 5 4 5 8 3 0.\n        ---------------------.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 7 + 0 = 7. Write down 7.\n\nTens: 4 + 3 = 7. Write 7.\n\nHundreds: 7 + 8 = 15. Wait, wait, hold on. Wait the first number's hundreds place is 7? Let me check the digits again.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the digit positions. Let me write each number's digits step by step.\n\nFirst number: 862,618,747.\n\nBreaking down each digit from right to left (since addition is right to left):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 7.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 4.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 7.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 8.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 1.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 6.\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 2.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 6.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 8.\n\nSecond number: 273,545,830.\n\nBreaking down similarly:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 0.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 3.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 8.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 5.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 4.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 5.\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 3.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 7.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 2.\n\nSo now adding each position:.\n\nStarting with position 1 (ones):.\n\n7 (from first) + 0 (second) = 7. Write 7.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):.\n\n4 + 3 = 7. Write 7.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):.\n\n7 + 8 = 15. So write 5, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):.\n\n8 +5 =13, plus carryover 1 =14. So write 4, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):.\n\n1 +4 =5, plus 1 =6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):.\n\n6 +5 =11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):.\n\n2 +3 =5, plus 1 =6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):.\n\n6 +7 =13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):.\n\n8 +2 =10, plus carryover 1 =11. So write 1, carryover 1. But since there are no more digits, that carryover becomes a new digit at the beginning.\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right, the digits we wrote are:.\n\nPosition 1:7.\n\n2:7.\n\n3:5.\n\n4:4.\n\n5:6.\n\n6:1.\n\n7:6.\n\n8:3.\n\n9:1 (with a carryover of 1).\n\nWait, but the carryover from the hundred millions place adds an extra digit. So the total digits will be 10.\n\nSo the final number is:.\n\n1 (from the carryover) followed by the digits from position 9 to 1:.\n\nWait let me see:.\n\nThe carryover after the hundred millions place was 1, so the total is:.\n\n1 (carryover) then the result of position 9 (which was 10 +1? Wait no, let me retrace.\n\nWait the hundred millions place:.\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit is 8, second is 2. Adding those gives 10, plus the carryover 1 from the previous position (position 8's carryover? Wait, let's go step by step again carefully.\n\nWait perhaps I messed up the carryover steps. Let me go through each step again carefully.\n\nStarting from the right, step by step:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):.\n\n7 +0 =7. No carryover.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):.\n\n4+3=7. No carryover.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):.\n\n7 (first) +8 (second)=15. So write 5, carryover 1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\nFirst number:8, second:5. 8+5=13, plus carryover 1 makes 14. So write 4, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\nFirst:1, second:4. 1+4=5, plus carryover1=6. Write6, no carryover.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\nFirst:6, second:5. 6+5=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\nFirst:2, second:3. 2+3=5, plus carryover1=6. Write6, no carryover.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\nFirst:6, second:7. 6+7=13. Write3, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\nFirst:8, second:2. 8+2=10, plus carryover1=11. So write1, carryover1.\n\nNow, after the hundred millions place, we still have a carryover of 1. Since there are no more digits, this carryover becomes a new digit to the left, so the total becomes:.\n\n1 followed by the digits from position9 to 1:.\n\nSo the digits from left to right are:.\n\n1 (carryover), then the result of position9 (1), position8 (3), position7 (6), position6 (1), position5 (6), position4 (4), position3 (5), position2 (7), position1 (7).\n\nWait, wait. Let me list each position's result:.\n\nAfter all positions:.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions): 1 (from 11 total, so digit 1, carryover1).\n\nBut the carryover adds a new digit at the beginning, so the total digits are:.\n\n1 (new digit) then the 9 digits from each position's result.\n\nWait, so the digits are:.\n\n1 (from carryover), then the result of position9 (which was 1), then position8 (3), position7 (6), position6 (1), position5 (6), position4 (4), position3 (5), position2 (7), position1 (7).\n\nSo putting all together:.\n\nDigits from left:.\n\n1 (carryover), 1 (position9), 3 (position8), 6 (position7), 1 (position6), 6 (position5), 4 (position4), 5 (position3),7 (position2),7 (position1).\n\nWait, that's 10 digits:</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
