(ones). .\n\nSecond number: 4 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 0 (ones). .\n\nWait, the hundred thousands place for the first number is indeed 6, and the second is 9. So adding those gives 6 + 9 = 15. But we need to check if there's a carryover from the previous step. Wait, the previous step was the thousands place. Let me go step by step carefully.\n\nStarting over with each digit position:.\n\nLet me write both numbers without commas for clarity:.\n\n315664273.\n451908900.\n\nAdding digit by digit from right to left:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 3 + 0 = 3. Write 3.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 7 + 0 =7. Write 7.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 2 + 9 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 4 + 8 = 12, plus 1 is 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 6 + 0 =6, plus 1 is 7. Write 7, no carryover.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 6 +9 =15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 5 +1 =6, plus 1 is 7. Write 7, no carryover.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 1 +5 =6. Write 6.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 3 +4 =7. Write 7.\n\nWait, let me check that again step by step:.\n\nWait the digits from right to left (positions 1 to 9):.\n\nNumber1: digits are 3 (1),7 (2),2 (3),4 (4),6 (5),6 (6),5 (7),1 (8),3 (9).\n\nWait, maybe I should list all digits properly:.\n\nWait the first number is 315,664,273. Breaking down each digit from right (position 1) to left (position 9):.\n\nPosition 1: 3 (ones).\nPosition 2:7 (tens).\nPosition3:2 (hundreds).\nPosition4:4 (thousands).\nPosition5:6 (ten thousands).\nPosition6:6 (hundred thousands).\nPosition7:5 (millions).\nPosition8:1 (ten millions).\nPosition9:3 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number: 451,908,900. Breaking down similarly:.\n\nPosition1:0 (ones).\nPosition2:0 (tens).\nPosition3:9 (hundreds).\nPosition4:8 (thousands).\nPosition5:0 (ten thousands).\nPosition6:9 (hundred thousands).\nPosition7:1 (millions).\nPosition8:5 (ten millions).\nPosition9:4 (hundred millions).\n\nNow adding each position:.\n\nPosition1: 3 +0 =3.\n\nPosition2:7 +0=7.\n\nPosition3:2+9=11 → write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition4:4 +8 =12 +1=13 → write3, carryover1.\n\nPosition5:6 +0 =6 +1=7 → no carryover.\n\nPosition6:6 +9=15 → write5, carryover1.\n\nPosition7:5 +1=6 +1 (carryover?) Wait, the carryover from position6 is 1, so adding to position7:.\n\nWait position7: 5 (from first number) +1 (from second) =6, plus carryover 1 from position6 gives 7. So write7, no carryover.\n\nPosition8:1 (from first) +5 (second)=6 → write6.\n\nPosition9:3 +4 =7 → write7.\n\nSo putting all together from position9 to 1:.\n\n7 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions),7 (millions), then 7 (ten thousands?), wait let me reconstruct the digits in order:.\n\nWait the written digits from position9 (highest) to position1 (lowest):.\n\nPosition9:7.\n\nPosition8:6.\n\nPosition7:7.\n\nPosition6:5.\n\nPosition5:7.\n\nPosition4:3.\n\nPosition3:1.\n\nPosition2:7.\n\nPosition1:3.\n\nWait that would be 7 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions),7 (millions), then 5 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands?), wait let's see:.\n\nWait the positions are:.\n\nPositions:.\n\n9: Hundred millions.\n\n8: Ten millions.\n\n7: Millions.\n\n6: Hundred thousands.\n\n5: Ten thousands.\n\n4: Thousands.\n\n3: Hundreds.\n\n2: Tens.\n\n1: Ones.\n\nSo the digits in order are:.\n\nPosition9:7.\n\nPosition8:6.\n\nPosition7:7.\n\nPosition6:5.\n\nPosition5:7.\n\nPosition4:3.\n\nPosition3:1.\n\nPosition2:7.\n\nPosition1:3.\n\nSo combining these digits:.\n\n7 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 1 (hundreds),7 (tens),3 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
