 first number's millions place is 1 (since 721,785,238 is 7 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 1 (millions), then the rest). Wait, maybe I messed up the places. Let me write both numbers with commas for clarity:.\n\nFirst number: 721,785,238 → 7 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 8 (units).\n\nSecond number: 122,585,810 → 1 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 0 (units).\n\nSo when adding, let's line them up:.\n\n      7 2 1 7 8 5 2 3 8.\n    + 1 2 2 5 8 5 8 1 0.\n    ----------------------.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):.\n\n8 + 0 =8 → units place:8.\n\nNext (tens place):3 +1=4 → tens:4.\n\nHundreds:2 +8=10 → write 0, carryover 1.\n\nThousands:5 +5=10 +1=11 → write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands:8 +8=16 +1=17 → write7, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:7 (from first) +5 (from second)=12 +1=13 → write 3, carryover1.\n\nMillions place:1 (first) +2 (second)=3 +1 (carry)=4 → write4, no carry.\n\nTen millions place:2 (first) +2 (second)=4 → write4.\n\nHundred millions place:7 (first) +1 (second)=8 → write8.\n\nPutting it all together, the sum is 8 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 8 (units). Wait, let me write that step by step.\n\nWait, let me reconstruct the digits from the addition step by step:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\n1. Units: 8 → digit 8.\n\n2. Tens:4 → digit 4.\n\n3. Hundreds:0 (with carryover 1).\n\n4. Thousands:1 (with carryover 1).\n\n5. Ten thousands:7 (with carryover 1).\n\n6. Hundred thousands:3 (with carryover1).\n\n7. Millions:4 (from 1+2+1 carry?).\n\nWait, let me retrace:.\n\nWait, when adding the millions place:.\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 1 (since 721,785,238 has 1 in millions place), second number's millions digit is 2 (since 122,585,810 has 2 in millions place). So 1+2=3, plus the carryover 1 from the previous step (which was the hundred thousands place which had 12 +1=13, so carried over 1 to the millions place). So 3+1=4. So millions digit is 4, no carryover.\n\nNext, ten millions place: first number's ten millions is 2, second's is 2. 2+2=4. So ten millions digit is 4.\n\nHundred millions place: first's 7 and second's 1. 7+1=8. So hundred millions digit is 8.\n\nPutting all together, the digits from right to left (units to hundred millions):.\n\nUnits:8, Tens:4, Hundreds:0, Thousands:1, Ten thousands:7, Hundred thousands:3, Millions:4, Ten millions:4, Hundred millions:8.\n\nWait, but arranging them in order from highest to lowest place, the number would be:.\n\n8 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands),7 (ten thousands),1 (thousands),0 (hundreds),4 (tens),8 (units)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 19%|#8        | 94/500 [2:11:53<5:39:47, 50.21s/it]
