ons)\n\nPosition8:6 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:3 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number:506,786,558.\n\nDigits:\n\nPosition1:8 (ones)\n\nPosition2:5 (tens)\n\nPosition3:5 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:6 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:8 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:7 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:6 (millions)\n\nPosition8:0 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:5 (hundred millions)\n\nSo for position7 (millions place):\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 6 (since 366,520,298: the millions digit is the 7th from the right: 6).\n\nSecond number's millions digit is 6 (from 506,786,558: the 7th digit is 6).\n\nSo 6 +6=12 plus carryover1 from position6 (which was 1 from hundred thousands place) \u2192 13. So write 3, carryover1.\n\nThen position8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 6 (the 8th digit from the right).\n\nSecond number's ten millions digit is 0 (the 8th digit is 0).\n\nSo 6 +0 +1 (carryover)=7.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):3 +5=8.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nPosition9:8\n\nPosition8:7\n\nPosition7:3\n\nPosition6:3 (from 13, so 3 with carryover)\n\nPosition5:0\n\nPosition4:5\n\nPosition3:9\n\nPosition2:5\n\nPosition1:6\n\nSo the number is 8 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nWhich is 873,305,956.\n\nBut the second method gave 873,306,856. The difference is in the thousands and hundred thousands places.\n\nWait, in the second method, adding the millions first:\n\n366,520,298 +506,786,558 = (366,000,000 +506,000,000) + (520,298 +786,558) = 872,000,000 +1,306,856 = 873,306,856.\n\nHmm, so there's a conflict. Which is correct?\n\nWait let's do the addition straight numerically:\n\n366,520,298\n\n+506,786,558\n\n= ?\n\nLet me add them digit by digit again carefully.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 8+8=16 \u21926, carry1.\n\nTens:9+5=14 +1=15 \u21925, carry1.\n\nHundreds:2+6=8 +1=9, no carry.\n\nThousands:0+5=5 \u21925.\n\nTen thousands:2+8=10 \u21920, carry1.\n\nHundred thousands:5+7=12 +1=13 \u21923, carry1.\n\nMillions:6+6=12 +1=13 \u21923, carry1.\n\nTen millions:6 (from first number) +0 (from second) =6 +1=7 \u21927, no carry.\n\nHundred millions:3+5=8.\n\nSo putting together:\n\nHundred millions:8\n\nTen millions:7\n\nMillions:3\n\nHundred thousands:3\n\nTen thousands:0\n\nThousands:5\n\nHundreds:9\n\nTens:5\n\nOnes:6.\n\nSo that's 873,305,956.\n\nBut in the second method, adding 520,298 +786,558 gives 1,306,856, which when added to 872,000,000 gives 873,306,856. Wait, why the discrepancy?\n\nAh! Because in the second method, I added 520,298 +786,558 as 1,306,856, but let me recalculate that addition:\n\n520,298\n\n+786,558\n\nLet's do this step by step:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:8+8=16 \u21926, carry1.\n\nTens:9+5=14 +1=15 \u21925, carry1.\n\nHundreds:2+5=7 +1=8 \u21928, no carry.\n\nThousands:0+6=6 \u21926.\n\nTen thousands:2 (from 520k) +8 (from 786k) =10 \u21920, carry1.\n\nHundred thousands:5 (from 520k) +7 (from 786k) =12 +1=13 \u21923, carry1.\n\nMillions place: There's a carryover 1 here, but since both numbers don't have digits beyond hundred thousands (since 520,298 is up to hundred thousands place?), wait, actually, 520,298 is a 6-digit number after the million? Wait, 520,298 is 520 thousand plus 298, so it's a 6-digit number? Wait no, 520,298 is 6 digits? Wait 5-2-0-2-9-8 \u2192 six digits. But 786,558 is also six digits. So when adding them, the total would be up to 7 digits (since 520k+786k=1,306,856). Wait, but when adding 520,298 (which is 0.520298 million) and 786,558 (0.786558 million), their sum is 1.306856 million, which is 1,306,856. So that's correct.\n\nSo adding to 872 million gives 873,306,856.\n\nBut according to the first method, the total is 873,305,956. The difference is in the thousands and hundreds of thousands place.\n\nWait, perhaps I made an error in the first method's hundred thousands place.\n\nWait let me re-express the first method's breakdown:\n\nAfter adding a