ions):8.\n\nWait, so now adding each position:.\n\nPosition 1: 4 +9 =13 → 3, carryover1.\n\nPosition2:4+3=7 +1=8 →8, carryover0.\n\nPosition3:0+3=3 →3, carryover0.\n\nPosition4:0+8=8 →8, carryover0.\n\nPosition5:4 +5=9 →9, carryover0.\n\nPosition6:2 +3=5 →5, carryover0.\n\nPosition7:6 +5=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nPosition8:8 +8=16 +1 (carryover)=17 →7, carryover1.\n\nPosition9:7 +8=15 +1 (carryover)=16 →6, carryover1.\n\nNow, since we have a carryover of 1 after the hundred millions place, we need to add a new digit at the beginning (billions place): 1.\n\nSo compiling the digits from last to first:.\n\nThe result digits from position1 to position10:.\n\nPosition1:3.\n\nPosition2:8.\n\nPosition3:3.\n\nPosition4:8.\n\nPosition5:9.\n\nPosition6:5.\n\nPosition7:1 (from position7's 11 sum, but wait let me check again:.\n\nWait, let's retrace the positions step by step carefully:.\n\nWait in Position7 (millions place):.\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 6, second's is 5. So 6 +5 =11. So we write down 1 and carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions): first is8, second is8. 8+8=16, plus the carryover1 makes 17. So write 7, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions): 7 +8=15, plus carryover1 makes 16. So write 6, carryover1.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits, the final carryover1 is added as a new digit (billions place).\n\nSo the digits from right to left (positions 1 to 10):.\n\nPosition1:3.\n\nPosition2:8.\n\nPosition3:3.\n\nPosition4:8.\n\nPosition5:9.\n\nPosition6:5.\n\nPosition7:1.\n\nPosition8:7.\n\nPosition9:6.\n\nPosition10:1.\n\nSo writing the number from left (highest to lowest):.\n\n1 (billions), 6 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 3 (ones). Wait, no, I think I might have messed up the order here. Let me list the digits in the correct order.\n\nWait, the digits we've calculated are:.\n\nStarting from the first position (ones digit) to the tenth (billions):.\n\nPos