ce:** The first number has 7 and the second has 6. Adding the carried 1: 7 + 6 + 1 = 14. So, 4 here and carry over 1.\n\n10. **Billions place:** Finally, the first number has nothing here (since it's 750 million..) but the second number has 6? Wait, no, wait. Wait, let me check the original numbers again. The first number is 750,800,466 which is 7 hundred million, 50 million, etc. The second number is 651,850,945. Let me confirm the digits:.\n\nBreaking down 750,800,466:.\n- Billions place: 7 (since it's 700 million).\n- Then 5 in the hundred millions, 0 in the ten millions, 8 in the millions, 0 in the hundred thousands, 0 in the ten thousands, 4 in the thousands, 6 in the hundreds, 6 in the tens, 6 in the ones.\n\nWait, maybe I need to write each digit's place properly. Let's list each digit position for both numbers to ensure alignment.\n\nLet me write both numbers digit by digit from right to left (since addition is done right to left):.\n\nFirst number: 750,800,466.\nBreaking into digits (right to left):.\nPosition 1 (ones): 6.\n2 (tens): 6.\n3 (hundreds):4.\n4 (thousands):0.\n5 (ten thousands):0.\n6 (hundred thousands):8.\n7 (millions):0.\n8 (ten millions):5.\n9 (hundred millions):7.\n\nSecond number: 651,850,945.\nBreaking into digits (right to left):.\nPosition 1 (ones):5.\n2 (tens):4.\n3 (hundreds):9.\n4 (thousands):0.\n5 (ten thousands):5.\n6 (hundred thousands):8.\n7 (millions):1.\n8 (ten millions):5.\n9 (hundred millions):6.\n\nWait, let me confirm that again. Let me write both numbers digit by digit in order from right (ones) to left (billions):.\n\nFirst number: 750,800,466.\nDigits (right to left, positions 1 to 9):.\n1:6.\n2:6.\n3:4.\n4:0.\n5:0.\n6:8.\n7:0.\n8:5.\n9:7.\n\nSecond number: 651,850,945.\nDigits (right to left, positions 1 to 9):.\n1:5.\n2:4.\n3:9.\n4:0.\n5:5.\n6:8.\n7:1.\n8:5.\n9:6.\n\nWait, but the second number is 651,850,945. Let's parse it properly:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\nOnes digit: 5.\nTens: 4.\nHundreds:9.\nThousands:0.\nTen thousands:5 (since it's ..945, so the ten thousands place is the fifth digit from the right. Let me count:.\n\nNumber: 651,850,945.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 1):.\n\n1:5 (ones).\n2:4 (tens).\n3:9 (hundreds).\n4:0 (thousands).\n5:5 (ten thousands).\n6:0 (hundred thousands?) Wait, no, maybe I'm mixing up the commas. Let's see:.\n\nThe number is written as 651,850,945. The commas are placed every three digits. So:.\n\n- The rightmost comma separates thousands: 945 is the thousands part.\n- The next comma separates millions: 850, so that's 850 thousand? Wait, perhaps better to split as:.\n\n651,850,945.\n\nBreaking into groups:.\n\n- The first comma from the right separates thousands: 945 (units).\n- The next comma separates millions: 850 (thousands).\n- The remaining part is 651 (millions).\n\nWait, actually, standard comma placement is three digits each, so 651,850,945 would be:.\n\n651 (millions), 850 (thousands), 945 (units). So the full breakdown is:.\n\nBillions: 6 (since 651 million is 0.651 billion).\nHundred millions: 5.\nTen millions:1.\nMillions: 8.\nHundred thousands:5.\nTen thousands:0.\nThousands:9.\nHundreds:4.\nTens:5.\nWait, perhaps it's better to write all digits:.\n\nNumber 651,850,945 has digits:.\n\nStarting from the left: 6 (hundred millions?), let's see:.\n\nWait, 651,850,945:.\n\nLet me count the digits. Let's see: 6 (1st digit), 5 (2nd), 1 (3rd), comma, then 8 (4th),5 (5th),0 (6th), comma, 9 (7th),4 (8th),5 (9th). So total 9 digits. So the first digit is 6 in the hundred millions place (since 9 digits: the places are hundred millions (digit1), ten millions (digit2), millions (digit3), hundred thousands (digit4), ten thousands (digit5), thousands (digit6), hundreds (digit7), tens (digit8), ones (digit9). Wait, maybe it's better to list each digit with its place value.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made a mistake in the digit breakdown earlier. Let me try again.\n\nFirst number: 750,800,466.\n\nBreaking down ea