d millions).\n\nSecond number digits (same positions):.\n\nPosition1:0 (ones).\n\nPosition2:9 (tens).\n\nPosition3:3 (hundreds).\n\nPosition4:3 (thousands).\n\nPosition5:9 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition6:8 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition7:0 (millions).\n\nPosition8:6 (ten millions).\n\nPosition9:5 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, that might be the confusion. Let's recheck the second number: 560,893,390.\n\nBreaking it down:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nDigit 1:0 (ones).\n\nDigit2:9 (tens).\n\nDigit3:3 (hundreds).\n\nDigit4:3 (thousands).\n\nDigit5:9 (ten thousands).\n\nDigit6:8 (hundred thousands).\n\nDigit7:0 (millions).\n\nDigit8:6 (ten millions).\n\nDigit9:5 (hundred millions).\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nSo, adding each position:.\n\nStarting from position1 (ones):.\n\n9 +0 =9.\n\nPosition2 (tens):7+9=16 → 6, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):1 (from first) +3 (second)=4 plus the 1 carried →5.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):1 (first) +3 (second) =4.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):0 (first) +9 (second)=9.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1 (first) +8 (second)=9.\n\nPosition7 (millions):4 (first) +0 (second)=4.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):6 (first) +6 (second)=12 → 2, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):4 (first) +5 (second)=9, plus the carried 1 makes 10. So that would be 0 in the hundred millions place and carryover 1 to a new billion place.\n\nWait, so the total digits now would be 10 digits because of the carryover.\n\nSo putting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right, the digits are:.\n\nPosition1:9.\n\nPosition2:6.\n\nPosition3:5.\n\nPosition4:4.\n\nPosition5:9.\n\nPosition6:9.\n\nPosition7:4.\n\nPosition8:2.\n\nPosition9:0 (from 4+5+1? Wait, let me recast:.\n\nWait, the hundred millions place (position9):.\n\nOriginal digits 4 and5. 4+5=9, plus the carryover from position8 (which was 1 from the ten millions place addition: 6+6=12 → carryover1). So total for position9: 9 +1=10. So we write 0 and carryover 1 to the next position (billions place).\n\nSo 