o left):.\n\nPositions (starting from the right, position 1 is ones):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 8 + 6 = 14 → 4, carry 1.\nPosition 2 (tens): 6 + 1 = 7 + carry 1 → 8? Wait, original digits here are 6 (from first number's tens) and 1 (from second's tens). Wait, let me list all digits properly:.\n\nWait maybe I should list each digit step by step. Let me do that.\n\nFirst number: 319,925,668.\nBreaking down each digit from right to left (ones to millions):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 8.\nPosition 2 (tens): 6.\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 6.\nPosition 4 (thousands): 5.\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 2.\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 9.\nPosition 7 (millions): 9.\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 1.\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 3.\n\nSecond number: 404,993,616.\n\nBreaking down similarly:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 6.\nPosition 2 (tens): 1.\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 6.\nPosition 4 (thousands): 3.\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 9.\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 9.\nPosition 7 (millions): 4.\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 0.\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 4.\n\nWait, let me confirm that. The second number is 404,993,616. Let's parse it:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\n6 (ones), 1 (tens), 6 (hundreds), 3 (thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (hundred thousands), 4 (millions), 0 (ten millions), 4 (hundred millions). Yes, that's correct.\n\nNow adding each position:.\n\nStarting with Position 1 (ones):.\n\n8 + 6 = 14 → write 4, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):.\n\n6 (from first) + 1 (from second) = 7, plus carryover 1 → 8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):.\n\n6 +6 = 12 → write 2, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):.\n\n5 +3 = 8, plus carryover 1 → 9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):.\n\n2 +9 = 11 → write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):.\n\n9 +9 = 18, plus carryover 1 → 19 → write 9, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):.\n\n9 (first) +4 (second) =13, plus carryover 1 →14 → write 4, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):.\n\n1 (first) +0 (second) =1, plus carryover 1 →2. Write 2, no carryover.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):.\n\n3 +4 =7. Write 7.\n\nNow compiling all the digits from right to left (positions 1 to 9):.\n\nPositions:.\n\n1:4.\n\n2:8.\n\n3:2.\n\n4:9.\n\n5:1.\n\n6:9.\n\n7:4.\n\n8:2.\n\n9:7.\n\nSo putting them together: 7 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands?), Wait, maybe I need to order them correctly.\n\nWait, the digits are written from position 1 (ones) to position 9 (hundred millions). So the final number is:.\n\nStarting from position 9 (leftmost) to position1 (rightmost):.\n\n7 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands?), Wait, let's see:.\n\nWait, the positions are:.\n\nPosition 9: 7 (hundred millions place).\n\nPosition8:2 (ten millions).\n\nPosition7:4 (millions).\n\nPosition6:9 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition5:1 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition4:9 (thousands).\n\nPosition3:2 (hundreds).\n\nPosition2:8 (tens).\n\nPosition1:4 (ones).\n\nSo combining these:.\n\n7 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 4 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
