rom right, starting at 1):.\n\n1: 0 (ones).\n2:9 (tens).\n3:1 (hundreds).\n4:7 (thousands).\n5:4 (ten thousands).\n6:4 (hundred thousands).\n7:0 (millions).\n8:4 (ten millions).\n9:8 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number: 320,583,247 is also 9 digits:.\n\n1:7 (ones).\n2:4 (tens).\n3:2 (hundreds).\n4:3 (thousands).\n5:8 (ten thousands).\n6:5 (hundred thousands).\n7:0 (millions).\n8:2 (ten millions).\n9:3 (hundred millions).\n\nSo when adding, let's line them up:.\n\nHundred millions | Ten millions | Millions | Hundred thousands | Ten thousands | Thousands | Hundreds | Tens | Ones.\n\nFirst number: 8 | 4 | 0 | 4 | 4 |7 |1 |9 |0.\n\nSecond number:3 |2 |0 |5 |8 |3 |2 |4 |7.\n\nWait, maybe I need to write each digit position step by step. Let me try adding each digit column by column from right to left, keeping track of carries.\n\nStarting with the ones place (rightmost digit):.\n\nOnes: 0 +7 =7. Write 7, carry 0.\n\nTens place:9 +4=13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nHundreds place:1 +2=3, plus the carryover 1 makes 4. Write 4, carry 0.\n\nThousands place:7 +3=10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nTen thousands place:4 +8=12, plus carryover 1 =13. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nHundred thousands place:4 +5=9, plus carryover 1 =10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nMillions place:0 +0=0, plus carryover 1 =1. Write 1, carry 0.\n\nTen millions place:4 +2=6. Write 6, carry 0.\n\nHundred millions place:8 +3=11. Write 11. Wait, but since we're in the hundred millions place, writing 11 would require an extra digit. Wait, that's a problem. Because adding 8 (hundred millions) and 3 (hundred millions) gives 11 hundred millions, which is 1,100,000,000. So that would be a 1 in the billions place and 1 in the hundred millions.\n\nAh, right! So when adding the hundred millions place, the sum is 8 +3 =11. So we need to carry over 1 to the billions place. Since both numbers don't have a billions digit beyond that, the total sum will have a billions digit of 1, and the hundred millions digit is 1.\n\nSo let me correct the previous steps:.\n\nStarting from the right, as before, up to the hundred millions place:.\n\nAfter processing up to the hundred millions place (the 9th digit from the right):.\n\nThe hundred millions digits are 8 and 3, sum to 11. So we write 1 in the hundred millions place and carry over 1 to the billions place. Since there are no higher digits, the billions place now has that 1.\n\nTherefore, putting all together:.\n\nThe digits from right to left (ones to billions):.\n\nOnes:7.\n\nTens:3.\n\nHundreds:4.\n\nThousands:0.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nHundred thousands:0.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nTen millions:6.\n\nHundred millions:1.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nSo combining these digits, starting from the highest (billions), it's 1,1 (hundred millions),6 (ten millions),1 (millions),0 (hundred thousands),3 (ten thousands),0 (thousands),4 (hundreds),3 (tens),7 (ones).\n\nWait, let's list them in order:.\n\nBillions place:1.\n\nHundred millions:1.\n\nTen millions:6.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nHundred thousands:0.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nThousands:0.\n\nHundreds:4.\n\nTens:3.\n\nOnes:7.\n\nWait, that seems a bit confusing. Let me write the number step by step.\n\nStarting from the billions place:.\n\nBillions digit: 1.\n\nHundred millions: 1.\n\nTen millions:6.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nHundred thousands:0.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nThousands:0.\n\nHundreds:4.\n\nTens:3.\n\nOnes:7</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
