looking back, in position5 (ten thousands), first number has 3, second has 3. So 3+3=6 plus carryover 1 makes 7. So no carryover here. That changes things.\n\nPosition6 (Hundred thousands):.\n\nFirst number has7, second has7 →7+7=14. Plus any carryover? From previous step, position5 had no carryover. So 14 → write 4, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (Millions):.\n\nFirst number has7, second has6 →7+6=13 plus carryover1 →14 → write 4, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (Ten millions):.\n\nFirst has7, second has8 →7+8=15 plus carryover1 →16 → write6, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (Hundred millions):.\n\nFirst has6, second has5 →6+5=11 plus carryover1 →12 → write2, carryover1.\n\nNow since there are no more digits, the carryover1 becomes the next digit (billions place). .\n\nSo putting all the digits together from right to left:.\n\nPositions 1-9 plus the carryover:.\n\nStarting from position1 (ones):7.\n\nPosition2:8.\n\nPosition3:9.\n\nPosition4:4.\n\nPosition5:7.\n\nPosition6:4.\n\nPosition7:4.\n\nPosition8:6.\n\nPosition9:2.\n\nPlus the carryover1 at the billions place.\n\nSo the final number is 1 (billions), 2 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nWait, let me write them in order:.\n\nBillions place:1.\n\nHundred millions:2.\n\nTen millions:6.\n\nMillions:4.\n\nHundred thousands:4.\n\nTen thousands:7.\n\nThousands:4.\n\nHundreds:9.\n\nTens:8.\n\nOnes:7</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
