irst number and 4 in the second. So 9 +4=13 +1 (carryover)=14 → write4, carryover1. Since there are no more digits, we add that 1 as a new digit at the beginning.\n\nSo putting it all together, the result is:\n\n1 (from the final carryover) followed by 4 (billions), 6 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 0 (ones). Wait let me reconstruct step by step:\n\nWait let me list each digit position with the result:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones):\n\nPosition 1 (ones):0\n\nPosition2 (tens):6\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):8\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):0\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):3\n\nPosition7 (millions):4\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):6\n\nPosition10 (billions):4\n\nPlus the final carryover 1 in the ten billions place.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n0 (ones),6 (tens),8 (hundreds),3 (thousands),0 (ten thousands),3 (hundred thousands),4 (millions),3 (ten millions),6 (hundred millions),4 (billions),1 (ten billions).\n\nTherefore, writing the number from left to right: 1 4 6 3 4 3 0 3 8 6 0.\n\nWait that would be 1,463,430,3860? Wait commas are every three digits. Let me structure it properly:\n\nThe digits are:\n\nTen billions:1\n\nBillions:4\n\nHundred millions:6\n\nTen millions:3\n\nMillions:4\n\nHundred thousands:3\n\nTen thousands:0\n\nThousands:3\n\nHundreds:8\n\nTens:6\n\nOnes:0\n\nWait, arranging them:\n\n1 (ten billions), 4 (billions), 6 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 14,634,303,860? Wait let me check the commas:\n\nBreaking down 14,634,303,860:\n\n- 14 (billions part?), but commas are placed every three digits starting from the right. Let me count:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0 (ones)\n\n6 (tens)\n\n8 (hundreds)\n\n3 (thousands)\n\n0 (ten thousands)\n\n3 (hundred thousands)\n\n4 (millions)\n\n3 (ten millions)\n\n6 (hundred millions)\n\n4 (billions)\n\n1 (ten billions)\n\nSo grouping into threes from the right:\n\nThe number is 1 4 6 3 4 3 0 3 8 6 0\n\nBreaking into groups:\n\nStarting from the end:\n\n0, 860 (last three)\n\nthen 303 (next three)\n\nthen 434 (next three)\n\nthen 6 (next single digit? Wait need to go in threes):\n\nWait let me write all digits:\n\n1 (ten billions),4 (billions),6 (hundred millions),3 (ten millions),4 (millions),3 (hundred thousands),0 (ten thousands),3 (thousands),8 (hundreds),6 (tens),0 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 14,634,303,860.\n\nWait commas are placed every three digits from the right:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0 (ones)\n\n6 (tens)\n\n8 (hundreds) → 860\n\n3 (thousands)\n\n0 (ten thousands)\n\n3 (hundred thousands) → 303 thousand\n\n4 (millions)\n\n3 (ten millions)\n\n6 (hundred millions) → 6,34 (wait this is getting confusing.\n\nWait perhaps it's easier to count the digits:\n\nThe total digits in the result are 11 digits (from the carryover 1 plus 10 digits). So 1 followed by 4,6,3,4,3,0,3,8,6,0. So the number is 1,463,430,3860? No, commas should be placed every three digits from the right. Let's count:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition 1:0\n\n2:6\n\n3:8 → first comma after 3 digits: 860\n\n4:3\n\n5:0\n\n6:3 → second comma after next three: 033 (but leading zeros don't count?), so maybe 3,033?\n\nWait perhaps better to write the number as 14,634,303,860.\n\nWait 1 followed by 4, then 6,3,4,3,0,3,8,6,0. Let's see:\n\nBreaking into groups of three starting from the end:\n\nThe number is 14,634,303,860. Let's check:\n\n- 860 (last three)\n\n- 303 (next three)\n\n- 434 (next three)\n\n- 14 (remaining two digits at the front)\n\nSo written with commas: 14,634,303,860.\n\nWait but 14,634,303,860 has commas after every three digits from the right:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n860\n\n303\n\n434\n\n14\n\nSo combined as 14,634,303,860. That seems correct.\n\nWait but let me confirm with an alternative method. Maybe adding the two numbers using another approach.\n\nFirst number: 997,960,631\n\nSecond number:463,643,229\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me add the millions first:\n\n997,960,631 is 997,960,631\n\n463,643,229 is 463,643,229\n\nAdding:\n\nStart with the units:\n\n1 +9 =10, carryover 1.\n\nThen tens: 3+2 +1=6.\n\nHundreds:6+2=8.\n\nThousands:0+3=3.\n\nTen thousands:6+4=10, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands:9+3=12 +1=13, carryover1.\n\nMillions:7+6=13 +1=14, carryover1.\n\nTen millions:9+3=12 +1=13, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:9+6=15 +1=16, carryover1.\n\nBillions:9 (from first number) +4 (from second)=13 +1 (carryover)=14, carryover1.\n\nThen the final carryover 1.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n0,6,8,3,0,3,4,3,6,4,1 → which is 1 followed by 4,6,3,4,3,0,3,8,6,0.\n\nSo written as 1,463,430,3860? Wait no, commas should be placed correctly. The correct way would be 1,463,430,3860 is incorrect because the last four digits should be separated. Wait let me count the digits:\n\nThe total number is 14,634,303,860.\n\nYes, that's 14 billion, 634 million, 303 thousand, 860. So commas at 14,634,303,860. So the final answer should be 1,461,603,860? Wait wait, did I make a mistake here?\n\nWait let me check with another method. Let me add the two numbers using another approach, maybe breaking them into smaller parts.\n\nFirst number: 997,960,631\n\nSecond number:463,643,229\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet's add the millions:\n\n997,960,631 = 900,000,000 + 97,960,631\n\n463,643,229 = 400,000,000 + 63,643,229\n\nAdding the hundreds of millions:\n\n900,000,000 + 400,000,000 = 1,300,000,000\n\nThen the remaining parts:\n\n97,960,631 + 63,643,229 = ?\n\nAdding those:\n\n97,960,631 + 63,643,229\n\nLet's do 97,960,631 + 60,000,000 = 157,960,631\n\nThen add 3,643,229: 157,960,631 + 3,643,229 = 161,603,860\n\nSo total sum is 1,300,000,000 + 161,603,860 = 1,461,603,860.\n\nWait a minute! Now this is conflicting with the previous result of 14,634,303,860