ition starts from the right):.\n\nPositions (from right, starting at 1):.\n\n1. 4 (ones).\n2. 8 (tens).\n3. 6 (hundreds).\n4. 6 (thousands).\n5. 3 (ten thousands).\n6. 6 (hundred thousands).\n7. 0 (millions).\n8. 8 (ten millions).\n9. 2 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number: 685,594,101.\n\nBreaking down similarly:.\n\n1. 1 (ones).\n2. 0 (tens).\n3. 1 (hundreds).\n4. 4 (thousands).\n5. 9 (ten thousands).\n6. 5 (hundred thousands).\n7. 5 (millions).\n8. 8 (ten millions).\n9. 6 (hundred millions).\n\nSo now, adding each column starting from the ones place (position 1):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 4 + 1 = 5. Write down 5, no carryover.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 8 + 0 = 8. Write 8. Still no carryover.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 6 + 1 = 7. Write 7. No carryover.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands): 6 + 4 = 10. So write 0 and carryover 1.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 3 + 9 = 12, plus the carried 1 makes 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 6 + 5 = 11, plus 1 is 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 7 (millions): 0 + 5 = 5, plus 1 is 6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 8 + 8 = 16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 2 + 6 = 8, plus the carried 1 makes 9. Write 9.\n\nPutting all together, the digits from right to left would be:.\n\nPosition 1:5, 2:8, 3:7, 4:0, 5:3, 6:2, 7:6, 8:6, 9:9.\n\nSo the number is 966,960,785?.\n\nWait, let me check that again. Let me write the digits in order from highest to lowest place:.\n\nStarting from position 9 (hundred millions): 9.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):6.\n\nPosition7 (millions):6.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):2.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):0.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):7.\n\nPosition2 (tens):8.\n\nPosition1 (ones):5.\n\nSo combining them: 9 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 5 (ones). So that's 966,230,785? Wait, that can't be right. Wait, perhaps I made a mistake in the positions.\n\nWait, let me reconstruct the number properly. Each position corresponds to:.\n\nPosition 9: Hundred millions place.\n\nSo the digits are:.\n\nHundred millions:9.\n\nTen millions:6.\n\nMillions:6.\n\nHundred thousands:2.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nThousands:0.\n\nHundreds:7.\n\nTens:8.\n\nOnes:5.\n\nSo putting that together:.\n\n9 (hundred million), 6 (ten million), 6 (million) → 966 million,.\n\nthen 2 (hundred thousand), 3 (ten thousand), 0 (thousand) → 230 thousand,.\n\nthen 7 (hundred), 8 (ten), 5 (one) → 785.\n\nSo total is 966,230,785?.\n\nWait, but let me add the original numbers using another method to check.\n\n280,366,684 + 685,594,101.\n\nLet me add them step by step:.\n\nFirst, 280,366,684 + 600,000,000 = 880,366,684.\n\nThen add 85,594,101 (since 685,594,101 is 600M +85,594,101).\n\nAdding 85,594,101 to 880,366,684:.\n\n880,366,684 + 80,000,000 = 960,366,684.\n\nThen add 5,594,101:</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
