ich was 11, so we had written 1 there, but actually, let's see:\n\nWait, let me reconstruct the digits properly. Let's list each digit place from right to left:\n\nOnes:0\n\nTens:6\n\nHundreds:7\n\nThousands:4\n\nTen thousands:7\n\nHundred thousands:7\n\nMillions:9\n\nTen millions:1 (since 3+8=11, so we wrote 1 there and carried over 1 to the next)\n\nHundred millions:0 (from 1+8+1=10, so 0 with a carryover)\n\nBillions:1 (the carried over 1)\n\nSo the number is:\n\nBillions place:1\n\nHundred millions:0\n\nTen millions:1\n\nMillions:9\n\nHundred thousands:7\n\nTen thousands:7\n\nThousands:4\n\nHundreds:7\n\nTens:6\n\nOnes:0\n\nSo putting it all together, starting from billions down:\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands),7 (hundreds),6 (tens),0 (ones).\n\nWait, that would be 1,019,774,760? Wait, that's conflicting with the previous total. Hmm, I must have messed up the place values here.\n\nWait let's see:\n\nThe digits from right to left are:\n\nOnes:0\n\nTens:6\n\nHundreds:7\n\nThousands:4\n\nTen thousands:7\n\nHundred thousands:7\n\nMillions:9\n\nTen millions:1\n\nHundred millions:0\n\nBillions:1\n\nSo when written as a number, it's:\n\nBillions digit:1,\n\nHundred millions:0,\n\nTen millions:1,\n\nMillions:9,\n\nHundred thousands:7,\n\nTen thousands:7,\n\nThousands:4,\n\nHundreds:7,\n\nTens:6,\n\nOnes:0.\n\nSo the number is 1,019,774,760? But when I did the other method I got 1,009,774,760. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let me recheck the digit-by-digit addition.\n\nWait, the ten millions place:\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 3 (since 137,931,054: the digits are 1 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 7 (millions), etc.)\n\nSecond number's ten millions digit is 8 (from 871,843,706: 8 (hundred millions),7 (ten millions),1 (millions), etc.)\n\nSo adding ten millions digits: 3 +8 =11. So we write down 1 in the ten millions place and carryover 1 to the hundred millions place.\n\nThen the hundred millions digits are 1 (from first) +8 (second) +1 (carryover) =10. So that gives 0 in the hundred millions and carryover 1 to billions.\n\nSo the billions place is 1.\n\nSo the digits from left (billions) to right (ones):\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:0,\n\nTen millions:1,\n\nMillions:9 (Wait, where did the millions place come from?)\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nMillions place: first number has 7 (millions place?), let's clarify the exact place values:\n\nFirst number: 137,931,054\n\nBreaking down each digit:\n\n1 (hundred millions)\n\n3 (ten millions)\n\n7 (millions)\n\n9 (hundred thousands)\n\n3 (ten thousands)\n\n1 (thousands)\n\n0 (hundreds)\n\n5 (tens)\n\n4 (ones)\n\nSecond number:871,843,706\n\n8 (hundred millions)\n\n7 (ten millions)\n\n1 (millions)\n\n8 (hundred thousands)\n\n4 (ten thousands)\n\n3 (thousands)\n\n7 (hundreds)\n\n0 (tens)\n\n6 (ones)\n\nWait, so:\n\nMillions place for first is 7, second is 1. Adding those plus any carryover?\n\nWait, the millions place addition:\n\nFirst number's millions digit:7\n\nSecond's millions digit:1\n\nPlus any carryover from the hundred thousands place.\n\nWait, the hundred thousands place was 9 +8 =17, so carryover 1 to the millions place.\n\nSo millions place total:7+1+1=9. So millions digit is 9, no carryover.\n\nThen ten millions place:3 (first) +7 (second)=10? Wait no, second's ten millions is 7?\n\nWait second number's ten millions digit is the second digit from the left in 871,843,706. Let's count:\n\n8 (hundred millions),7 (ten millions),1 (millions), etc. Yes, so ten millions digit is 7. First's ten millions is 3. 3+7=10. Wait, that's different from earlier. Wait did I make a mistake earlier?\n\nWait, earlier I thought second number's ten millions digit was 8, but actually it's 7. Wait, let's parse the second number again:\n\n871,843,706. Let's separate the digits:\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 1):\n\nPosition 9:8 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition 8:7 (ten millions)\n\nPosition7:1 (millions)\n\nPosition6:8 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition5:4 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition4:3 (thousands)\n\nPosition3:7 (hundreds)\n\nPosition2:0 (tens)\n\nPosition1:6 (ones)\n\nWait, so the ten millions digit is the second digit from the left, which is 7. So the ten millions digits are 3 (first) and 7 (second). 3+7=10. So that would mean ten millions place is 0 with a carryover of 1 to hundred millions?\n\nWait, but earlier I thought second number's ten millions was 8. That was my mistake!\n\nAh, here's the error. Earlier, I miscalculated the ten millions digit of the second number. Let me redo the digit-by-digit addition correctly now.\n\nStarting over:\n\nFirst number:137,931,054\n\nSecond number:871,843,706\n\nLet me write both numbers with their place values:\n\nFirst number (137,931,054):\n\n- Hundred millions:1\n\n- Ten millions:3\n\n- Millions:7\n\n- Hundred thousands:9\n\n- Ten thousands:3\n\n- Thousands:1\n\n- Hundreds:0\n\n- Tens:5\n\n- Ones:4\n\nSecond number (871,843,706):\n\n- Hundred millions:8\n\n- Ten millions:7\n\n- Millions:1\n\n- Hundred thousands:8\n\n- Ten thousands:4\n\n- Thousands:3\n\n- Hundreds:7\n\n- Tens:0\n\n- Ones:6\n\nNow adding digit by digit from the right (ones place):\n\n1. Ones:4 +6 =10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\n2. Tens:5 +0 +1=6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\n3. Hundreds:0 +7 =7. Write7.\n\n4. Thousands:1 +3=4. Write4.\n\n5. Ten thousands:3 +4=7. Write7.\n\n6. Hundred thousands:9 +8=17. Write7, carryover1.\n\n7. Millions:7 (first) +1 (second) +1 (carryover)=9. Write9.\n\n8. Ten millions:3 (first) +7 (second)=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\n9. Hundred millions:1 (first) +8 (second) +1 (carryover)=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits, the carryover1 becomes the next digit (billions place). \n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\nOnes:0,\n\nTens:6,\n\nHundreds:7,\n\nThousands:4,\n\nTen thousands:7,\n\nHundred thousands:7,\n\nMillions:9,\n\nTen millions:0,\n\nHundred millions:0,\n\nBillions:1.\n\nPutting it together, the number is 1,009,774,760. \n\nYes, that matches the previous total when I did the alternative method. So the correct answer is 1,009,774,760. The mistake earlier was in the ten millions place where I incorrectly took the second number's digit as 8 instead of 7. Now it's corrected. So the final sum is one billion nine million seven hundred seventy-four thousand seven hundred sixty.\n\nAlright, so the reasoning steps need to be filled in accordingly.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 137931054 and 871843706?  \n# Reasoning:  \nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.  \n1. **Ones place**: \\(4 + 6 = 10\\). Write down **0**, carry over **1**.  \n2. **Tens place**: \\(5 + 0 + 1 = 6\\). Write down **6**.  \n3. **Hundreds place**: \\(0 + 7 = 7\\). Write down **7**.  \n4. **Thousands place**: \\(1 + 3 = 4\\). Write down **4**.  \n5. **Ten thousands place**: \\(3 + 4 = 7\\). Write down **7**.  \n6. **Hundred thousands place**: \\(9 + 8 = 17\\). Write down **7**, carry over **1**.  \n7. **Millions place**: \\(7 + 1 + 1 = 9\\). Write down **9**.  \n8. **Ten millions place**: \\(3 + 7 = 10\\). Write down **0**, carry over **1**.  \n9. **Hundred millions place**: \\(1 + 8 + 1 = 10\\). Write down **0**, carry over **1**.  \n10. **Billions place**: The carryover **1** is written here.  \n\nAnswer:  \nTherefore, the final computed sum is **1009774760**.", ''] 42%|####2     | 212/500 [4:32:18<6:48:02, 85.01s/it]
