s place. The first number has a 9, and the second has a 3. 9 + 3 = 12. Again, that's over 10, so I write down 2 and carry over 1.\n\nFinally, the billions place. The first number has a 0 (since it's a 9-digit number?), wait no, wait. Wait, 940,174,722 is 9 digits, so the highest place is hundred millions. Wait, but 940 million is 9 digits. The second number is 390,188,070 which is also 9 digits. So when adding, the highest place would be the hundred millions. Wait, but when adding, maybe they sum to a 10-digit number because of the carryover?.\n\nWait, let me check again. Let's recount the places properly. Let me write both numbers with their place values:.\n\nFirst number: 940,174,722.\nBreaking down:.\n- Billions place: 9 (since 900,000,000).\n- Hundred millions: 4.\n- Ten millions: 0.\n- Millions: 1.\n- Hundred thousands: 7.\n- Ten thousands: 4.\n- Thousands: 7.\n- Hundreds: 2.\n- Tens: 2.\n- Ones: 2?.\n\nWait, maybe I need to structure this properly. Let me write them digit by digit:.\n\nNumber1: 9 4 0 1 7 4 7 2 2.\nNumber2: 3 9 0 1 8 8 0 7 0.\n\nWait, actually, when written out, 940,174,722 is 9 digits:.\n\nPosition from right (starting at 1):.\n1: 2 (ones).\n2: 2 (tens).\n3: 7 (hundreds).\n4: 4 (thousands).\n5: 7 (ten thousands).\n6: 1 (hundred thousands).\n7: 0 (millions).\n8: 4 (ten millions).\n9: 9 (hundred millions).\n\nSimilarly, 390,188,070 is also 9 digits:.\n\nPosition from right:.\n1: 0.\n2: 7.\n3: 0.\n4: 8.\n5: 8.\n6: 1.\n7: 9 (millions place here? Wait, let's see:.\n\nWait, maybe I should write them aligned properly:.\n\nLet me write both numbers with their place values:.\n\nFirst number (940,174,722):.\nBillions: 9.\nHundred millions: 4.\nTen millions: 0.\nMillions: 1.\nHundred thousands: 7.\nTen thousands: 4.\nThousands: 7.\nHundreds: 2.\nTens: 2.\nOnes: 2? Wait, no, maybe I'm getting confused. Let's do it step by step.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to align them digit by digit from the right, so that each digit is correctly added.\n\nLet me write them as:.\n\n940,174,722.\n+390,188,070.\n------------.\n\nLet me write each digit:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nFirst number: 2.\nSecond number: 0 → sum ones: 2.\n\nTens place:.\nFirst: 2.\nSecond:7 → 2+7=9.\n\nHundreds place:.\nFirst:7.\nSecond:0 →7.\n\nThousands place:.\nFirst:4.\nSecond:8 →4+8=12 → write 2, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands place:.\nFirst:7.\nSecond:8 →7+8=15 + 1 (carryover)=16 → write 6, carryover 1.\n\nWait, hold on, maybe I made a mistake here. Let me go step by step again carefully.\n\nWait, maybe I should write both numbers with commas to see the places:.\n\nFirst number: 940,174,722 → which is 9 4 0 1 7 4 7 2 2.\n\nSecond number: 390,188,070 → 3 9 0 1 8 8 0 7 0.\n\nWait, perhaps breaking down each digit:.\n\nLet me list each digit from right to left (starting with ones):.\n\nNumber1: positions 1 to 9 (from right):.\n\n1: 2 (ones).\n2: 2 (tens).\n3:7 (hundreds).\n4:4 (thousands).\n5:7 (ten thousands).\n6:1 (hundred thousands).\n7:0 (millions).\n8:4 (ten millions).\n9:9 (hundred millions).\n\nNumber2: positions 1 to 9:.\n\n1:0.\n2:7.\n3:0 (hundreds place).\n4:8 (thousands).\n5:8 (ten thousands).\n6:1 (hundred thousands).\n7:9 (millions).\n8:3 (ten millions).\n9:3? Wait no. Wait, the number is 390,188,070.\n\nBreaking down 390,188,070:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nDigits: 0 (ones), 7 (tens), 0 (hundreds), 8 (thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 1 (hundred thousands), 9 (millions), 0 (ten millions?), wait no, let's parse it properly.\n\nWait, 390,188,070:.\n\nCommas are placed every three digits. So:.\n\n390,188,070 is 390 million, 188 thousand, and 70.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\n3 (hundred millions place), 9 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 0 (ones). Wait, that might not be right.\n\nWait, let's count each digit:.\n\n3 9 0 1 8 8 0 7 0.\n\nStarting from the left:.\n\nDigit 1: 3 (hundred millions place).\nDigit 2:9 (ten millions).\nDigit3:0 (millions).\nDigit4:1