ousands):3.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):8.\n\nPosition2 (tens):9.\n\nPosition1 (ones):2.\n\nSecond number:5 4 6 8 6 1 5 6 3.\n\nDigits from left to right:.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):5.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):4.\n\nPosition7 (millions):6.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):8.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):5.\n\nPosition2 (tens):6.\n\nPosition1 (ones):3.\n\nNow, adding each position from 1 to 9:.\n\nPosition1 (ones): 2 +3 =5.\n\nPosition2 (tens):9 +6=15 →5, carry1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):8 +5=13 +1 (carry) =14 →4, carry1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3 +1=4 + carry1 →5, no carry? Wait, 3 (first number's thousands place) plus 1 (second number's thousands place) is 4, plus the carried 1 makes 5. So write 5, no carry.\n\nWait, that's different from my first calculation. Previously I thought thousands place was 7+1, but that was a mistake. Because in the first number, the thousands place (position4) is 3, and the second number's thousands place is 1. So 3+1=4 plus carryover 1 from hundreds place makes 5. So that's correct.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7 (first number) +6 (second number) =13. No carryover from previous? Wait, the carryover from position4 was zero. So 7+6=13. So write 3 and carryover 1?.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, position5 is ten thousands place. Let me check again:.\n\nFirst number's ten thousands digit:7 (position5).\n\nSecond number's ten thousands digit:6 (position5).\n\nSo 7 +6 =13. Since there was no carryover from position4 (since we had 3+1+1=5?), so no carryover into position5. Wait, position4's addition resulted in 5 with no carryover. So yes, position5 is 7+6=13. So we write 3 and carryover 1 to the next position (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):8 (first number) +8 (second number) =16, plus the carried 1 makes 17. So write 7 and carryover 1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):9 (first number) +6 (second number) =15, plus the carried 1 →16. So write 6 and carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten