ber has 4, second has 8. 4+8=12 plus the 1 makes 13. Write 3, carry over 1. .\n\nTen millions place: first number has 7, second has 4. 7+4=11 plus 1 is 12. Write 2, carry over 1. .\n\nHundred millions place: first number is 6, second is 1. 6+1=7 plus the carried 1 makes 8. .\n\nWait, let me check if I missed any digits. Let's count the digits in each number. .\n\nFirst number: 647,958,859. Let's break it down:.\nDigits: 6 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 9 (ones). So that's 9 digits. .\n\nSecond number: 148,735,059. Breaking down:.\n1 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 9 (ones). Also 9 digits. .\n\nWait, so when adding, each corresponding place should be added. Let me go through again step by step to make sure I didn't make a mistake in the places. .\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes place: 9 +9=18 (8, carry 1).\nTens: 5+5+1=11 (1, carry1).\nHundreds:8+0+0=8? Wait, second number's hundreds digit is 0? Wait the second number is 148,735,059. Let me confirm:.\n\nBreaking down the second number again: 1 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 9 (ones). Yes, so hundreds place is 0. So first number's hundreds is 8. 8 +0 + carryover 1 from previous step? Wait no, the carryover from the tens place was 1, which was added there. Wait, let me redo the steps properly.\n\nWait maybe I made a mistake in the order. Let me list all digits with their place values:.\n\nFirst number: 6 4 7 9 5 8 8 5 9.\n\nSecond number:1 4 8 7 3 5 0 5 9.\n\nWait, let's write each digit in order from right (ones) to left (hundred millions):.\n\nFirst number: .\n\nPosition 1 (ones):9.\n\n2 (tens):5.\n\n3 (hundreds):8.\n\n4 (thousands):8.\n\n5 (ten thousands):5.\n\n6 (hundred thousands):9.\n\n7 (millions):7.\n\n8 (t