)*1,000 =4,000\n\n9 (hundreds)*100 =900\n\n4 (tens)*10 =40\n\n3 (units)=3.\n\nAdding these up: 200,000,000 +10,000,000 =210,000,000.\n\n+5,000,000 \u2192215,000,000.\n\n+600,000 \u2192215,600,000.\n\n+70,000 \u2192215,670,000.\n\n+4,000 \u2192215,674,000.\n\n+900 \u2192215,674,900.\n\n+40 \u2192215,674,940.\n\n+3 \u2192215,674,943. Correct.\n\nSimilarly, the second number 945,050,561:\n\n9 (hundred millions)*100M =900,000,000\n\n4 (ten millions)*10M =40,000,000\n\n5 (millions)*1M =5,000,000\n\n0 (hundred thousands) \u21920.\n\n5 (ten thousands)*10k =50,000.\n\n0 (thousands)*1k \u21920.\n\n5 (hundreds)*100 \u2192500.\n\n6 (tens)*10 \u219260.\n\n1 (units) \u21921.\n\nAdding up:\n\n900M +40M =940M.\n\n+5M \u2192945M.\n\n+0 \u2192945M.\n\n+50k \u2192945,050,000.\n\n+0 \u2192945,050,000.\n\n+500 \u2192945,050,500.\n\n+60 \u2192945,050,560.\n\n+1 \u2192945,050,561. Correct.\n\nNow adding the two numbers:\n\n215,674,943 +945,050,561.\n\nLet me add the hundred millions:\n\n200M +900M =1,100M.\n\nTen millions:\n\n10M +40M =50M.\n\nMillions:\n\n5M +5M =10M.\n\nHundred thousands:\n\n600k +0 =600k.\n\nTen thousands:\n\n70k +50k =120k.\n\nThousands:\n\n4k +0 =4k.\n\nHundreds:\n\n900 +500 =1,400.\n\nTens:\n\n40 +60 =100.\n\nUnits:\n\n3 +1 =4.\n\nNow adding all these:\n\nStart with the hundred millions:1,100,000,000.\n\nAdd ten millions:50,000,000 \u21921,150,000,000.\n\nAdd millions:10,000,000 \u21921,160,000,000.\n\nAdd hundred thousands:600,000 \u21921,160,600,000.\n\nAdd ten thousands:120,000 \u21921,160,720,000.\n\nAdd thousands:4,000 \u21921,160,724,000.\n\nAdd hundreds:1,400 \u21921,160,725,400.\n\nAdd tens:100 \u21921,160,725,500.\n\nAdd units:4 \u21921,160,725,504.\n\nThis matches the breakdown method's result of 1,160,725,504.\n\nBut the digit-by-digit approach gave 1,169,725,504. There's a contradiction.\n\nWait the digit-by-digit must be wrong. Let me check again.\n\nWait in the digit-by-digit method, when adding the hundred millions place (position9):\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit is2, second's is9. 2+9=11. So we write down1 and carryover1.\n\nThus, the hundred millions digit is1, and the carryover1 becomes the billions digit.\n\nThus the total would be 1 (billions) +1 (hundred millions) + the rest. So the number should be 1,169,725,504?\n\nWait but according to the breakdown, the hundred millions digits added are 2+9=11, so that gives a 1 in the hundred millions place and carryover to billions. So the billions digit is1, hundred millions is1, ten millions is6, millions is9, etc. So the number is indeed 1,169,725,504. But the breakdown method gives a different result. Why?\n\nAh! The breakdown method added the hundred millions, ten millions, etc., but in the digit-by-digit method, the ten millions place was 1+5=6. In the breakdown method, the ten millions place of first number is1, second is4 (wait no):\n\nWait in the breakdown method's ten millions place addition:\n\nFirst number's ten millions is1 (from 215,674,943's breakdown: ten millions is10,000,000 part).\n\nSecond number's ten millions is40,000,000 \u2192 so 4 in the ten millions place.\n\nWait so adding ten millions digits:1+4=5, but in the digit-by-digit method, the ten millions digits were 1 and5 (from the second number's ten millions digit).\n\nWait this is the key mistake!\n\nWait in the second number:945,050,561.\n\nBreaking down its ten millions place:\n\nThe number is 945,050,561.\n\nThe digits are (from right):\n\nPosition8 (ten millions) is the 8th digit from the right, which is the second digit from the left: '4' in 945,050,561?\n\nWait let's clarify the digit positions for the second number:\n\nSecond number:9 4 5 0 5 0 5 6 1.\n\nWait written as 945,050,561:\n\nDigits:\n\nPosition1 (units):1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):6.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):5.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):0.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):5.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0.\n\nPosition7 (millions):4 (the seventh digit from the right, which is the third digit from the left: '5' is the fi