n7 (hundred thousands),\n\n0 (millions),\n\n0 (ten millions),\n\n3 (hundred millions),\n\nand 1 (billions).\n\nSo when written in the correct order (billions down to ones), it's:\n\n1 (billions),\n\n3 (hundred millions),\n\n0 (ten millions),\n\n0 (millions),\n\n7 (hundred thousands),\n\n4 (ten thousands),\n\n1 (thousands),\n\n5 (hundreds),\n\n8 (tens),\n\n6 (ones).\n\nWait no, let's arrange them properly:\n\nThe reversed digits list (from the addition steps) is:\n\nAfter all positions and carryover, the reversed digits are:\n\n1 (billions place),\n\nthen the hundred millions digit is3,\n\nten millions0,\n\nmillions0,\n\nhundred thousands7,\n\nten thousands4,\n\nthousands1,\n\nhundreds5,\n\ntens8,\n\nones6.\n\nWait, perhaps the correct way is to take the reversed digits as:\n\nThe carryover1 is the leftmost digit (billions place),\n\nthen the position9 result is3 (hundred millions),\n\nposition8 result0 (ten millions),\n\nposition7 result0 (millions),\n\nposition6 result7 (hundred thousands),\n\nposition5 result4 (ten thousands),\n\nposition4 result1 (thousands),\n\nposition3 result5 (hundreds),\n\nposition2 result8 (tens),\n\nposition1 result6 (ones).\n\nTherefore, the actual number in standard form is:\n\n1 (billions),\n\n3 (hundred millions),\n\n0 (ten millions),\n\n0 (millions),\n\n7 (hundred thousands),\n\n4 (ten thousands),\n\n1 (thousands),\n\n5 (hundreds),\n\n8 (tens),\n\n6 (ones).\n\nSo putting that together: 1,300,741,586.\n\nWait, let's check with commas:\n\n1,300,741,586.\n\nWait, let's verify by adding the numbers another way.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made an error in the hundreds place earlier. Let me try adding using another method.\n\nFirst number: 357,304,362.\n\nSecond number:943,437,224.\n\nAdding them:\n\n357,304,362\n\n+943,437,224\n\n------------\n\nLet's add the millions:\n\n357,304,362 is 357 million,\n\nplus 943,437,224 is 943 million,\n\nso 357 +943 = 1,300 million.\n\nThen the remaining parts:\n\n304,362 +437,224 = ?\n\n304,362 +437,224: