(ten millions),\n\n8:7 (hundred millions).\n\nNow redoing the addition with correct digit positions:\n\nPosition0:3+0=3.\n\nPosition1:7+1=8.\n\nPosition2:3+1=4.\n\nPosition3:6+5=11 \u21921, carry1.\n\nPosition4:7 (first's ten thousands) +8 (second's ten thousands) =15 +1=16 \u21926, carry1.\n\nPosition5:5 (first's hundred thousands) +5 (second's) =10 +1=11 \u21921, carry1.\n\nPosition6:7 (first's millions) +6 (second's millions)=13 +1=14 \u21924, carry1.\n\nPosition7:8 (first's ten millions) +9 (second's ten millions)=17 +1=18 \u21928, carry1.\n\nPosition8:7 (first's hundred millions) +7 (second's)=14 +1=15 \u21925, carry1.\n\nThen the carryover1 to position9 (billions):1.\n\nSo the digits now are:\n\nPosition9:1,\n\nPosition8:5,\n\nPosition7:8,\n\nPosition6:4,\n\nPosition5:1,\n\nPosition4:6,\n\nPosition3:1,\n\nPosition2:4,\n\nPosition1:8,\n\nPosition0:3.\n\nTherefore, the number is:\n\n1 (billions),\n\n5 (hundred millions),\n\n8 (ten millions),\n\n4 (millions),\n\n1 (hundred thousands),\n\n6 (ten thousands),\n\n1 (thousands),\n\n4 (hundreds),\n\n8 (tens),\n\n3 (units).\n\nPutting commas:\n\n1,5 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 4 (millions) \u2192 1,584, then 161,483.\n\nSo it's 1,584,161,483. That matches the other method's result. So my earlier mistake was misassigning the digits in positions6 and7 for the first number. I had the millions digit as 8 instead of7, which threw off the calculations.\n\nTherefore, the correct sum is 1,584,161,483.\n\nPhew! That was a bit of a rollercoaster. So the key was ensuring that each digit's position was correctly identified, especially in the higher places. It's easy to miscount when dealing with large numbers, so breaking them down carefully is essential.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 787576373 and 796585110?  \n# Reasoning:  \nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.  \n1. The ones place: \\(3 + 0 = 3\\)  \n2. The tens place: \\(7 + 1 = 8\\)  \n3. The hundreds place: \\(3 + 1 = 4\\)  \n4. The thousands place: \\(6 + 5 = 11\\) (Write down 1, carry over 1)  \n5. The ten thousands place: \\(7 + 8 + 1 = 16\\) (Write down 6, carry over 1)  \n6. The hundred thousands place: \\(5 + 5 + 1 = 11\\) (Write down 1, carry over 1)  \n7. The millions place: \\(7 + 6 + 1 = 14\\) (Write down 4, carry over 1)  \n8. The ten millions place: \\(8 + 9 + 1 = 18\\) (Write down 8, carry over 1)  \n9. The hundred millions place: \\(7 + 7 + 1 = 15\\) (Write down 5, carry over 1)  \n10. The billions place: \\(1\\) (from the carryover)  \n\nCombining all the results:  \n- Billions: 1  \n- Hundred millions: 5  \n- Ten millions: 8  \n- Millions: 4  \n- Hundred thousands: 1  \n- Ten thousands: 6  \n- Thousands: 1  \n- Hundreds: 4  \n- Tens: 8  \n- Ones: 3  \n\nAnswer:  \nTherefore, the final computed sum is **1,584,161,483**.",
