llions), 4 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 4 (hundreds?), Wait no, maybe I need to reconstruct the digits step by step.\n\nWait the positions after adding:\n\nPositions 0 to 8 plus the carryover:\n\nPositions:\n\nAfter adding each position, we have:\n\nPosition 0: 3\n\n1:8\n\n2:4\n\n3:1 (with carryover)\n\n4:6 (from 16, so the digit here is 6, carryover 1)\n\n5:1 (from 11, carryover1)\n\n6:5 (from 15, carryover1)\n\n7:7 (from 17, carryover1)\n\n8:5 (from 15, carryover1)\n\nThen the carryover 1 becomes position 9 (billions).\n\nSo the digits from position 0 to 9 are:\n\nPosition 9:1\n\n8:5\n\n7:7\n\n6:5\n\n5:1\n\n4:6\n\n3:1\n\n2:4\n\n1:8\n\n0:3\n\nSo writing the number from highest to lowest:\n\n1 (billions), 5 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions?), Wait let's see:\n\nWait the positions are:\n\nPosition 9: billions digit is 1.\n\nPosition8: hundred millions:5\n\nPosition7: ten millions:7\n\nPosition6: millions digit:5\n\nPosition5: hundred thousands:1\n\nPosition4: ten thousands:6\n\nPosition3: thousands digit:1\n\nPosition2: hundreds:4\n\nPosition1: tens:8\n\nPosition0: units:3\n\nTherefore the number is 1,5 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 5 (millions), so 1,575,161,483? Wait, no, let's build it step by step:\n\nStarting from position9 (billions): 1\n\nThen position8 (hundred millions):5 → so 1,5...\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):7 → 1,57...\n\nPosition6 (millions):5 → 1,575...\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):1 → 1,575,1...\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):6 → 1,575,16...\n\nPosition3 (thousands):1 → 1,575,161...\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):4 → 1,575,161,4...\n\nPosition1 (tens):8 → 1,575,161,48...\n\nPosition0 (units):3 → 1,575,161,483.\n\nWait that would make it 1,575,161,483. But earlier I thought 1,584,161,483. Hmm, discrepancy here. Where did I go wrong?\n\nWait let's re-examine the steps:\n\nStarting with position 8 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit: 7\n\nSecond number's hundred millions digit:7\n\nAd