fusing. Let me try adding each place's value:\n\nMillions:\n\nFirst number's millions part is 9,000,000.\n\nSecond number's millions part is 1,000,000.\n\nTotal millions: 10,000,000 → which is 10M, which when added to the ten millions parts.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to just stick with the digit-wise addition which gave 881,511,657. Let me see:\n\n469,821,234 + 411,690,423.\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\n469,821,234\n\n+411,690,423\n\nLet me add the first three digits (hundred millions, ten millions, millions):\n\n469 (million) + 411 (million) = 880 million.\n\nThen the next parts:\n\n821 thousand (from first number) + 690 thousand (from second) = 1,511 thousand (since 821+690=1,511). So that's 1,511,000.\n\nThen the remaining parts:\n\n234 +423 = 657.\n\nSo putting it all together: 880,000,000 + 1,511,000 = 881,511,000, plus 657 gives 881,511,657. Exactly matches the previous result. So that must be correct.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 881,511,657.\n\nBut let me check once more with another approach. Maybe using approximate rounding to see if it's in the ballpark.\n\n469 million plus 411 million is roughly 880 million. The exact numbers would be a bit higher because the remaining parts (821,234 and 690,423) add to about 1.5 million. So total is 881.5 million, which aligns with the exact calculation. So that's a good check.\n\nAlright, I think that's solid. The sum is 881,511,657.\n</think>", '']
