ousands: 9 + 0 = 9 → 9, no carryover.\n6. Hundred thousands: 2 + 8 = 10 → 0 with carryover 1.\n7. Millions: 0 + 8 + 1 (carryover) = 9 → 9, no carryover.\n8. Ten millions: 5 + 6 = 11 → 1 with carryover 1.\n9. Hundred millions: 6 + 9 + 1 (carryover) = 16 → 6 with carryover 1.\n10. Billions: The carryover 1 → 1.\n\nNow, writing all the digits from the right to left (since we started from ones as first digit):.\n\nThe digits are:.\n\nBillions place: 1.\n\nHundred millions: 6.\n\nTen millions: 1 (from the 11 in ten millions place, but wait, no. Wait, the ten millions place sum was 11, so the digit written is 1 and carryover 1. Then the hundred millions place sum was 16 (so digit 6 with carryover 1 to billions). So the digits in order from highest to lowest (billions down to ones):.\n\nBillions: 1.\n\nHundred millions: 6.\n\nTen millions: 1.\n\nMillions: 9.\n\nHundred thousands: 0.\n\nTen thousands: 9.\n\nThousands: 9.\n\nHundreds: 9.\n\nTens: 6.\n\nOnes: 8.\n\nPutting all together:.\n\n1 (billion), 6 (hundred million), 1 (ten million), 9 (million), 0 (hundred thousand), 9 (ten thousand), 9 (thousand), 9 (hundred), 6 (ten), 8 (one).\n\nWait, let me structure this properly:.\n\nThe number is:.\n\n1,610,999,689?.\n\nWait, let me see:.\n\nWait, let me count each digit's place:.\n\nBillions place (10^9): 1.\n\nHundred millions (10^8): 6.\n\nTen millions (10^7): 1.\n\nMillions (10^6): 9.\n\nHundred thousands (10^5): 0.\n\nTen thousands (10^4): 9.\n\nThousands (10^3): 9.\n\nHundreds (10^2): 9.\n\nTens (10^1): 6.\n\nOnes (10^0):8.\n\nSo writing this as a number:.\n\n1,610,999,689?.\n\nWait, let's check:.\n\nBreaking down the digits:.\n\nBillions digit:1.\n\nHundred millions:6 → so 1 (billion) + 6 (hundred million) → 1,600,000,000.\n\nThen ten millions:1 → adding 10,000,000 → total so far 1,610,000,000.\n\nMillions:9 → 9,000,000 → total 1,619,000,000.\n\nHundred thousands:0 → no addition here.\n\nTen thousands:9 → 90,000 → 1,619,090,000.\n\nThousands:9 → 9,000 → 1,619,099,000.\n\