osition9, we have a carryover of1, so we add a new digit at position10:1.\n\nNow the digits from position1 to position10 (right to left):\n\nPosition1:8\n\n2:6\n\n3:9\n\n4:9\n\n5:9\n\n6:0\n\n7:9\n\n8:1\n\n9:6\n\n10:1\n\nTo write the number from left to right (highest to lowest place):\n\nPosition10 (billions):1,\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):6,\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):1,\n\nPosition7 (millions):9,\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0,\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):9,\n\nPosition4 (thousands):9,\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9,\n\nPosition2 (tens):6,\n\nPosition1 (ones):8.\n\nThus, the number is 1,610,999,968.\n\nWait, but according to the other method where I added 650M +968M =1618M, plus the smaller parts totaling 1,099,968 gives 1,619,099,968. There's a difference here. Which is correct?\n\nHmm, I must have messed up in one of the methods. Let me do the addition directly:\n\nLet me write the two numbers:\n\nFirst number:650,292,614\n\nSecond number:968,807,354\n\nAdding them:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n4 +4 =8.\n\nNext digits:\n\n1+5=6.\n\n6+3=9.\n\n2+7=9.\n\n9+0=9.\n\n2+8=10 (write 0, carry1).\n\n0 (millions place of first) +8 (millions of second) plus 1 carried =9.\n\n5 (ten millions of first) +6 (ten millions of second)=11 (write1, carry1).\n\n6 (hundred millions of first) +9 (hundred millions of second) +1 carried=16 (write6, carry1).\n\nFinally the carried 1 becomes the billions digit.\n\nSo the result is 1 (billion), 6 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nWhich is 1,610,999,968.\n\nWait, but when I broke it down into millions earlier, I must have made a mistake. Let me check that again.\n\nFirst number:650,292,614 is 650 million plus 292,614.\n\nSecond number:968,807,354 is 968 million plus 807,354.\n\nAdding the millions:650 +968 =1,618 million.\n\nAdding the smaller parts:292,614 +807,354.\n\nLet's compute 292,614 +807,354:\n\n292,614 +800,000 =1,092,614\n\nThen add 7,354: 1,092,614 +7,354 =1,099,968.\n\nThus total is 1,618,000,000 +1,099,968 =1,619,099,968.\n\nHmm, so there's a discrepancy between the two methods. Which is correct?\n\nWait, this is confusing. Let me add the two numbers digit by digit again:\n\nFirst number:650,292,614\n\nSecond number:968,807,354\n\nAdding:\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n     650,292,614\n+  968,807,354\n-----------------\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:4+4=8\n\nTens:1+5=6\n\nHundreds:6+3=9\n\nThousands:2+7=9\n\nTen thousands:9+0=9\n\nHundred thousands:2+8=10 (write 0, carryover1)\n\nMillions:0+8 +1=9\n\nTen millions:5+6=11 (write1, carryover1)\n\nHundred millions:6+9 +1=16 (write6, carryover1)\n\nBillions: carryover1.\n\nSo the total is 1,610,999,968.\n\nBut when I broke it into millions, I got 1,619,099,968. Where's the mistake?\n\nAh! I see now. In the breakdown method:\n\nFirst number is 650,292,614: 650 million plus 292,614. That's correct.\n\nSecond number is 968,807,354: 968 million plus 807,354. Correct.\n\nAdding millions:650 +968 =1,618 million. Correct.\n\nThen adding the smaller parts:292,614 +807,354.\n\nWait, 292,614 +807,354:\n\nLet me compute this again:\n\n292,614 +807,354:\n\nAdding the thousands:\n\n292,000 +807,000 =1,100,000.\n\nThen the remaining parts: 614 +354 =968.\n\nThus total is 1,100,000 +968 =1,100,968.\n\nWait, so 292,614 +807,354 =1,100,968.\n\nThen adding to 1,618,000,000:\n\n1,618,000,000 +1,100,968 =1,619,100,968? Wait, no:\n\nWait, 1,618,000,000 +1,100,968 =1,619,100,968? Wait, no, 1,618,000,000 + 1,100,968 is 1,619,100,968?\n\nWait, 1,618,000,000 + 1,000,000 =1,619,000,000.\n\nThen add the remaining 100,968 \u2192 total is 1,619,100,968. But this contradicts the digit-by-digit result of 1,610,999,968.\n\nWait, there's a big discrepancy here. I must have messed up in the breakdown approach.\n\nWait, perhaps my mistake was in the breakdown approach. Let me recalculate the smaller parts.\n\nFirst number's smaller part is 292,614.\n\nSecond number's smaller part is 807,354.\n\nAdding those two:\n\n292,614\n\n+807,354\n\n--------\n\nAdding step by step:\n\nUnits:4+4=8\n\nTens:1+5=6\n\nHundreds:6+3=9\n\nThousands:2+7=9\n\nTen thousands:9+0=9\n\nHundred thousands:2+8=10 \u2192 write 0, carryover1.\n\nWait, but since we are adding only the smaller parts (up to hundred thousands?), wait no. Wait the smaller parts are the remaining digits beyond the millions.\n\nWait the first number is 650,292,614: the \"smaller part\" after 650 million is 292,614, which is up to hundred thousands.\n\nSimilarly, the second number's smaller part is 807,354 (after 968 million).\n\nAdding those two smaller parts:\n\n292,614 +807,354.\n\nLet me do it digit by digit:\n\nOnes:4+4=8\n\nTens:1+5=6\n\nHundreds:6+3=9\n\nThousands:2+7=9\n\nTen thousands:9+0=9\n\nHundred thousands:2+8=10 \u2192 write 0, carryover1.\n\nSo the total of the smaller parts is 1,099,968 (since the carryover1 adds to the next digit, which is the millions place). Wait, but since we're only adding the smaller parts (which are below a million?), no, actually the smaller parts include up to hundred thousands, so when adding, the carryover would go to the millions.\n\nWait, the total of the smaller parts would be 1,099,968? Let me compute directly:\n\n292,614 + 807,354:\n\n292,614 + 800,000 = 1,092,614\n\nThen add 7,354: 1,092,614 +7,354 = 1,099,968. Yes, that's correct.\n\nThus total is 1,618,000,000 +1,099,968 =1,619,099,968.\n\nBut according to digit-by-digit, it's 1,610,999,968. There's a difference of 8,100,000. Where is this coming from?\n\nAh! I realize now. In the digit-by-digit addition, the millions place was calculated as follows:\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 0 (from 650,292,614: the millions place is the 7th digit from the right, which is 0).\n\nSecond number's millions digit is 8 (from 968,807,354: the millions digit is the 7th digit from the right, which is 8).\n\nAdding those:0+8 plus the carryover from hundred thousands (which was 1) gives 0+8+1=9. So the millions digit is 9. That's correct.\n\nThen the ten millions place: first number's ten millions digit is5 (from 650,292,614: 5 is in the 8th digit from the right), second number's ten millions digit is6 (from 968,807,354: 6 is in the 8th digit). Adding 5+6=11. So write 1, carryover1 to hundred millions.\n\nHundred millions: first is6, second is9, plus carryover1:6+9+1=16 \u21926 with carryover1.\n\nThus the hundred millions digit is6, and the billions digit is1.\n\nThus, the total is:\n\n1 (billions), 6 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nWhich is 1,610,999,968.\n\nBut the breakdown method gave 1,619,099,968. Wait, the difference is in the hundred millions place: 6 vs 9?\n\nWait in the breakdown approach, I said 650 million plus 968 million =1,618 million. But 650+968 is indeed 1,618 million. However, the digit-by-digit method's hundred millions digit is 6, implying the hundred millions part is 600 million, but the breakdown says 1,618 million.\n\nWait, the digit-by-digit method's breakdown is:\n\nThe hundred millions digit is 6 (from the sum 6+9+1=16 \u2192 digit6, carry1). The total hundred millions place is 600,000,000. Then the ten millions digit is1, so 10,000,000. The millions digit is9, so 9,000,000. So the total from hundred millions down is 600M +10M +9M =619M. But the breakdown approach had 1,618M from the millions and higher. Wait, confusion arises because the digit-by-digit result includes the carryover into the billions.\n\nWait, the total number is 1,610,999,968.\n\nBreaking that down:\n\n1,610,999,968:\n\n- 1 billion (1,000,000,000)\n\n- 610 million (610,000,000)\n\n- 999 thousand (999,000)\n\n- 968.\n\nSo total is 1,000,000,000 +610,000,000 =1,610,000,000, plus 999,968 =1,610,999,968.\n\nWhereas the breakdown approach got 1,619,099,968.\n\nAh! The error in the breakdown approach was in the millions addition. The first number is 650 million, the second is 968 million. 650 +968 is indeed 1,618 million. But in the digit-by-digit result, the total is 1,610 million plus..., which is less.\n\nWait, clearly there's a mistake here. Let me compute the two numbers with another method, perhaps using calculator-like steps:\n\n650,292,614\n\n+968,807,354\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n4 +4 =8.\n\n1+5=6.\n\n6+3=9.\n\n2+7=9.\n\n9+0=9.\n\n2+8=10 (carryover1).\n\n0+8+1=9.\n\n5+6=11 (carryover1).\n\n6+9+1=16 (carryover1).\n\nFinally the carryover1.\n\nSo writing the digits as per positions:\n\nThe digits from right to left are 8,6,9,9,9,0,9,1,6,1.\n\nSo when written left to right, it's 1 (billion),6 (hundred millions),1 (ten millions),9 (millions),0 (hundred thousands),9 (ten thousands),9 (thousands),9 (hundreds),6 (tens),8 (ones). So 1,610,999,968.\n\nTherefore, the digit-by-digit method is correct, and the breakdown method had an error.\n\nThe mistake in the breakdown approach was in the calculation of the smaller parts. Wait, let me check again the smaller parts:\n\nFirst number's smaller part is 292,614 (after 650 million). Second's is807,354 (after 968 million). Their sum is 292,614 +807,354 =1,100,  (wait 292,614+807,354: 292,614 +800,000 =1,092,614 +7,354 =1,100, 968. So 1,100,968.\n\nAdding to the 1,618,000,000:\n\n1,618,000,000 +1,100,968 =1,619,100,968.\n\nWait but this contradicts the digit-by-digit. So where is the error?\n\nAh! Wait a second, the breakdown approach is wrong because the first number is 650,292,614. Breaking it into 650 million and 292,614 is correct. The second number is 968,807,354, which is 968 million and 807,354. Correct.\n\nAdding the millions: 650+968=1,618 million.\n\nAdding the smaller parts:292,614 +807,354=1,100,968.\n\nTotal sum is 1,618,000,000 +1,100,968 =1,619,100,968.\n\nBut according to digit-by-digit, it's1,610,999,968.\n\nThe discrepancy is 1,619,100,968 vs1,610,999,968 \u2192 difference of 8,100,000.\n\nWait, this suggests that one method is off by a significant amount. There must be an error in one of the methods.\n\nLet me try a different approach: adding the two numbers step by step.\n\nFirst number:650,292,614\n\nSecond number:968,807,354.\n\nAdding the two:\n\nLet's add 650,292,614 + 900,000,000 =1,550,292,614.\n\nThen add 68,807,354 to that result:\n\n1,550,292,614 +68,807,354.\n\nBreakdown:\n\n1,550,292,614 +60,000,000 =1,610,292,614.\n\nThen add 8,807,354:\n\n1,610,292,614 +8,000,000 =1,618,292,614.\n\nAdd remaining 807,354:\n\n1,618,292,614 +800,000 =1,619,092,614.\n\nThen add 7,354:1,619,092,614 +7,354 =1,619,100,  (wait 1,619,092,614 +7,354 =1,619,099,968.\n\nAh! Here we get 1,619,099,968. Which matches the breakdown method. So this contradicts the digit-by-digit approach's result of1,610,999,968.\n\nTherefore, my earlier digit-by-digit calculation must have an error.\n\nLet me re-examine the digit-by-digit steps carefully:\n\nFirst number:650,292,614\n\nDigits from right (position1 to position9):\n\nPosition1:4\n\n2:1\n\n3:6\n\n4:2\n\n5:9\n\n6:2\n\n7:0\n\n8:5\n\n9:6\n\nSecond number:968,807,354\n\nDigits from right:\n\nPosition1:4\n\n2:5\n\n3:3\n\n4:7\n\n5:0\n\n6:8\n\n7:8\n\n8:6\n\n9:9\n\nAdding each position with carryover:\n\nStart with carry=0.\n\nPosition1:4+4=8 \u2192 digit8, carry0.\n\nPosition2:1+5=6 \u21926, carry0.\n\nPosition3:6+3=9 \u21929, carry0.\n\nPosition4:2+7=9 \u21929, carry0.\n\nPosition5:9+0=9 \u21929, carry0.\n\nPosition6:2+8=10 \u21920, carry1.\n\nPosition7:0+8 +1(carry)=9 \u21929, carry0.\n\nPosition8:5+6=11 \u21921, carry1.\n\nPosition9:6+9 +1(carry)=16 \u21926, carry1.\n\nThen, the carryover1 becomes the next digit (position10):1.\n\nThus the digits from right to left (positions1-10):\n\n8,6,9,9,9,0,9,1,6,1.\n\nSo writing left to right (from position10 to 1):\n\nPosition10:1 (billions place),\n\nPosition9:6 (hundred millions),\n\nPosition8:1 (ten millions),\n\nPosition7:9 (millions),\n\nPosition6:0 (hundred thousands),\n\nPosition5:9 (ten thousands),\n\nPosition4:9 (thousands),\n\nPosition3:9 (hundreds),\n\nPosition2:6 (tens),\n\nPosition1:8 (ones).\n\nThus the number is:\n\n1 (billions), 6 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nThis is 1,610,999,968.\n\nBut the other methods give 1,619,099,968. There's a discrepancy of 8,100,000.\n\nWait, the error must be in the digit positions. Let me check the digit positions again.\n\nWait, the first number is 650,292,614.\n\nBreaking down the digits:\n\n- 6 (hundred millions),\n\n- 5 (ten millions),\n\n- 0 (millions),\n\n- 2 (hundred thousands),\n\n- 9 (ten thousands),\n\n- 2 (thousands),\n\n- 6 (hundreds),\n\n- 1 (tens),\n\n- 4 (ones).\n\nWait, maybe I misaligned the digits in the digit-by-digit method. Let's re-express the first number:\n\n650,292,614:\n\n- Billions:0,\n\n- Hundred millions:6,\n\n- Ten millions:5,\n\n- Millions:0,\n\n- Hundred thousands:2,\n\n- Ten thousands:9,\n\n- Thousands:2,\n\n- Hundreds:6,\n\n- Tens:1,\n\n- Ones:4.\n\nSecond number:968,807,354:\n\n- Hundred millions:9,\n\n- Ten millions:6,\n\n- Millions:8,\n\n- Hundred thousands:0,\n\n- Ten thousands:7,\n\n- Thousands:3,\n\n- Hundreds:5,\n\n- Tens:3,\n\n- Ones:4.\n\nWait no, perhaps I made a mistake in the digit positions for the second number.\n\nWait let's write both numbers with commas to see the places:\n\nFirst number:650,292,614 \u2192 650 million, 292 thousand, 614.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n6 (hundred millions),\n\n5 (ten millions),\n\n0 (millions),\n\n2 (hundred thousands),\n\n9 (ten thousands),\n\n2 (thousands),\n\n6 (hundreds),\n\n1 (tens),\n\n4 (ones).\n\nSecond number:968,807,354 \u2192 968 million, 807 thousand, 354.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n9 (hundred millions),\n\n6 (ten millions),\n\n8 (millions),\n\n8 (hundred thousands),\n\n0 (ten thousands),\n\n7 (thousands),\n\n3 (hundreds),\n\n5 (tens),\n\n4 (ones).\n\nAh! Here's the mistake in the digit-by-digit approach earlier.\n\nIn the digit-by-digit breakdown earlier, I had for the second number's hundred thousands digit as8 (correct), but in my previous digit-by-digit calculation, I considered the first number's hundred thousands digit as2 and the second's as8, which is correct. But for the millions digit:\n\nFirst number's millions digit is0 (since it's 650,292,614 \u2192 millions place is the fourth digit from the left after 6 (hundred millions),5 (ten millions),0 (millions). Wait, the millions digit is indeed0.\n\nSecond number's millions digit is8 (from 968,807,354 \u2192 8 in millions place).\n\nSo adding the millions digits:0 (first) +8 (second) + carryover1 from hundred thousands place (which was 2+8=10, so carryover1) \u21920+8+1=9. That's correct.\n\nTen millions place:\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is5, second's is6. 5+6=11 \u21921 with carryover1. Correct.\n\nHundred millions place:\n\nFirst's 6 + second's9 + carryover1 \u219216 \u21926 with carryover1. Correct.\n\nThus the digit-by-digit gives 1,610,999,968, but the other methods give 1,619,099,968. This suggests I made a mistake in one of the other methods.\n\nWait in the step-by-step addition when adding 650,292,614 + 968,807,354:\n\nLet me write them down:\n\n650,292,614\n\n+968,807,354\n\n=?\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:4+4=8\n\nTens:1+5=6\n\nHundreds:6+3=9\n\nThousands:2+7=9\n\nTen thousands:9+0=9\n\nHundred thousands:2+8=10 (0, carry1)\n\nMillions:0+8+1=9\n\nTen millions:5+6=11 (1, carry1)\n\nHundred millions:6+9+1=16 (6, carry1)\n\nBillions:1.\n\nSo the result is 1,610,999,968.\n\nBut when I did the addition via breaking into millions:\n\nFirst number:650,292,614 =650,000,000 +292,614\n\nSecond number:968,807,354=968,000,000 +807,354\n\nAdding the millions parts:650+968=1,618,000,000\n\nAdding the smaller parts:292,614+807,354=1,100, 968.\n\nTotal:1,618,000,000+1,100,968=1,619,100,968.\n\nWait, there's a discrepancy here. The digit-by-digit method gives 1,610,999,968 vs breakdown's 1,619,100,968.\n\n