ryover 1)\n\n6+9+1=16 (write 6, carryover1)\n\nFinally the carryover 1 at the beginning.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n8,6,9,9,9,0,9,1,6,1.\n\nWait, so when written from left to right, it\'s 1 (from the final carryover), then 6 (from the hundred millions place sum 16?), wait no, let me see.\n\nWait, when adding, the first digit (leftmost) is the carryover 1, then the next digits are the results from the hundred millions place (6+9+1=16 → 6 with carryover 1), but actually, the positions are:\n\nThe first carryover is from the hundred millions addition. Let me think again.\n\nWait, when adding the hundred millions digits (the 9th digit from the right):\n\nFirst number\'s hundred millions digit is 6 (since 650,292,614 is 6 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions) etc.), and the second number\'s hundred millions digit is 9 (from 968,807,354).\n\nWait, actually, perhaps I need to consider the exact digit positions again.\n\nWait, perhaps an alternative approach: Let me convert both numbers to their digit arrays and add each digit with carries.\n\nLet me write both numbers as digit lists from right to left (ones to billions):\n\nFirst number: 650,292,614 → digits (right to left):\n\n4,1,6,2,9,2,0,5,6.\n\nSecond number:968,807,354 → digits (right to left):\n\n4,5,3,7,0,8,8,6,9.\n\nWait, let\'s confirm:\n\nFirst number breakdown (right to left):\n\nPosition 1: ones:4\n\n2:tens:1\n\n3:hundreds:6\n\n4: thousands:2\n\n5: ten thousands:9\n\n6: hundred thousands:2\n\n7: millions:0\n\n8: ten millions:5\n\n9: hundred millions:6\n\nSecond number:\n\nPosition1:4\n\n2:5 (tens)\n\n3:3 (hundreds)\n\n4:7 (thousands)\n\n5:0 (ten thousands)\n\n6:8 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:8 (millions)\n\n8:6 (ten millions)\n\n9:9 (hundred millions)\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nStarting from position1 to position9, with possible carryover.\n\nLet me tabulate:\n\nPosition: 1 to 9, with carryover.\n\nInitialize carry =0.\n\nPosition1:\n\n4 +4 =8 +0 → total 8. Write 8, carry 0.\n\nPosition2:\n\n1+5=6 +0 →6. Write6, carry0.\n\nPosition3:\n\n6+3=9 +0 →9. Write9, carry0.\n\nPosition4:\n\n2+7=9 +0 →9. Write9, carry0.\n\nPosition5:\n\n9 +0=9 +0 →9. Write9, carry0.\n\nPosition6:\n\n2 (from first) +8 (from second)=10. 10 +0 carryover → total10. Write0, carry1.\n\nPosition7:\n\n0 (first) +8 (second)=8 + carry1 →9. Write9, carry0.\n\nPosition8:\n\n5 (first) +6 (second)=11 +0 →11. Write1, carry1.\n\nPosition9:\n\n6 (first) +9 (second)=15 + carry1 →16. Write6, carry1.\n\nAfter all positions, there\'s a remaining carry of 1. So we add a new digit (position10) with 1.\n\nThus, the digits from position1 to position10 (right to left) are:\n\nPosition1:8\n\n2:6\n\n3:9\n\n4:9\n\n5:9\n\n6:0\n\n7:9\n\n8:1\n\n9:6\n\n10:1.\n\nTherefore, writing from position10 to position1 (left to right):\n\n1 (position10),6 (position9),1 (position8),9 (position7),9 (position6),0 (position5?), wait wait need to be careful.\n\nWait, the digits from left to right (billions to ones):\n\nThe highest digit is position10 (billions place): 1.\n\nThen position9 (hundred millions):6.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):9.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):9.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):9.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9.\n\nPosition2 (tens):6.\n\nPosition1 (ones):8.\n\nSo the number is:\n\n1 (billion), 6 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nThus, the number is 1,610,999,968.\n\nWait a second, let me confirm with another method. Let me add the two numbers using another approach:\n\n650,292,614 + 968,807,354.\n\nLet me break them into parts:\n\n650,292,614 + 900,000,000 = 1,550,292,614.\n\nThen add 68,807,354:\n\n1,550,292,614 + 68,807,354.\n\nAdding 68 million to 1,550,292,614 gives 1,619,100,  (wait, 1,550,292,614 + 68,000,000 = 1,618,292,614. Then add the remaining 807,354.\n\nWait, maybe this is getting confusing. Let me compute 650,292,614 + 968,807,354.\n\nLet me add the millions:\n\n650,292,614 is 650 million plus 292,614.\n\n968,807,354 is 968 million plus 807,354.\n\nAdding the millions parts: 650 + 968 = 1,618 million.\n\nAdding the smaller parts: 292,614 + 807,354.\n\n292,614 + 800,000 = 1,092,614. Then add 7,354: 1,092,614 +7,354 = 1,100,  (wait 1,092,614 +7,354 = 1,099,968).\n\nSo total sum: 1,618,000,000 + 1,099,968 = 1,619,099,968.\n\nAh, so that\'s 1,619,099,968.\n\nWait, but in the previous calculation using digit-by-digit, I got 1,610,999,968. There\'s a discrepancy here. Which is correct?\n\nHmm, clearly I made an error in the digit-by-digit approach earlier. Let me retrace.\n\nWait in the digit-by-digit breakdown:\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions place):\n\nFirst number:6 (hundred millions digit), second number:9 (hundred millions digit). Adding them plus carryover from position8.\n\nWait let me re-express the digit positions properly.\n\nWait perhaps my confusion was in the digit positions. Let me try the digit-by-digit again carefully.\n\nThe first number is 650,292,614:\n\nBreaking down from right to left (positions 1 to 9):\n\nPosition1:4 (ones)\n\n2:1 (tens)\n\n3:6 (hundreds)\n\n4:2 (thousands)\n\n5:9 (ten thousands)\n\n6:2 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:0 (millions)\n\n8:5 (ten millions)\n\n9:6 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number 968,807,354:\n\nPosition1:4 (ones)\n\n2:5 (tens)\n\n3:3 (hundreds)\n\n4:7 (thousands)\n\n5:0 (ten thousands)\n\n6:8 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:8 (millions)\n\n8:6 (ten millions)\n\n9:9 (hundred millions)\n\nNow adding each position with carryover:\n\nStart with carry=0.\n\nPosition1:4+4=8 +0 →8, carry0.\n\nPosition2:1+5=6 +0 →6, carry0.\n\nPosition3:6+3=9 →9, carry0.\n\nPosition4:2+7=9 →9, carry0.\n\nPosition5:9+0=9 →9, carry0.\n\nPosition6:2+8=10 →0, carry1.\n\nPosition7:0+8 +1(carry)=9 →9, carry0.\n\nPosition8:5+6=11 →1, carry1.\n\nPosition9:6+9 +1(carry)=16 →6, carry1.\n\nNow, after position9, we have a carryover of1, so we add a new digit at position10:1.\n\nNow the digits from position1 to position10 (right to left):\n\nPosition1:8\n\n2:6\n\n3:9\n\n4:9\n\n5:9\n\n6:0\n\n7:9\n\n8:1\n\n9:6\n\n10:1\n\nTo write the number from left to right (highest to lowest place):\n\nPosition10 (billions):1,\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):6,\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):1,\n\nPosition7 (millions):9,\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0,\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):9,\n\nPosition4 (thousands):9,\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9,\n\nPosition2 (tens):6,\n\nPosition1 (ones):8.\n\nThus, the number is 1,610,999,968.\n\nWait, but according to the other method where I added 650M +968M =1618M, plus the smaller parts totaling 1,099,968 gives 1,619,099,968. There\'s a difference here. Which is correct?\n\nHmm, I must have messed up in one of the methods. Let me do the addition directly:\n\nLet me write the two numbers:\n\nFirst number:650,292,614\n\nSecond number:968,807,354\n\nAdding them:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n4 +4 =8.\n\nNext digits:\n\n1+5=6.\n\n6+3=9.\n\n2+7=9.\n\n9+0=9.\n\n2+8=10 (write 0, carry1).\n\n0 (millions place of first) +8 (millions of second) plus 1 carried =9.\n\n5 (ten millions of first) +6 (ten millions of second)=11 (write1, carry1).\n\n6 (hundred millions of first) +9 (hundred millions of second) +1 carried=16 (write6, carry1).\n\nFinally the carried 1 becomes the billions digit.\n\nSo the result is 1 (billion), 6 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nWhich is 1,610,999,968.\n\nWait, but when I broke it down into millions earlier, I must have made a mistake. Let me check that again.\n\nFirst number:650,292,614 is 650 million plus 292,614.\n\nSecond number:968,807,354 is 968 million plus 807,354.\n\nAdding the millions:650 +968 =1,618 million.\n\nAdding the smaller parts:292,614 +807,354.\n\nLet\'s compute 292,614 +807,354:\n\n292,614 +800,000 =1,092,614\n\nThen add 7,354: 1,092,614 +7,354 =1,099,968.\n\nThus total is 1,618,000,000 +1,099,968 =1,619,099,968.\n\nHmm, so there\'s a discrepancy between the two methods. Which is correct?\n\nWait, this is confusing. Let me add the two numbers digit by digit again:\n\nFirst number:650,292,614\n\nSecond number:968,807,354\n\nAdding:\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n     650,292,614\n+  968,807,354\n-----------------\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:4+4=8\n\nTens:1+5=6\n\nHundreds:6+3=9\n\nThousands:2+7=9\n\nTen thousands:9+0=9\n\nHundred thousands:2+8=10 (write 0, carryover1)\n\nMillions:0+8 +1=9\n\nTen millions:5+6=11 (write1, carryover1)\n\nHundred millions:6+9 +1=16 (write6, carryover1)\n\nBillions: carryover1.\n\nSo the total is 1,610,999,968.\n\nBut when I broke it into millions, I got 1,619,099,968. Where\'s the mistake?\n\nAh! I see now. In the breakdown method:\n\nFirst number is 650,292,614: 650 million plus 292,614. That\'s correct.\n\nSecond number is 968,807,354: 968 million plus 807,354. Correct.\n\nAdding millions:650 +968 =1,618 million. Correct.\n\nThen adding the smaller parts:292,614 +807,354.\n\nWait, 292,614 +807,354:\n\nLet me compute this again:\n\n292,614 +807,354:\n\nAdding the thousands:\n\n292,000 +807,000 =1,100,000.\n\nThen the remaining parts: 614 +354 =968.\n\nThus total is 1,100,000 +968 =1,100,968.\n\nWait, so 292,614 +807,354 =1,100,968.\n\nThen adding to 1,618,000,000:\n\n1,618,000,000 +1,100,968 =1,619,100,968? Wait, no:\n\nWait, 1,618,000,000 +1,100,968 =1,619,100,968? Wait, no, 1,618,000,000 + 1,100,968 is 1,619,100,968?\n\nWait, 1,618,000,000 + 1,000,000 =1,619,000,000.\n\nThen add the remaining 100,968 → total is 1,619,100,968. But this contradicts the digit-by-digit result of 1,610,999,968.\n\nWait, there\'s a big discrepancy here. I must have messed up in the breakdown approach.\n\nWait, perhaps my mistake was in the breakdown approach. Let me recalculate the smaller parts.\n\nFirst number\'s smaller part is 292,614.\n\nSecond number\'s smaller part is 807,354.\n\nAdding those two:\n\n292,614\n\n+807,354\n\n-------