mber\'s ten millions digit is5 (from 650,292,614: 5 is in the 8th digit from the right), second number\'s ten millions digit is6 (from 968,807,354: 6 is in the 8th digit). Adding 5+6=11. So write 1, carryover1 to hundred millions.\n\nHundred millions: first is6, second is9, plus carryover1:6+9+1=16 →6 with carryover1.\n\nThus the hundred millions digit is6, and the billions digit is1.\n\nThus, the total is:\n\n1 (billions), 6 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nWhich is 1,610,999,968.\n\nBut the breakdown method gave 1,619,099,968. Wait, the difference is in the hundred millions place: 6 vs 9?\n\nWait in the breakdown approach, I said 650 million plus 968 million =1,618 million. But 650+968 is indeed 1,618 million. However, the digit-by-digit method\'s hundred millions digit is 6, implying the hundred millions part is 600 million, but the breakdown says 1,618 million.\n\nWait, the digit-by-digit method\'s breakdown is:\n\nThe hundred millions digit is 6 (from the sum 6+9+1=16 → digit6, carry1). The total hundred millions place is 600,000,000. Then the ten millions digit is1, so 10,000,000. The millions digit is9, so 9,000,000. So the total from hundred millions down is 600M +10M +9M =619M. But the breakdown approach had 1,618M from the millions and higher. Wait, confusion arises because the digit-by-digit result includes the carryover into the billions.\n\nWait, the total number is 1,610,999,968.\n\nBreaking that down:\n\n1,610,999,968:\n\n- 1 billion (1,000,000,000)\n\n- 610 million (610,000,000)\n\n- 999 thousand (999,000)\n\n- 968.\n\nSo total is 1,000,000,000 +610,000,000 =1,610,000,000, plus 999,968 =1,610,999,968.\n\nWhereas the breakdown approach got 1,619,099,968.\n\nAh! The error in the breakdown approach was in the millions addition. The first number is 650 million, the second is 968 million. 650 +968 is indeed 1,618 million. But in the digit-by-digit result, the total is 1,610 million plus..., which is less.\n\nWait, clearly there\'s a mistake here. Let me compute the two numbers with another method, perhaps using calculator-like steps:\n\n650,292,614\n\n+968,807,354\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n4 +4 =8.\n\n1+5=6.\n\n6+3=9.\n\n2+7=9.\n\n9+0=9.\n\n2+8=10 (carryover1).\n\n0+8+1=9.\n\n5+6=11 (carryover1).\n\n6+9+1=16 (carryover1).\n\nFinally the carryover1.\n\nSo writing the digits as per positions:\n\nThe digits from right to left are 8,6,9,9,9,0,9,1,6,1.\n\nSo when written left to right, it\'s 1 (billion),6 (hundred millions),1 (ten millions),9 (millions),0 (hundred thousands),9 (ten thousands),9 (thousands),9 (hundreds),6 (tens),8 (ones). So 1,610,999,968.\n\nTherefore, the digit-by-digit method is correct, and the breakdown method had an error.\n\nThe mistake in the breakdown approach was in the calculation of the smaller parts. Wait, let me check again the smaller parts:\n\nFirst number\'s smaller part is 292,614 (after 650 million). Second\'s is807,354 (after 968 million). Their sum is 292,614 +807,354 =1,100,  (wait 292,614+807,354: 292,614 +800,000 =1,092,614 +7,354 =1,100, 968. So 1,100,968.\n\nAdding to the 1,618,000,000:\n\n1,618,000,000 +1,100,968 =1,619,100,968.\n\nWait but this contradicts the digit-by-digit. So where is the error?\n\nAh! Wait a second, the breakdown approach is wrong because the first number is 650,292,614. Breaking it into 650 million and 292,614 is correct. The second number is 968,807,354, which is 968 million and 807,354. Correct.\n\nAdding the millions: 650+968=1,618 million.\n\nAdding the smaller parts:292,614 +807,354=1,100,968.\n\nTotal sum is 1,618,000,000 +1,100,968 =1,619,100,968.\n\nBut according to digit-by-digit, it\'s1,610,999,968.\n\nThe discrepancy is 1,619,100,968 vs1,610,999,968 → difference of 8,100,000.\n\nWait, this suggests that one method is off by a significant amount. There must be an error in one of the methods.\n\nLet me try a