llions:6.\n- Hundred thousands:0.\n- Ten thousands:6.\n- Thousands:4.\n- Hundreds:9.\n- Tens:5.\n- Ones:7.\n\nWait, that might not be right. Let me parse each digit:.\n\nThe number 436,064,957:.\n\nDigits from left to right:.\n\n4 (hundred millions place).\n\n3 (ten millions).\n\n6 (millions).\n\n0 (hundred thousands).\n\n6 (ten thousands).\n\n4 (thousands).\n\n9 (hundreds).\n\n5 (tens).\n\n7 (ones).\n\nYes, so:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):7.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):5.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0.\n\nPosition7 (millions):6.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):4.\n\nSecond number: 631,579,777:.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\n6 (hundred millions).\n\n3 (ten millions).\n\n1 (millions).\n\n5 (hundred thousands).\n\n7 (ten thousands).\n\n9 (thousands).\n\n7 (hundreds).\n\n7 (tens).\n\n7 (ones).\n\nWait, let's list each digit:.\n\nNumber: 631,579,777.\n\nDigits from left to right:.\n\n6 (hundred millions).\n\n3 (ten millions).\n\n1 (millions).\n\n5 (hundred thousands).\n\n7 (ten thousands).\n\n9 (thousands).\n\n7 (hundreds).\n\n7 (tens).\n\n7 (ones).\n\nSo positions:.\n\nPosition1 (ones):7.\n\nPosition2 (tens):7.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):7? Wait, no, wait:.\n\nWait, the second number's breakdown:.\n\nWait, the number is 631,579,777.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\n- The first comma separates millions: 631 (million), then 579 (thousand), 777.\n\nSo:.\n\nHundred millions:6.\n\nTen millions:3.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nHundred thousands:5.\n\nTen thousands:7.\n\nThousands:9.\n\nHundreds:7.\n\nTens:7.\n\nOnes:7.\n\nWait, that would be:.\n\nPosition1 (ones):7.\n\nPosition2 (tens):7.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):7.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):9.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):5.\n\nPosition7 (millions):1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):6.\n\nWait, so let me confirm each digit again for the second number:.\n\n631,579,777:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes digit:7.\n\nTens digit:7 (since the last three digits are 777).\n\nHundreds digit:7 (the third from the right in the last three digits).\n\nThousands digit:9 (the first digit of the 'thousands' group, which is 579, so 9 is the thousands place? Wait, no. Wait the number is 631,579,777. The breakdown is:.\n\nThe rightmost three digits are 777 (units, tens, hundreds).\n\nNext three: 579 (thousands, ten thousands, hundred thousands? Wait, actually:.\n\nWait, the standard breakdown is:.\n\nEach comma separates thousands, millions, etc.\n\nSo 631,579,777 is 631 million, 579 thousand, 777.\n\nSo the digits:.\n\n- Millions part: 631 (each digit is hundred millions, ten millions, millions).\n\n- Thousand part: 579 (hundred thousands, ten thousands, thousands).\n\n- Units part:777 (hundreds, tens, ones).\n\nTherefore:.\n\nPosition1 (ones):7.\n\nPosition2 (tens):7.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):7.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):9.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):5.\n\nPosition7 (millions):1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):6.\n\nOkay, so going back to the addition:.\n\nStarting from Position1 (ones):.\n\nFirst number's ones:7.\n\nSecond number's ones:7.\n\n7 +7 =14. So 4 with carryover 1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):.\n\nFirst number's tens:5.\n\nSecond's tens:7.\n\n5+7=12, plus carryover 1 =13. So 3, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):.\n\nFirst number's hundreds:9.\n\nSecond's hundreds:7.\n\n9+7=16, plus carryover1=17. So 7, carryover1.\n\nWait, 9+7 is 16? Wait 9+7 is 16? Wait no, 9+7 is 16? Wait, 9+7 is 16? Wait, no, 9+7=16? Wait, no! 9+7 is actually 16? Wait, 9+7 is 16? Wait, no, 9+7 is 16? Wait, 9+7 is 16? Wait, 9+7 is 16? Wait, no, that's wrong. 9+7 is 16? Wait, no, 9+7=16? Wait, 9+7 is 16? Wait, no, that's incorrect. 9+7 equals 16? Wait, 9+7 is 16? Wait, no, 9+7 is 16? Wait, 9+7 is 16? Wait, that's wrong. 9+7 is 16? Wait, no, 9+7 is 16? Wait, I think I'm confused. Wait 9+7 is 16? Wait no, 9+7 is 16? Wait 