ion4 (thousands place):.\n\nFirst number's thousands digit is 4, second's thousands digit is 9 (from the second number's breakdown). .\n\nSo 4 +9 =13, plus the carryover 1 makes 14. So write 4, carryover 1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands place):.\n\nFirst number's ten thousands digit is 6, second's is 7 (from second number's breakdown). .\n\n6+7=13 plus carryover 1 is 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands place):.\n\nFirst number's hundred thousands digit is 0, second's is 5. .\n\n0+5=5 plus carryover 1 is 6. No carryover here.\n\nPosition7 (millions place):.\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 6, second's is 1. .\n\n6+1=7. No carryover.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions place):.\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 3, second's is 3. .\n\n3+3=6. No carryover.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions place):.\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit is4, second's is6. .\n\n4+6=10. So write 0 and carryover 1. .\n\nBut since there are no more digits, that carryover 1 becomes a new digit at the beginning.\n\nSo putting all together, starting from the right:.\n\nPositions:.\n\n1:4.\n\n2:3.\n\n3:7.\n\n4:4.\n\n5:4.\n\n6:6.\n\n7:7.\n\n8:6.\n\n9:0 (with a carryover 1).\n\nAnd the final carryover 1 adds a new digit at the front.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:.\n\n4 (ones), 3 (tens),7 (hundreds),4 (thousands),4 (ten thousands),6 (hundred thousands),7 (millions),6 (ten millions),0 (hundred millions), and then the carryover 1 makes it 1 at the billions place.\n\nSo the final number is 1,067,646,744?.\n\nWait let me write the digits in order from highest to lowest:.\n\nStarting with the carried over 1 (billions place), then 0 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands),7 (hundreds),3 (tens),4 (ones).\n\nWait, let's list each position's result:.\n\nStarting from the first carryover:.\n\nPosition 10 (billions place):1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):0.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):6.\n\nPosition7 (millions):7.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):6.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):4.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):7.\n\nPosition2 (tens):3.\n\nPosition1 (ones):4.\n\nSo the number is 1,0 (hundred millions),6 (ten millions),7 (millions),6 (hundred thousands),4 (ten thousands),4 (thousands),7 (hundreds),3 (tens),4 (ones). .\n\nSo combining these digits:.\n\n1 (billions) 0 (hundred millions) 6 (ten millions) 7 (millions) 6 (hundred thousands) 4 (ten thousands) 4 (thousands) 7 (hundreds) 3 (tens) 4 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
