(ones).\n2:2 (tens).\n3:7 (hundreds).\n4:3 (thousands).\n5:8 (ten thousands).\n6:8 (hundred thousands).\n7:0 (millions).\n8:9 (ten millions).\n9:1 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to write both numbers with all digits aligned:.\n\nLet me write both numbers with leading zeros to make them the same length. Both are 9 digits. Let's see:.\n\n681,337,223 is 6 8 1 3 3 7 2 2 3.\n\n109,883,727 is 1 0 9 8 8 3 7 2 7.\n\nWait, actually, when written out as digits:.\n\nFirst number: 6 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 3 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 1 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nSo aligning each digit:.\n\nHundred millions: 6 + 1 = 7.\nTen millions: 8 + 0 = 8.\nMillions: 1 + 9 = 10.\nHundred thousands: 3 + 8 = 11.\nTen thousands: 3 + 8 = 11.\nThousands:7 +3 =10.\nHundreds:2 +7=9.\nTens:2 +2=4.\nOnes:3 +7=10.\n\nWait, but when adding, we have to do it column by column from the right, carrying over as needed. Maybe I need to go step by step properly.\n\nStarting from the ones place (rightmost digit):.\n\nOnes place: 3 (from first number) + 7 (from second) = 10. Write down 0, carryover 1.\n\nTens place: 2 + 2 = 4, plus carryover 1 = 5. Write 5, no carryover.\n\nHundreds place: 2 (from first number) +7 (from second) =9. Wait, first number's hundreds digit is 2? Let me confirm:.\n\nWait first number is 681,337,223. Breaking down:.\n\nLet me write it as digits from left to right:.\n\n6 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 3 (ones).\n\nYes, so the hundreds place is the 7th digit from the right? Wait, perhaps I need to list each digit's place properly.\n\nAlternatively, let me write both numbers digit by digit from right (ones) to left (hundred millions):.\n\nFirst number: 3 (1), 2 (10), 2 (100), 7 (1000), 3 (10,000), 3 (100,000), 1 (1,000,000), 8 (10,000,000), 6 (100,000,000).\n\nSecond number:7 (1), 2 (10), 7 (100), 3 (1000), 8 (10,000), 8 (100,000), 9 (1,000,000), 0 (10,000,000), 1 (100,000,000).\n\nNow adding each column:.\n\n1. Ones place: 3 +7 =10 → 0, carryover 1.\n\n2. Tens place: 2 +2 =4 +1 (carry) =5 → 5, no carry.\n\n3. Hundreds place:2 +7 =9 →9, no carry.\n\nWait, first number's hundreds digit is the third from the right, which is 2 (since first number's digits from right are 3,2,2,7,..). Second number's hundreds digit is 7 (third from right: 7). So 2+7=9.\n\n4. Thousands place: first number's thousands digit is 7, second's is 3 →7+3=10. So write 0, carryover 1.\n\n5. Ten thousands place: first has 3, second has 8 →3+8=11, plus carryover 1 =12 → write 2, carryover 1.\n\n6. Hundred thousands place: first has 3, second has 8 →3+8=11 +1 (carry) =12 → write 2, carryover 1.\n\n7. Millions place: first has 1, second has9 →1+9=10 +1 (carry)=11 → write 1, carryover1.\n\n8. Ten millions place: first has8, second has0 →8+0=8 +1 (carry)=9 → write9, no carry.\n\n9. Hundred millions place:6 +1=7 →7.\n\nPutting all digits together from last to first:.\n\nStarting from the ones digit we have 0 (ones),5 (tens),9 (hundreds),0 (thousands),2 (ten thousands),2 (hundred thousands),1 (millions),9 (ten millions),7 (hundred millions). Wait but let me check the steps again.\n\nWait let me go step by step again carefully:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones):.\n\n1. Ones place: 3+7=10 → 0, carryover 1.\n\n2. Tens: 2+2=4 +1=5 →5, carryover 0.\n\n3. Hundreds: 2 +7=9 →9, carryover 0.\n\n4. Thousands:7 (from first number) +3 (second)=10 →0, carryover1.\n\n5. Ten thousands:3 +8=11 +1 (carry)=12 →2, carryover1.\n\n6. Hundred thousands:3 +8=11 +1 (carry)=12 →2, carryover1.\n\n7. Millions:1 (from first number) +9 (second)=10 +1 (carry)=11 →1, carryover1.\n\n8. Ten millions:8 (first) +0 (second)=8 +1 (carry)=9 →9, no carry.\n\n9. Hundred millions:6 +1=7 →7.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:.\n\n0 (ones),5,9,0,2,2,1,9,7.\n\nWait, but when written as a number, the digits are arranged from highest to lowest place, so the final number would be 7 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands),9 (hundreds),5 (tens),0 (ones). Wait, let me arrange them correctly.\n\nWait the digits in each place:.\n\n- Hundred millions:7.\n\n- Ten millions:9.\n\n- Millions:1.\n\n- Hundred thousands:2.\n\n- Ten thousands:2.\n\n- Thousands:0.\n\n- Hundreds:9.\n\n- Tens:5.\n\n- Ones:0</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
