ousands place: 2 + 0 = 2, plus 1 is 3. Write 3, no carry.\n\nMillions place: 7 + 9 = 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n\nTen millions place: 2 + 7 = 9, plus 1 is 10. Write 0, carry 1.\n\nHundred millions place: 4 + 6 = 10, plus 1 is 11. Write 11.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the leftmost digit:.\n\n1 (from the carried 11?), wait let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the digit positions. Let me go step by step again carefully.\n\nLet me write each number with their place values:.\n\nFirst number: 427,275,637.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nHundred millions: 4.\n\nTen millions: 2.\n\nMillions:7.\n\nHundred thousands: 2.\n\nTen thousands:7.\n\nThousands:5.\n\nHundreds:6.\n\nTens:3.\n\nOnes:7.\n\nSecond number: 679,063,968.\n\nHundred millions:6.\n\nTen millions:7.\n\nMillions:9.\n\nHundred thousands:0.\n\nTen thousands:6.\n\nThousands:3.\n\nHundreds:9.\n\nTens:6.\n\nOnes:8.\n\nNow adding each column from right to left:.\n\nOnes: 7 +8=15. Record 5, carryover 1.\n\nTens:3 +6=9 +1=10. Record 0, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:6 +9=15 +1=16. Record6, carryover1.\n\nThousands:5 +3=8 +1=9. Record9, no carry.\n\nTen thousands:7 +6=13. Record3, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:2 +0=2 +1=3. Record3, no carry.\n\nMillions:7 +9=16. Record6, carryover1.\n\nTen millions:2 +7=9 +1=10. Record0, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:4 +6=10 +1=11. So record 1 and carryover1, but since there are no more digits, that 1 is added as a new digit at the front.\n\nSo putting all the digits together from the right:.\n\nStarting with the ones digit: 5.\n\nTens:0.\n\nHundreds:6.\n\nThousands:9.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nHundred thousands:3.\n\nMillions:6.\n\nTen millions:0.\n\nHundred millions:1 (from the 11?), but wait the hundred millions place after adding had 11, so the total would be 11 in the hundred millions plus the carried over 1? Wait no, let's retrace.\n\nWait when adding the hundred millions place:.\n\nThe two numbers have 4 and 6 in the hundred millions place. Adding those gives 10, plus the carried over 1 from the ten millions place addition (which was 2+7 +1=10). Wait, let me redo the steps again carefully:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\n1. Ones: 7 +8=15. Write 5, carryover1.\n\n2. Tens: 3 +6=9 +1=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\n3. Hundreds:6 +9=15 +1=16. Write6, carryover1.\n\n4. Thousands:5 +3=8 +1=9. Write9, carryover0.\n\n5. Ten thousands:7 +6=13. Write3, carryover1.\n\n6. Hundred thousands:2 +0=2 +1=3. Write3, carryover0.\n\n7. Millions:7 +9=16. Write6, carryover1.\n\n8. Ten millions:2 +7=9 +1=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\n9. Hundred millions:4 +6=10 +1(carry)=11. So here, we write 1 and carryover1. But since there are no more digits, the carryover1 becomes a new digit at the beginning.\n\nWait, so the total sum would be 1 (from the carry) followed by 1 (from the hundred millions place's 11?), then the other digits. Let me see:.\n\nWait the hundred millions place after adding 4+6+1 (carry from ten millions) is 11. So that is 1 in the hundred millions place and carryover1 to the next (billions) place. Since there are no digits beyond hundred millions, that carryover becomes a 1 in the billions place. So the total digits would be:.\n\nBillions place:1.\n\nHundred millions:1.\n\nTen millions:0.\n\nMillions:6.\n\nHundred thousands:3.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nThousands:9.\n\nHundreds:6.\n\nTens:0.\n\nOnes:5.\n\nWait let me arrange this properly:.\n\nStarting from the highest digit:.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:1.\n\nTen millions:0.\n\nMillions:6.\n\nHundred thousands:3.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nThousands:9.\n\nHundreds:6.\n\nTens:0.\n\nOnes:5</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
