final_output:  ["347,000,000 + 988,711 = 347,988,711.\n\nWait, but that contradicts my previous result of 335,148,711. Hmm, so where did I go wrong?\n\nAh, I see the mistake now. Let me retrace my first method.\n\nIn the first method, when adding digit by digit, I must have miscalculated one of the positions. Let me redo the digit-by-digit addition carefully.\n\nFirst number: 222,611,279.\n\nSecond number:125,377,432.\n\nAligning them:\n\n```\n  2 2 2 6 1 1 2 7 9\n+ 1 2 5 3 7 7 4 3 2\n--------------------\n```\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 9 + 2 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):7 + 3 =10 +1=11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):2 +4 =6 +1=7. Write 7, no carryover.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):1 +7 =8. Write8.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):1 +3 =4. Write4.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):6 +7 =13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait in the second number's hundred thousands place (position6 from the right?), let me check.\n\nWait the second number is 125,377,432.\n\nBreaking down its digits from the right:\n\nPosition1:2 (ones).\n\nPosition2:3 (tens).\n\nPosition3:4 (hundreds).\n\nPosition4:7 (thousands).\n\nPosition5:3 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition6:7 (hundred thousands).\n\nWait yes, because 125,377,432 is 1 (crore?) Wait in standard places:\n\nLet me write the second number as digits from right:\n\nPositions:\n\n1:2 (ones).\n\n2:3 (tens).\n\n3:4 (hundreds).\n\n4:7 (thousands).\n\n5:3 (ten thousands).\n\n6:7 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:5 (millions).\n\n8:2 (ten millions).\n\n9:1 (hundred millions).\n\nAh! So position6 (hundred thousands place) of the second number is 7, not 5. Because the second number is 1 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands?), wait let's see:\n\nWait 125,377,432 is:\n\nDigits from left to right:\n\n1 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions),5 (millions),3 (hundred thousands),7 (ten thousands),7 (thousands),4 (hundreds),3 (tens),2 (ones). Wait no:\n\nWai