e a carryover of 1 after the hundred millions, we need to add a new digit at the billions place: 1.\n\nSo putting all digits together from position9 to position1:\n\nBillions place:1\n\nHundred millions:4 (from position9)\n\nTen millions:0 (position8)\n\nMillions:8 (position7)\n\nHundred thousands:5 (position6)\n\nTen thousands:4 (position5)\n\nThousands:2 (position4)\n\nHundreds:9 (position3)\n\nTens:4 (position2)\n\nOnes:0 (position1)\n\nSo the number is:\n\n1 (billions), 4 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nWait that's 1,408,542,940?\n\nWait, let's write it out:\n\n1,408,542,940.\n\nWait, but let's check with the other method.\n\nEarlier when I broke the numbers into parts:\n\nFirst number: 823,866,285 = 823,000,000 + 866,285.\n\nSecond number:564,876,655 = 564,000,000 + 876,655.\n\nAdding the millions parts: 823,000,000 + 564,000,000 = 1,387,000,000.\n\nAdding the remaining parts: 866,285 +876,655 = ?\n\n866,285 +800,000 = 1,666,285\n\nThen add 76,655: 1,666,285 +76,655 = 1,742,940.\n\nTotal sum:1,387,000,000 +1,742,940 =1,388,742,940.\n\nWait, but according to the digit-by-digit addition now, it's 1,408,542,940. These don't match. There's a problem here.\n\nHmm, so I must have made a mistake in one of the methods. Let's recalculate the digit-by-digit:\n\nLet me go step by step again carefully.\n\nFirst number: 823,866,285.\n\nDigits (from right, positions 1-9):\n\n1:5\n\n2:8\n\n3:2\n\n4:6\n\n5:6\n\n6:8\n\n7:3\n\n8:2\n\n9:8\n\nSecond number:564,876,655.\n\nDigits:\n\n1:5\n\n2:5\n\n3:6\n\n4:6\n\n5:7\n\n6:6\n\n7:4\n\n8:8\n\n9:5\n\nNow adding each position with carryover:\n\nInitialize carryover =0.\n\nPosition1 (ones):\n\n5 +5 =10. Sum digit:0, carryover=1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):\n\n8 +5 =13 +1 (carryover)=14. Sum digit:4, carryover=1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n2 +6 =8 +1=9. Sum digit:9, carryover=0.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n6 +6 =12 +0=12. Sum digit: