on3:2 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:6 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:6 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:8 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:3 (millions)\n\nPosition8:2 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:8 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number:564,876,655.\n\nPosition1:5 (ones)\n\nPosition2:5 (tens)\n\nPosition3:6 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:6 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:7 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:6 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:4 (millions)\n\nPosition8:8 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:5 (hundred millions)\n\nWait a second, in the millions place (position7):\n\nFirst number: 3 (millions)\n\nSecond number:4 (millions)?\n\nWait, position7 for the first number is millions place?\n\nWait let me verify the digit positions again, perhaps I miscounted.\n\nLet's take the first number: 823,866,285.\n\nBreaking it down:\n\nFrom right to left (positions 1 to 9):\n\nPosition1 (ones):5\n\nPosition2 (tens):8\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2\n\nPosition4 (thousands):6\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):8\n\nPosition7 (millions):3\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):2\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nSecond number:564,876,655.\n\nPosition1:5\n\nPosition2:5\n\nPosition3:6\n\nPosition4:6\n\nPosition5:7\n\nPosition6:6\n\nPosition7:4 (millions place: the fourth digit from the right? Wait, let's count:\n\nThe number is 564,876,655.\n\nBreaking down:\n\nFrom right:\n\nPosition1:5 (ones)\n\nPosition2:5 (tens)\n\nPosition3:6 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:6 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:7 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:6 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:4 (millions)\n\nPosition8:8 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:5 (hundred millions)\n\nWait, but the number is 564,876,655. Let me write it out:\n\n564,876,655 is 5 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions),4 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands),7 (ten thousands),6 (thousands),6 (hundreds),5 (tens),5 (ones).\n\nWait, so the digits are:\n\nHundred millions:5\n\nTen millions:6\n\nMillions:4\n\nHundred thousands:8\n\nTen thousands:7\n\nThousands:6\n\nHundreds:6\n\nTens:5\n\nOnes:5.\n\nTherefore, position7 (millions place) is 4, which is correct.\n\nSo the millions digit for the second number is 4.\n\nThus in position7 (millions place):\n\nFirst number:3, second number:4 \u2192 3+4=7 plus any carryover?\n\nWait, let's retrace the digit-by-digit addition:\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\nFirst number's digit:3, second's:4.\n\nAdding:3+4 =7, plus carryover from previous step (position6).\n\nWait, let me go step by step again with positions 1 to 9:\n\nPosition1 (ones):\n\n5+5=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):\n\n8+5=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n2+6=8 +1=9 \u21929, carryover0.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n6+6=12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n6+7=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n8+6=14 +1=15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n3+4=7 +1 (carryover)=8 \u21928, carryover0.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n2+8=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n8+5=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nThen the final carryover1.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:4\n\nTen millions:0\n\nMillions:8\n\nHundred thousands:5 (from position6? Wait position6 was hundred thousands: sum digit5.\n\nWait let's list all digits from position9 to position1:\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):4\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):0\n\nPosition7 (millions):8\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):5\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):4\n\nPosition4 (thousands):2\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9\n\nPosition2 (tens):4\n\nPosition1 (ones):0\n\nSo arranging the digits:\n\nBillions place:1\n\nHundred millions:4 \u2192 1,4...\n\nTen millions:0 \u21921,40...\n\nMillions:8 \u21921,408...\n\nHundred thousands:5 \u21921,408,5...\n\nTen thousands:4 \u21921,408,54...\n\nThousands:2 \u21921,408,542...\n\nHundreds:9 \u21921,408,542,9...\n\nTens:4 \u21921,408,542,94...\n\nOnes:0 \u21921,408,542,940.\n\nWait, so according to this, it's 1,408,542,940.\n\nBut the other method gave 1,388,742,940.\n\nThere's a discrepancy in the ten millions and hundred millions places.\n\nWait, where did the 1,388... come from?\n\nIn the first method when I split the numbers:\n\nFirst number:823,866,285 = 800,000,000 + 23,866,285.\n\nWait, actually, no, I did 823 million plus 866,285. Wait 823 million is 823,000,000. Adding 866,285 gives the first number.\n\nSimilarly, the second number:564,876,655 is 564,000,000 +876,655.\n\nAdding the millions parts: 823,000,000 +564,000,000 =1,387,000,000.\n\nAdding the smaller parts:866,285 +876,655 =1,742,940.\n\nTotal sum:1,387,000,000 +1,742,940 =1,388,742,940.\n\nHmm, so why the discrepancy?\n\nAh! Wait a second, in the digit-by-digit addition, the hundred millions digit (position9) of the first number is 8, and the second number's is5, so 8+5 + carryover1 (from position8's carryover) \u21928+5=13 +1=14 \u21924 with carryover1. So the hundred millions digit is 4, and the ten millions digit (position8) was 2 (first) +8 (second) =10, so 0 with carryover1. So ten millions digit is 0, and hundred millions is4.\n\nBut in the split method, the total is 1,388,742,940. Let's see:\n\n1,388,742,940:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n1,000,000,000 +\n\n388,742,940.\n\nWait, so the hundred millions digit is 3 (since 1,388...), but according to the digit-by-digit it should be4. So which is correct?\n\nWait let's compute 823,866,285 +564,876,655.\n\nLet me write both numbers:\n\n823,866,285\n\n+564,876,655\n\n------------\n\nAdding digit by digit again carefully:\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n\u202f 823,866,285\n\n+564,876,655\n\n------------\n\nLet me add column by column from the right:\n\nStarting with the ones column:\n\nOnes:5+5=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nTens column:8+5=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:2+6=8 +1=9 \u21929, carryover0.\n\nThousands:6+6=12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:6+7=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:8+6=14 +1=15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nMillions:3+4=7 +1=8 \u21928, carryover0.\n\nTen millions:2+8=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:8+5=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nBillions: carryover1 \u21921.\n\nSo the sum is 1,408,542,940.\n\nWait, but according to the split method, it's 1,388,742,940.\n\nThere must be an error in the split method.\n\nWait let me recalculate the split method:\n\nFirst number:823,866,285 = 823,000,000 + 866,285.\n\nSecond number:564,876,655 = 564,000,000 + 876,655.\n\nAdding the millions parts:823,000,000 +564,000,000 = 1,387,000,000.\n\nAdding the smaller parts:866,285 +876,655.\n\nLet me compute 866,285 +876,655:\n\n866,285 +800,000 =1,666,285.\n\nThen add 76,655: 1,666,285 +76,655.\n\n1,666,285 +70,000 =1,736,285.\n\nThen +6,655 \u21921,736,285 +6,655 =1,742,940. So total is 1,742,940.\n\nAdding to the millions sum:1,387,000,000 +1,742,940 =1,388,742,940.\n\nWait a second, but according to digit-by-digit it's 1,408,542,940. So there's a discrepancy of 20,000,000.\n\nAh! I see the mistake now. In the split method, I incorrectly split the first number as 823 million plus 866,285. But wait, 823 million is 823,000,000. The actual first number is 823,866,285, which is 800,000,000 +23,866,285? Wait no.\n\nWait 823,866,285: the first three digits are 823, which is 800+23 million. Wait, 823 million is indeed 823,000,000. So adding the remaining 866,285 is correct.\n\nSecond number:564,876,655 is 564,000,000 +876,655.\n\nBut when adding 823,000,000 +564,000,000, that's 1,387,000,000.\n\nAdding the 1,742,940 gives 1,388,742,940.\n\nBut digit-by-digit gives 1,408,542,940. So where is the error?\n\nWait, perhaps in the digit-by-digit calculation, the ten millions place was miscalculated.\n\nLooking at the ten millions place (position8):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 2 (since the first number is 823,866,285 \u2192 the digits from left are 8 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 3 (millions)... Wait, hold on, maybe I messed up the digit positions.\n\nWait let me re-express the first number 823,866,285:\n\nBreaking it down digit by digit from left (billions) to right (ones):\n\nDigit1 (hundred millions):8\n\nDigit2 (ten millions):2\n\nDigit3 (millions):3\n\nDigit4 (hundred thousands):8\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):6\n\nDigit6 (thousands):6\n\nDigit7 (hundreds):2\n\nDigit8 (tens):8\n\nDigit9 (ones):5.\n\nWait, no, that's not correct. Let's parse the number properly:\n\nThe number is 823,866,285.\n\nBreaking it into groups:\n\n823,866,285 \u2192 823 (million) 866 (thousand) 285.\n\nSo in terms of digits:\n\nPosition (from right):\n\nPosition1:5 (ones)\n\nPosition2:8 (tens)\n\nPosition3:2 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:6 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:6 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:8 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:3 (millions)\n\nPosition8:2 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:8 (hundred millions)\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nSecond number:564,876,655 \u2192564 (million) 876 (thousand) 655.\n\nDigits from right:\n\nPosition1:5 (ones)\n\nPosition2:5 (tens)\n\nPosition3:6 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:6 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:7 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:6 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:4 (millions)\n\nPosition8:8 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:5 (hundred millions)\n\nThus, in the ten millions place (position8):\n\nFirst number has 2, second has8. So 2+8=10 \u21920 with carryover1. So ten millions digit is0, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions place (position9):8+5 +1 (carryover) \u219214 \u21924 with carryover1.\n\nThus the hundred millions digit is4, so the total up to hundred millions is 1 (carryover) followed by 4 in hundred millions place.\n\nSo the total should be 1,408,542,940.\n\nBut the split method gives 1,388,742,940.\n\nAh! Now I see the mistake in the split method.\n\nWait the first number is 823,866,285. Split into 823,000,000 and 866,285.\n\nSecond number:564,876,655 split into 564,000,000 and 876,655.\n\nAdding the millions parts:823M +564M =1,387M.\n\nAdding the smaller parts:866,285 +876,655 =1,742,940.\n\nThus total is 1,387,000,000 +1,742,940 =1,388,742,940.\n\nWait, but according to digit-by-digit it's 1,408,542,940.\n\nThe discrepancy must be because of an error in the split method.\n\nWait 823,866,285 +564,876,655:\n\nLet me compute using another method.\n\nTake 823,866,285 +564,876,655.\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\nStart with 823,866,285 +500,000,000 =1,323,866,285.\n\nThen add 64,876,655:\n\n1,323,866,285 +60,000,000 =1,383,866,285.\n\nThen add 4,876,655:\n\n1,383,866,285 +4,000,000 =1,387,866,285.\n\nThen add 876,655:\n\n1,387,866,285 +800,000 =1,388,666,285.\n\nAdd 76,655:\n\n1,388,666,285 +70,000 =1,388,736,285.\n\nAdd 6,655:\n\n1,388,736,285 +6,000 =1,388,742,285.\n\nAdd 655:\n\n1,388,742,285 +655 =1,388,742,940.\n\nThis is the same result as the split method.\n\nBut digit-by-digit gives a different answer. Therefore, the error must be in the digit-by-digit process.\n\nWait, let me recheck digit-by-digit once more:\n\nPosition7 (millions place):\n\nFirst number:3, second:4 \u21923+4=7 plus carryover from previous (position6 had carryover1). So 7+1=8, carryover0.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number's digit:2, second:8 \u21922+8=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n8+5=13 +1 (carryover)=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nThus the hundred millions digit is4, ten millions is0, so the hundred millions place is 4, ten millions is0, millions is8.\n\nTherefore, the number is 1,408,542,940.\n\nBut according to the step-by-step addition, it's 1,388,742,940.\n\nWait, there's a contradiction here. I must have messed up the digit positions.\n\nWait, let me write the numbers again with proper digit places:\n\nFirst number:823,866,285.\n\nThis is:\n\nDigit places from left to right:\n\nHundred millions:8\n\nTen millions:2\n\nMillions:3\n\nHundred thousands:8\n\nTen thousands:6\n\nThousands:6\n\nHundreds:2\n\nTens:8\n\nOnes:5.\n\nSecond number:564,876,655.\n\nHundred millions:5\n\nTen millions:6\n\nMillions:4\n\nHundred thousands:8\n\nTen thousands:7\n\nThousands:6\n\nHundreds:6\n\nTens:5\n\nOnes:5.\n\nNow, adding column b