at doesn't match. Hmm, perhaps I made a mistake in the digit breakdown.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the digits are:\n\nAfter adding each column, the digits from right to left (starting ones):\n\nOnes digit:0\n\nTens:5\n\nHundreds:8\n\nThousands:2\n\nTen thousands:4\n\nHundred thousands:7\n\nMillions:8\n\nTen millions:8\n\nHundred millions:3\n\nBillions:1\n\nSo writing them from left to right (billions down to ones):\n\n1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 0 (ones). So the number is 1,388,742,850?\n\nWait, but when I added the parts earlier, I got 1,388,742,940. There's a discrepancy here. Hmm, so which is correct?\n\nWait let's do the addition again step by step:\n\nLet me write the numbers vertically aligned:\n\n\u202f 823,866,285\n\n+564,876,655\n\n------------\n\nLet me separate each digit:\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\nFirst number: 5\n\nSecond number:5 → 5+5=10, write 0, carryover 1.\n\nTens place:\n\nFirst:8\n\nSecond:5 →8+5=13 +1 (carryover) =14 → write 4? Wait wait, no! Wait, hold on, perhaps I made a mistake here earlier.\n\nWait, hold on, I think I messed up the digits in the tens place. Let me re-express the numbers with each digit:\n\nThe first number: 8 2 3 8 6 6 2 8 5\n\nThe second number:5 6 4 8 7 6 6 5 5\n\nWait, actually, let's write each digit position properly:\n\nLet me write each number digit by digit:\n\nFirst number: 823,866,285.\n\nBreaking down:\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 1):\n\nPosition 1 (ones):5\n\nPosition 2 (tens):8\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2\n\nPosition4 (thousands):6\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):8\n\nPosition7 (millions):3\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):2\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8\n\nSecond number:564,876,655.\n\nBreaking down:\n\nPosition1:5\n\nPosition2:5\n\nPosition3:6\n\nPosition4:6\n\nPosition5:7\n\nPosition6:8\n\nPosition7:4\n\nPosition8:6\n\nPosition9:5\n\nWait, so let me re-add each position properly:\n\nPosition 1 (ones):\n\n5 +5 =10 → 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):\n\n8 (first number) +5 (second number) =13 +1 (carryover) =14 →4, carryover1.\n\nWait, here I see my mistake earlier! In the tens place, the first number's tens digit is 8, the second's is 5 (since second number's tens digit is 5). So 8+5=13 plus carryover 1 is 14. So the tens digit is 4, carryover 1. Earlier I thought it was 8+6, which was incorrect. Oh no, so that's an error.\n\nSo I messed up the digits in the tens place earlier. Let me start over carefully.\n\nLet me write both numbers with their digits aligned properly:\n\nFirst number: 8 2 3 8 6 6 2 8 5\n\nSecond number:5 6 4 8 7 6 6 5 5\n\nWait, actually, perhaps writing them digit by digit from right (ones) to left (billions):\n\nFirst number (823,866,285):\n\nPositions 1-9 (right to left):\n\n1:5 (ones)\n\n2:8 (tens)\n\n3:2 (hundreds)\n\n4:6 (thousands)\n\n5:6 (ten thousands)\n\n6:8 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:3 (millions)\n\n8:2 (ten millions)\n\n9:8 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number (564,876,655):\n\nPositions 1-9:\n\n1:5 (ones)\n\n2:5 (tens)\n\n3:6 (hundreds)\n\n4:6 (thousands)\n\n5:7 (ten thousands)\n\n6:6 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:4 (millions)\n\n8:8 (ten millions)\n\n9:5 (hundred millions)\n\nNow, adding each position:\n\nStarting at position 1:\n\n1:5+5=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):\n\n8 (from first) +5 (second) =13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n2+6=8 +1=9 →9, carryover0.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n6+6=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n6+7=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n8+6=14 +1=15 →5, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n3+4=7 +1=8 →8, carryover0.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n2+8=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n8+5=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nNow, since we have a carryover of 1 after the hundred millions, we need to add a new digit at the billions place: 1.\n\nSo putting