ousands):9.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):4.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):4.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):3.\n\nWait, so when adding, each digit position needs to be summed.\n\nLet me list each digit position from 1 to 9:.\n\nPosition 1 (units):.\n\nFirst num:0, second num:8 → 8. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition2 (tens):.\n\n9 +5=14 → write 4, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):.\n\n6 +0=6 +1=7. Carryover0.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\n3 (first) +7 (second)=10 → write0, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\n7 (first) +9 (second)=16 + carryover1 →17. So write7, carryover1.\n\nWait, no: 7+9 is 16, plus the carryover 1 from position4 makes 17? Wait no, the carryover from position4 is 1, so 7+9+1=17? Wait, no. Wait position4's carryover is 1, so that's added to position5's sum. So position5's digits are 7 +9 =16, plus 1 carryover gives 17. So write 7, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\nFirst num's hundred thousands digit is 8, second is1 →8+1=9, plus carryover1 →10. So write 0, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\nFirst num's millions digit is3, second is4 →3+4=7, plus carryover1 →8. Write8, no carryover.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\nFirst num's ten millions digit is6, second is4 →6+4=10. So write0, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\nFirst num's hundred millions digit is4, second is3 →4+3=7, plus carryover1 →8. Write8.\n\nSo putting all together from right to left:.\n\nPositions:.\n\n1:8.\n\n2:4.\n\n3:7.\n\n4:0.\n\n5:7.\n\n6:0.\n\n7:8.\n\n8:0.\n\n9:8.\n\nWait, let me list them in order from position9 (leftmost) to position1 (rightmost):.\n\nPosition9:8.\n\nPosition8:0 (since the ten millions place was 10, so we wrote 0 and carried over 1, but that carryover was added to the hundred millions place. Wait let me recheck.\n\nWait position9 (hundred millions):.\n\nFirst number's digit is4, second's is3. 4+3=7 plus the carryover from position8's addition which was 1 (since position8's sum was 6+4=10, so carryover1). So total 7+1=8. So position9 is 8, carryover0? Wait, position8's addition: 6+4=10, so they write 0 and carryover1. So when moving to position9, we have 4+3 + that carryover1, which is 8. So no further carryover beyond position9.\n\nTherefore the digits are:.\n\nPosition9:8.\n\nPosition8:0 (from ten millions place's result, since 6+4=10 gives 0 with carryover).\n\nPosition7:8 (millions place: 3+4+1=8?).\n\nWait let's retrace each step:.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\nFirst's millions digit is 3, second's is4. 3+4=7 plus any carryover? The carryover from position6 (hundred thousands) was 1. So 7+1=8. So yes, position7 is8, no carryover.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\n6 (first) +4 (second)=10. So write 0, carryover1. So that's position8's digit is0, and the carryover1 to position9.\n\nPosition9:4+3 +1 (carryover) =8.\n\nSo the digits from highest to lowest:.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):0.\n\nPosition7 (millions):8.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):0.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):7.\n\nPosition2 (tens):4.\n\nPosition1 (units):8.\n\nWait, so putting all together the digits are:.\n\nStarting from the highest position (hundred millions):.\n\n8 (hundred millions).\n\n0 (ten millions).\n\n8 (millions).\n\n0 (hundred thousands).\n\n7 (ten thousands).\n\n0 (thousands).\n\n7 (hundreds).\n\n4 (tens).\n\n8 (units)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
