 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 4 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 9 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nNow adding each column starting from the right (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 4 + 8 = 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nTens: 9 + 3 = 12, plus 1 is 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 8 + 4 = 12, plus 1 =13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\nThousands: 0 +4 =4 +1=5. Write 5, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands: 8 +8=16. Write 6, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands: 2 +8=10, plus 1=11. Write 1, carryover1.\n\nMillions: 1 +4=5 +1=6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\nTen millions: 1 +4=5. Write5, no carryover.\n\nHundred millions:1 +9=10. Write 10.\n\nPutting all together: Starting from the left, the hundred millions place gives 10, so that's 1 and 0. Wait, so actually, the hundred millions place sum is 1+9=10, which would be a 0 with a carryover of 1 to the next (billions) place. Wait, I think I messed up the order here.\n\nWait, let me go step by step again properly:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones):.\n\n1. Ones place: 4 +8 =12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\n2. Tens: 9 +3 =12 +1 =13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\n3. Hundreds:8 +4 =12 +1=13. Write3, carryover1.\n\n4. Thousands:0 +4 =4 +1=5. Write5, no carryover.\n\n5. Ten thousands:8 +8 =16. Write6, carryover1.\n\n6. Hundred thousands:2 +8 =10 +1=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\n7. Millions:1 +4 =5 +1=6. Write6, no carryover.\n\n8. Ten millions:1 +4 =5. Write5, no carryover.\n\n9. Hundred millions:1 +9 =10. Here, since this is the highest place, we can't carryover beyond, so we write 10 here. But actually, this would create a new digit in the billions place. Wait, right! Because adding two 9-digit numbers can result in a 10-digit number. So the hundred millions place adding to 10 means we have to carryover 1 to a new billions place.\n\nSo let me adjust:.\n\nWhen adding the hundred millions place (1+9=10), we write 0 in the hundred millions place and carryover 1 to the billions place. So the final digits from right to left would be:.\n\nOnes:2.\n\nTens:3.\n\nHundreds:3.\n\nThousands:5.\n\nTen thousands:6.\n\nHundred thousands:1.\n\nMillions:6.\n\nTen millions:5.\n\nHundred millions:0.\n\nPlus the carried over 1 in the billions place.\n\nSo putting it all together, the sum is 1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 2 (ones).\n\nWait, let me structure that correctly. The digits from left to right should be:.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:0.\n\nTen millions:5.\n\nMillions:6.\n\nHundred thousands:1.\n\nTen thousands:6.\n\nThousands:5.\n\nHundreds:3.\n\nTens:3.\n\nOnes:2</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 59%|#####9    | 296/500 [6:48:05<5:37:35, 99.29s/it] 
