nted the digits. Let me check the original numbers again.\n\nWait, 522,254,446 is 9 digits: 5 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nThe second number is 497,845,686, which is also 9 digits: 4 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nSo going back to the hundred-millions place: first number has 5, second has 4. Wait, no, the first number is 522,254,446, so the hundred millions digit is 5, ten millions is 2, millions is 2, etc. The second number is 497,845,686, so hundred millions is 4, ten millions 9, millions 7, etc.\n\nSo when we get to the hundred-millions place, adding 5 (from first number) and 4 (second number) plus the carried over 1 from the previous step (which was the ten-millions place addition). Wait, maybe I need to track each step carefully.\n\nLet me start over, writing down each digit's place:.\n\nLet me list each digit position by position, starting from the right (ones place):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 6 +6 =12 → 2, carry 1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):4 +8=12 +1=13 →3, carry 1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):4 +6=10 +1=11 →1, carry1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4 +5=9 +1=10 →0, carry1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):5 +4=9 +1=10 →0, carry1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):2 +8=10 +1=11 →1, carry1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):2 +7=9 +1=10 →0, carry1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):2 +9=11 +1=12 →2, carry1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):5 +4=9 +1=10 →0, carry1.\n\nWait, but after the hundred millions place (position9), there's still a carryover of 1. Since there are no more digits, we add that 1 at the beginning.\n\nSo putting it all together, the digits from right to left are:.\n\nPosition1:2.\n\nPosition2:3.\n\nPosition3:1.\n\nPosition4:0.\n\nPosition5:0.\n\nPosition6:1.\n\nPosition7:0.\n\nPosition8:2.\n\nPosition9:0.\n\nPlus the final carryover 1, so the total digits would be 1 followed by the digits above. Wait, let me list them in order.\n\nWait, when building the sum, the first digit (rightmost) is position1 (2), then position2 (3), up to position9 (0), and then the final carryover 1 as the next digit.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:.\n\n2 (ones), 3 (tens),1 (hundreds),0 (thousands),0 (ten thousands),1 (hundred thousands),0 (millions),2 (ten millions),0 (hundred millions), and then 1 (billions place).\n\nSo arranging from left to right, it would be:.\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands),1 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 2 (ones).\n\nWait, that seems a bit confusing. Let me reconstruct the number step by step.\n\nStarting from the right, the digits after each step are:.\n\nPositions (from right):.\n\n1: 2.\n\n2: 3.\n\n3:1.\n\n4:0.\n\n5:0.\n\n6:1.\n\n7:0.\n\n8:2.\n\n9:0.\n\nPlus the carryover 1 at the 10th position (billions place).\n\nSo the final number is:.\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 2 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
