messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 457228733 and 874687978?\n# Reasoning:", "<think>\nOkay, so I need to add these two big numbers: 457,228,733 and 874,687,978. Hmm, let's see. I should start by writing them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly. Let me visualize that.\n\nFirst, I'll line up the numbers by their place values:.\n\n      457,228,733.\n    + 874,687,978.\n    --------------.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. Let's break it down step by step.\n\nOnes place: 3 + 8. That's 11. Wait, I need to write down 1 and carry over 1 to the tens place. Okay.\n\nTens place: 3 (from the first number) plus 7 (from the second number) plus the carried-over 1. That's 3+7=10, plus 1 is 11 again. So another 1 in the tens place and carry over 1 again.\n\nHundreds place: 7 + 9 = 16, plus the carried 1 makes 17. So write 7, carry over 1.\n\nThousands place: 8 + 7 = 15, plus 1 is 16. Write 6, carry 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: 2 + 8 = 10, plus 1 is 11. Write 1, carry 1.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 2 + 6 = 8, plus the carried 1 makes 9. No carryover here.\n\nMillions place: 7 (from first number) plus 4 (from second number) equals 11. Wait, hold on, let me check again. Wait, the first number is 457,228,733. Let me parse the digits properly.\n\nWait, maybe I need to write out each digit's place to avoid confusion. Let me list each digit with their positions:.\n\nFirst number: 4 5 7 2 2 8 7 3 3.\n\nSecond number:8 7 4 6 8 7 9 7 8.\n\nWait, actually, let me count the digits. 457,228,733 has 9 digits: 4 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 3 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 874,687,978 is also 9 digits: 8 (hundred millions),7 (ten millions),4 (millions),6 (hundred thousands),8 (ten thousands),7 (thousands),9 (hundreds),7 (tens),8 (ones).\n\nSo aligning them:.\n\n          4 5 7 2 2 8 7 3 3.\n        + 8 7 4 6 8 7 9 7 8.\n        --------------------.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 3 +8 =11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTens: 3 +7 =10, plus 1 is 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:7 +9=16, plus 1 is 17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\nThousands:8 +7=15, plus 1 is 16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nTen-thousands:2 +8=10, plus 1=11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nHundred-thousands:2 +6=8, plus 1=9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nMillions:7 +4=11. Wait, wait, the millions place in the first number is the 7 (since first digit is hundred millions). Wait let me check:.\n\nWait, the first number's digits from left to right:.\n\nPosition: Hundred millions (4), Ten millions (5), Millions (7), Hundred thousands (2), Ten thousands (2), Thousands (8), Hundreds (7), Tens (3), Ones (3).\n\nSecond number: Hundred millions (8), Ten millions (7), Millions (4), Hundred thousands (6), Ten thousands (8), Thousands (7), Hundreds (9), Tens (7), Ones (8).\n\nSo when adding the millions place (third digit from the left):.\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 7, second's is 4. So 7+4=11. Wait, but that's the millions place? Let me confirm the positions again.\n\nWait, perhaps I should number each digit's place step by step from right to left, since addition is done right to left.\n\nAlternatively, let me write each digit's place step by step:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\n1. Ones: 3 +8 =11 → 1, carryover 1.\n\n2. Tens: 3 +7 =10 +1=11 →1, carryover1.\n\n3. Hundreds:7 +9=16 +1=17 →7, carryover1.\n\n4. Thousands:8 +7=15 +1=16 →6, carryover1.\n\n5. Ten thousands:2 +8=10 +1=11 →1, carryover1.\n\n6. Hundred thousands:2 +6=8 +1=9 →9, carryover0.\n\n7. Millions:7 (from first number) +4 (from second)=11 →11. Wait here, but since we are in the millions place, adding 7 and 4 gives 11. So we write 1 and carryover 1?.\n\nWait, but we have to consider the next higher place.\n\nContinuing:.\n\n7. Millions place digits: 7 +4 =11. Plus any carryover? Wait, the carryover from the previous step (hundred thousands place) was 0. So 11. So write 1 and carryover 1.\n\n8. Ten millions place: 5 (from first number) +7 (second number) =12, plus the carryover 1 makes 13. So write 3, carryover1.\n\n9. Hundred millions place:4 +8=12, plus carryover1=13. So write 3 and carryover1. But since there are no more digits, we add that 1 at the beginning.\n\nSo putting it all together, starting from the right:.\n\nThe digits we wrote down, from right to left (ones to hundred millions):.\n\nOnes:1, Tens:1, Hundreds:7, Thousands:6, Ten thousands:1, Hundred thousands:9, Millions:1, Ten millions:3, Hundred millions:3, plus the final carryover 1 in the billions place.\n\nWait, so the total digits would be 10 digits now. Let me list them in order:.\n\nStarting from the ones place (rightmost):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):7.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):6.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):9.\n\nPosition7 (millions):1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):3.\n\nPosition10 (billions):1 (from the carryover of the hundred millions addition).\n\nSo arranging from left (billions) to right (ones):.\n\n1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands),7 (hundreds),1 (tens),1 (ones).\n\nWait, let me reconstruct the number step by step:.\n\nThe carryover after adding hundred millions place (4+8+1=13) gives us 3 in the hundred millions place and carryover 1 to billions. So the billions digit is 1.\n\nSo the digits from left to right:.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:3 (since 4+8=12 plus 1 carried over becomes 13, so 3 here with carryover 1 to the next higher place, but since there's no higher, it's the new digit).\n\nWait, actually, when adding the hundred millions digits (4 and 8):.\n\n4 (from first number) +8 (second number) plus the carryover from the ten millions place. Wait let me retrace.\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the steps. Let me go through each digit step by step again carefully.\n\nStarting over:.\n\nDigits of first number: 4 5 7 2 2 8 7 3 3.\n\nDigits of second number:8 7 4 6 8 7 9 7 8.\n\nLet me write them aligned by place:.\n\nPositions (from right, starting at 1):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):.\n\nFirst:3, second:8 → sum 11. 1, carryover1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):.\n\n3 and7 →3+7=10 +1=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):.\n\n7 and9 →16 +1=17 →7, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\n8 and7 →15 +1=16 →6, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\n2 and8 →10 +1=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\n2 and6 →8 +1=9 →9, carryover0.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\n7 and4 →11 →11, so 1 and carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\n5 and7 →12 +1 (carryover) →13 →3 and carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\n4 and8 →12 +1 (carryover) →13 →3 and carryover1.\n\nNow, after the hundred millions place, we have a carryover of 1, which becomes the next digit (billions place).\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:.\n\nPosition1:1.\n\nPosition2:1.\n\nPosition3:7.\n\nPosition4:6.\n\nPosition5:1.\n\nPosition6:9.\n\nPosition7:1.\n\nPosition8:3.\n\nPosition9:3.\n\nPosition10:1 (the carryover).\n\nSo arranging from left (billions) to right (ones):.\n\n1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions?), wait let me list them properly.\n\nWait, the positions from right to left are positions 1-9, but after adding, we have a 10th digit. So the total number has 10 digits. Let me list each digit's place:.\n\nThe final digits:.\n\nBillions place (position10):1.\n\nHundred millions (position9):3.\n\nTen millions (position8):3.\n\nMillions (position7):1.\n\nHundred thousands (position6):9.\n\nTen thousands (position5):1.\n\nThousands (position4):6.\n\nHundreds (position3):7.\n\nTens (position2):1.\n\nOnes (position1):1.\n\nSo writing them in order from billions down to ones:.\n\n1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 1 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
