plus the 1 makes 13. So we put 3 in the hundred millions place and carry over 1 to the billions place.\n\nFinally, the billions place: The first number has 5 and the second has 7. Wait, actually, hold on. Let me check the original numbers again. Wait, 591,931,376 is 5 (hundred millions?), no, wait. Wait, let's count the digits properly.\n\nWait, 591,931,376: Let's break down the digits. Starting from the right:.\n\n6 (units), 7 (tens), 3 (hundreds), 1 (thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 9 (hundred thousands), 1 (millions), 9 (ten millions), 5 (hundred millions), and the next is the billions. Wait, actually, 591,931,376 is 5 (hundred millions place?), no. Wait, commas are placed every three digits. Let's parse it properly:.\n\n591,931,376: The first comma separates the millions, so 591 million, so the first digit is 5 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 1 (millions). Then 931 thousand, so 9 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands). Then 376: 3 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 6 (units).\n\nSo the digits from left to right are:.\n\nBillions place: 5 (since 591 million is less than a billion? Wait, 591 million is 0.591 billion. So the first digit '5' is in the hundred millions place. So the number is 5 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 1 (millions), etc. So the highest place here is hundred millions. The second number is 744,089,601.\n\nLet me parse that similarly: 744,089,601 is 7 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 4 (millions), then 089 thousand, so 0 (hundred thousands?), 8 (ten thousands?), 9 (thousands), then 601: 6 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 1 (units).\n\nWait, perhaps I should write both numbers digit by digit to ensure alignment:.\n\nFirst number: 5 9 1 9 3 1 3 7 6.\n\nWait, maybe writing each digit:.\n\nLet me write both numbers with commas as thousand separators to make it clearer:.\n\nFirst number: 591,931,376 → 5 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 744,089,601 → 7 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 1 (ones).\n\nWait, so when adding, let's align each digit:.\n\nLet's write them vertically:.\n\n          5 9 1 9 3 1 3 7 6.\n        + 7 4 4 0 8 9 6 0 1.\n        ---------------------.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 6 + 1 = 7. Write 7.\n\nTens: 7 + 0 =7. Write 7.\n\nHundreds: 3 + 6 =9. Write 9.\n\nThousands: 1 (from first number) + 9 (second) = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands: 3 (first) + 8 (second) = 11, plus 1 carried over =12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands: 9 (first) + 0 (second) =9 + 1 carried over =10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nMillions: 1 (first) +4 (second) =5 + 1 carried over =6. Write 6, no carryover?.\n\nWait, wait, let me check again step by step:.\n\nWait, let's go step by step carefully:.\n\nLet me write each digit position:.\n\nStarting from the right (units digit):.\n\nPosition 1 (units): 6 + 1 =7. No carryover.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 7 +0 =7. No carryover.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):3 +6=9. No carryover.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):1 +9=10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3 +8=11 +1 (carryover) =12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):9 +0=9 +1 (carryover) =10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):1 (from first number's millions place) +4 (second number's millions) =5 +1 (carryover)=6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\nWait, the millions place: first number's millions digit is 1 (from 591,931,376: the millions digit is the fourth comma? Wait, maybe I got confused in the digit positions.\n\nWait, let's re-express both numbers digit by digit from right to left (units to higher places) to ensure alignment.\n\nFirst number: 591,931,376.\n\nBreaking down each digit from right (units) to left (billions):.\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 1):.\n\n1: 6 (units).\n2:7 (tens).\n3:3 (hundreds).\n4:1 (thousands).\n5:3 (ten thousands).\n6:9 (hundred thousands).\n7:1 (millions).\n8:9 (ten millions).\n9:5 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number:744,089,601.\n\nDigits from right:.\n\n1:1 (units).\n2:0 (tens).\n3:6 (hundreds).\n4:9 (thousands).\n5:8 (ten thousands).\n6:0 (hundred thousands).\n7:4 (millions).\n8:4 (ten millions).\n9:7 (hundred millions).\n\nSo when adding digit by digit:.\n\nStarting at digit 1 (units):.\n\n6 +1 =7. No carryover.\n\nDigit2 (tens):7 +0=7. No.\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):3 +6=9. No.\n\nDigit4 (thousands):1 +9=10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):3 +8=11 +1 (carryover) =12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):9 +0=9 +1 (carryover)=10. Write 0, carryover1.\n\nDigit7 (millions):1 +4=5 +1 (carryover)=6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\nDigit8 (ten millions):9 +4=13. Write 3? Wait, 9 (from first) +4 (second) =13. Wait, but we have to add the digits here, and check if there's a carryover from previous. Wait, previous digit (millions) didn't have a carryover. Wait, let's go step by step:.\n\nWait, digit7 (millions place) had 1 +4 +1 (carryover from digit6) =6, no carryover. So moving to digit8 (ten millions):.\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 9, second's is4. So 9 +4 =13. So write 3 in the ten millions place, carryover 1.\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):5 +7=12, plus the carryover1=13. So write 3 in the hundred millions place, and carryover 1. But since there are no more digits, that carryover becomes a new digit at the billions place.\n\nSo putting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nDigits 1-9:.\n\nPositions:.\n\n1:7.\n\n2:7.\n\n3:9.\n\n4:0 (with carryover 1).\n\n5:2 (with carryover1).\n\n6:0 (carryover1).\n\n7:6.\n\n8:3 (from 9+4=13, so 3 with carryover1).\n\n9:3 (from 5+7+1=13, so 3 with carryover1).\n\nThen the carryover1 becomes a new digit at position10 (billions place): 1.\n\nSo the total sum is:.\n\n1 (billions) 3 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands?), wait let me see: