\n\n0 (millions),\n\n5 (ten millions),\n\nplus the final carryover1.\n\nSo the total is 1 (hundred millions place) followed by 5 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 4 (ones).\n\nThus the number is 150,138,124.\n\nAh, right! Because the ten millions place after adding gives 5, and with the carryover1, we have a new digit at the hundred millions place (1). So the total is 150,138,124.\n\nSo the initial digit-wise addition for the smaller numbers was wrong because I incorrectly extended it to 9 digits instead of 8+8=9 digits with the carryover. So the correct total is 150,138,124. Therefore, the total of the original numbers is 1,500,000,000 +150,138,124 =1,650,138,124.\n\nBut in the original digit-wise addition of the full problem (874,473,157 +775,664,967), I had gotten 1,650,318,124. The discrepancy is in the hundred thousands and ten thousands places.\n\nLet me retrace the full digit-wise addition:\n\nOriginal problem:\n\n874,473,157\n\n+775,664,967\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:7+7=14 →4, carry1.\n\nTens:5+6=11+1=12 →2, carry1.\n\nHundreds:1+9+1=11 →1, carry1.\n\nThousands:3+4=7+1=8 →8, carry0.\n\nTen thousands:7+6=13 →3, carry1.\n\nHundred thousands:4 (first) +6 (second)=10 +1=11 →1, carry1.\n\nMillions:4 (first\'s millions digit) +5 (second\'s millions digit)=9 +1=10 →0, carry1.\n\nTen millions:7 (first) +7 (second)=14 +1=15 →5, carry1.\n\nHundred millions:8 (first) +7 (second)=15 +1=16 →6, carry1.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nThus the digits are:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:6,\n\nTen millions:5,\n\nMillions:0,\n\nHundred thousands:1,\n\nTen thousands:3,\n\nThousands:8,\n\nHundreds:1,\n\nTens:2,\n\nOnes:4.\n\nSo the number is 1,650,318,124.\n\nWait, but according to the other method it\'s 1,650,138,124. The difference is in the hundred thousands and ten thousands places.\n\nLooking at the ten thousands place in the full problem:\n\nTen thousands digit of first number is7 (from 874,473,1