*7 (first's hundred millions) +4 (second's hundred millions)=11. Write11 here, so that's 1 in the hundred-millions and 1 in the billions place? Wait, yes. Since the sum of 7+4 is 11, so we have to write 1 in the hundred millions and carryover 1 to the billions place. But since there are no higher digits beyond hundred millions, the total would be 1 (billion) and 1 in the hundred millions. Wait, actually, the digits:.\n\nWait, the first number is 740,562,855 which is 7 hundred million, so the places are:.\n\n7 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 0 (millions), etc. The second number is 443,867,185: 4 hundred millions. Adding the hundred millions:7+4=11, so that's 11 hundred millions, which is 1,100,000,000. So the total would be 1,1 (hundred millions place becomes 1 and carryover to the next higher place, which is billions. So the final digits would have a 1 in the billions place, then 1 in the hundred millions, etc.\n\nTherefore, putting all together:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nDigits are:.\n\nOnes:0.\n\nTens:4.\n\nHundreds:0.\n\nThousands:0.\n\nTen-thousands:3.\n\nHundred-thousands:4.\n\nMillions:4.\n\nTen-millions:8.\n\nHundred-millions:1 (since 7+4=11, so the hundred millions digit is 1, and the carryover 1 to billions).\n\nThen, the billions place: 1</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
