s 4, not 6. The 6 is in the ten millions place.\n\nSo that's where I messed up earlier. So the millions place addition is 4+7=11, so 1 with a carryover of 1.\n\nContinuing:.\n\nPosition7 (millions): 4 +7=11 →1, carry1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions): first number's ten millions digit is6, second's is8. So 6+8=14, plus the carryover 1 →15. So write 5 and carry1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions): first is3, second is4 →3+4=7, plus the carryover1 →8.\n\nSo putting all the digits together from right to left (positions 1 to 9):.\n\nPosition1:5.\n\nPosition2:2.\n\nPosition3:6.\n\nPosition4:4.\n\nPosition5:9.\n\nPosition6:5.\n\nPosition7:1.\n\nPosition8:5 (from ten millions place 15 →5 with carryover).\n\nWait, let me list all digits in order from the highest place to the lowest:.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):5 (since 6+8+1=15, so 5 here with carryover 1 to the hundred millions, but the hundred millions already accounted for that carryover).\n\nWait, let me list all digits step by step:.\n\nStarting from the highest digit (position9, hundred millions):.\n\nPosition9: 3 (first) +4 (second) + any carry from position8. Wait, the carryover from position8 is from the ten millions place.\n\nWait, let's go step by step again, building the digits from right to left:.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to write all the digits in order from the highest to the lowest:.\n\nThe positions:.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions): 3 +4 + carryover from ten millions?.\n\nWait, actually, the hundred millions place is the first digit of each number. So when adding the hundred millions digits (position9), they are 3 and4. But before that, when adding the ten millions place (position8), which is the second digit from the left, we had 6+8 plus any carryover from millions place.\n\nWait, let me start over, building the sum step by step from right to left with carries:.\n\nLet me list each digit pair with their positions:.\n\nStarting from the right (position1):.\n\nPosition1 (o