ically:.\n\n          7 7 0 8 7 6 1 3 1.\n        + 2 9 8 2 2 3 6 7 3.\n        --------------------.\n\nBut to add them correctly, starting from the rightmost digit (the ones place). Let me start adding each column step by step, keeping track of any carry-over.\n\nStarting with the ones place:.\n\nFirst number's ones digit: 1.\n\nSecond number's ones digit: 3.\n\nAdding them: 1 + 3 = 4. No carry-over here. So the ones digit of the sum is 4.\n\nNext, the tens place:.\n\nFirst number's tens digit: 3.\n\nSecond number's tens digit:7.\n\n3 +7 =10. So we write down 0 and carry over 1 to the next column.\n\nHundreds place:.\n\nFirst number's hundreds digit:1.\n\nSecond number's hundreds digit:6.\n\nPlus the carry-over 1: 1 +6 +1 =8. So hundreds digit is 8, no carry-over.\n\nThousands place:.\n\nFirst number's thousands digit:6.\n\nSecond number's thousands digit:3.\n\n6 +3 =9. So thousands digit is 9, no carry-over.\n\nTen thousands place:.\n\nFirst number's ten thousands digit:7.\n\nSecond number's ten thousands digit:2.\n\n7 +2 =9. Ten thousands digit is 9, no carry-over.\n\nHundred thousands place:.\n\nFirst number:8.\n\nSecond number:2.\n\n8 +2 =10. So here we write 0 and carry over 1.\n\nMillions place:.\n\nFirst number:0.\n\nSecond number:8.\n\nPlus carry-over 1: 0 +8 +1 =9. So millions digit is 9, no carry-over.\n\nTen millions place:.\n\nFirst number:7.\n\nSecond number:9.\n\n7 +9 =16. So write down 6 and carry over 1.\n\nHundred millions place:.\n\nFirst number:7.\n\nSecond number:2.\n\nPlus carry-over 1: 7+2+1=10. So here we have 0 in the hundred millions place and carry over 1 to the next (billions) place.\n\nSince there are no more digits, the carried-over 1 becomes the billions place.\n\nPutting all together, the sum is:.\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 4 (ones). Wait, let me check again step by step.\n\nWait, maybe I need to track each position again properly. Let me go through each digit step by step again carefully.\n\nLet me write the numbers with their place values:.\n\nFirst number (770,876,131):.\n\n- Billions place: 0 (since it's a 9-digit number, so billions would be the 10th digit, which isn't present).\n- Hundred millions:7.\n- Ten millions:7.\n- Millions:0.\n- Hundred thousands:8.\n- Ten thousands:7.\n- Thousands:6.\n- Hundreds:1.\n- Tens:3.\n- Ones:1.\n\nSecond number (298,223,673):.\n\n- Billions place:0.\n- Hundred millions:2.\n- Ten millions:9.\n- Millions:8.\n- Hundred thousands:2.\n- Ten thousands:2.\n- Thousands:3.\n- Hundreds:6.\n- Tens:7.\n- Ones:3.\n\nAdding each column from right to left (starting from ones):.\n\nOnes column:.\n\n1 +3 =4. Write 4. Carryover 0.\n\nTens column:.\n\n3 +7 =10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds column:.\n\n1 +6 +1 (carry) =8. Write 8, carryover 0.\n\nThousands column:.\n\n6 +3 =9. Write9, carryover0.\n\nTen thousands column:.\n\n7 +2 =9. Write9, carryover0.\n\nHundred thousands column:.\n\n8 +2 =10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nMillions column:.\n\n0 +8 +1 (carry) =9. Write9, carryover0.\n\nTen millions column:.\n\n7 +9 =16. Write6, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions column:.\n\n7 +2 +1 (carry) =10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nBillions column:.\n\n0 +0 +1 (carry) =1. Write1.\n\nSo the digits from right to left (ones to billions) are:.\n\nOnes:4.\n\nTens:0.\n\nHundreds:8.\n\nThousands:9.\n\nTen thousands:9.\n\nHundred thousands:0.\n\nMillions:9.\n\nTen millions:6.\n\nHundred millions:0.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nPutting it all together from highest to lowest place:.\n\nBillions (1), hundred millions (0), ten millions (6), millions (9), hundred thousands (0), ten thousands (9), thousands (9), hundreds (8), tens (0), ones (4)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
