 see:.\n\n252,128,435 is 9 digits: 2 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\n685,718,605 is also 9 digits: 6 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nSo when adding:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 5 +5=10. 0, carryover 1.\n\nTens: 3 +0 +1=4. Write 4, no carryover.\n\nHundreds:4 +6=10, so 0 carryover1.\n\nThousands:8+8=16 +0? Wait, the thousands place in the first number is 8, second number's thousands is 8. So 8+8=16, plus the carryover 1? Wait, no. Wait, the hundreds place had a carryover from the hundreds. Wait, let me go step by step again carefully.\n\nLet me write them aligned:.\n\n          2 5 2 1 2 8 4 3 5.\n        + 6 8 5 7 1 8 6 0 5.\n        ---------------------.\nStarting from the right (ones):.\n\n5 +5 =10 → 0, carryover 1.\n\nTens place: 3 +0 =3, plus 1 →4. So 4, no carryover.\n\nHundreds place:4 +6 =10 →0, carryover1.\n\nThousands place:8 (from first number's thousands) +8 (second's thousands) =16, plus the 1 →17. So write 7, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands place:2 (first's ten thousands) +1 (second's ten thousands) =3, plus 1 →4. Write 4, no carryover.\n\nHundred thousands place:1 (first's hundred thousands) +7 (second's hundred thousands) =8. Write 8.\n\nMillions place:2 (first's millions) +5 (second's millions) =7. Wait, wait, first number's millions digit is 2? Wait, let me check:.\n\nWait, the first number is 252,128,435. Breaking down:.\n\nDigits:.\n\nPosition (from right): 1 (5), 2 (3), 3 (4), 4 (8), 5 (2), 6 (1), 7 (2), 8 (5), 9 (2).\n\nWait, maybe I got confused in the place names. Let me write each digit with their positions:.\n\nFirst number: 2 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 6 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions),5 (millions),7 (hundred thousands),1 (ten thousands),8 (thousands),6 (hundreds),0 (tens),5 (ones).\n\nSo, moving to the millions place (which is the 7th digit from the right):.\n\nFirst number's millions place is 2 (the third digit from the left: 2 in 252..), and the second number's millions is 5 (third digit from left in 685..). So 2+5=7. So write 7 here, no carryover.\n\nTen millions place: first number has 5 (second digit from left), second has 8 (second digit from left). 5+8=13. So write 3 and carryover 1.\n\nHundred millions place: first number's first digit is 2, second's is 6. 2+6=8 plus the carried over 1 makes 9.\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes:0.\n\nTens:4.\n\nHundreds:0.\n\nThousands:7.\n\nTen thousands:4.\n\nHundred thousands:8.\n\nMillions:7.\n\nTen millions:3 (with carryover 1 to next).\n\nHundred millions:9</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
