messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 314229068 and 679409818?\n# Reasoning:", "<think>\nOkay, so I need to add these two numbers: 314,229,068 and 679,409,818. Let me see. Hmm, I should probably write them down one on top of the other to align the digits properly. Let me visualize that.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. The first number ends with 8, and the second ends with 8 as well. 8 plus 8 is 16. So I write down 6 in the ones place and carry over 1 to the tens place.\n\nNext, the tens place. The first number has a 6 here, and the second has a 1. Adding those gives 6 + 1 = 7, plus the carried over 1 makes 8. So the tens place is 8, no carryover.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place. The first number has 0 and the second has 8. 0 + 8 is 8. No carryover here either.\n\nThousands place: first number has 9, second has 9. 9 + 9 is 18. So write 8, carry over 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: first number is 2, second is 0. 2 + 0 = 2, plus the carryover 1 makes 3. So 3 here, no carryover.\n\nHundred-thousands place: first number has 2, second has 4. 2 + 4 = 6. That's straightforward.\n\nMillions place: first number is 4, second is 9. 4 + 9 = 13. So write 3 and carry over 1.\n\nTen-millions place: first number is 1, second is 7. 1 + 7 = 8, plus the carryover 1 makes 9.\n\nHundred-millions place: first number is 3, second is 6. 3 + 6 = 9.\n\nWait, let me check again step by step to make sure I didn't skip any digits or misalign anything. Let me recount each digit position:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\n1. Ones: 8 + 8 = 16 → 6, carry 1.\n2. Tens: 6 + 1 = 7 + 1 (carry) = 8 → 8.\n3. Hundreds: 0 + 8 = 8 → 8.\n4. Thousands: 9 + 9 = 18 → 8, carry 1.\n5. Ten-thousands: 2 + 0 = 2 +1 =3 →3.\n6. Hundred thousands: 2 +4 =6 →6.\n7. Millions:4 +9=13 →3, carry1.\n8. Ten millions:1 +7=8 +1=9 →9.\n9. Hundred millions:3 +6=9 →9.\n\nWait, but the first number is 314,229,068. Let me check the digits again:.\n\nBreaking down 314,229,068:.\n\nDigits from right (ones place):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 8.\n\n2 (tens):6.\n\n3 (hundreds):0.\n\n4 (thousands):9.\n\n5 (ten thousands):2.\n\n6 (hundred thousands):2.\n\n7 (millions):4.\n\n8 (ten millions):1.\n\n9 (hundred millions):3.\n\nSecond number 679,409,818:.\n\nPositions:.\n\n1:8.\n\n2:1.\n\n3:8.\n\n4:9.\n\n5:0.\n\n6:4.\n\n7:7.\n\n8:9.\n\n9:6.\n\nWait, so when adding the hundred thousands place (position 6), the first number has 2 and the second has 4? Let me confirm:.\n\nFirst number: 3 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 8 (ones). Wait a second, maybe I mixed up the places.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to write the numbers out properly to avoid confusion.\n\nLet me write both numbers with commas:.\n\nFirst number: 314,229,068. Breaking down:.\n\n3 1 4 2 2 9 0 6 8.\n\nBut commas are placed every three digits. So 314,229,068 is 314,229,068. So the digits from left to right are:.\n\nHundred millions: 3.\n\nTen millions:1.\n\nMillions:4.\n\nHundred thousands:2.\n\nTen thousands:2.\n\nThousands:9.\n\nHundreds:0.\n\nTens:6.\n\nOnes:8.\n\nSecond number: 679,409,818.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\n6 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nSo when adding:.\n\nLet me align them properly:.\n\n          3 1 4 2 2 9 0 6 8.\n\n        + 6 7 9 4 0 9 8 1 8.\n\n        ---------------------.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 8 +8 =16 →6 carryover 1.\n\nTens:6 +1 =7 plus carryover 1 →8.\n\nHundreds:0 +8 =8.\n\nThousands:9 +9=18 →8 carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:2 +0=2 +1 →3.\n\nHundred thousands:2 +4=6.\n\nMillions:4 +9=13 →3 carryover1.\n\nTen millions:1 +7=8 +1 →9.\n\nHundred millions:3 +6=9</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
