25 which is 3 hundred millions. Wait, when we get to the hundred millions place, the digits are 9 (from first number) and 3 (from second). Adding them with the carried-over 1 gives 13. So write 3 and carry over 1. But since there are no more digits left, that carried-over 1 becomes another digit. .\n\nWait, let me check the alignment again. Maybe I miscounted the places. Let's see:.\n\nBreaking down each number digit by digit:.\n\nFirst number: 9 9 6 9 3 1 2 8 7.\n\nSecond number: 3 7 9 5 9 8 9 2 5.\n\nWait, actually, both numbers have 9 digits. Let me align them properly:.\n\n       9 9 6 9 3 1 2 8 7.\n\n     + 3 7 9 5 9 8 9 2 5.\n\n     -----------------------.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 7+5=12 → 2, carry 1.\n\nTens: 8+2=10 +1=11 → 1, carry 1.\n\nHundreds: 2+9=11 +1=12 → 2, carry1.\n\nThousands: 1+8=9 +1=10 →0, carry1.\n\nTen-thousands: 3+9=12 +1=13 →3, carry1.\n\nHundred-thousands:9+5=14 +1=15 →5, carry1.\n\nMillions:6+9=15 +1=16 →6, carry1.\n\nTen-millions:9+7=16 +1=17 →7, carry1.\n\nHundred-millions:9+3=12 +1=13 →3, carry1.\n\nBillions place: the carryover 1. So the final digit is 1.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right, the digits would be:.\n\n1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 2 (ones). Wait, no, maybe I messed up the order. Let me reconstruct step by step.\n\nWait the result digits from each step:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 2.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):0.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):5.\n\nPosition7 (millions):6.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):7.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):3.\n\nPosition10 (billions):1 (from the final carryover)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
