=1.\nPosition 2 (tens): 2+6=8.\nPosition3 (hundreds):5+2=7.\nPosition4 (thousands):5+6=11. Wait, hold on. Wait, maybe I miscounted the digits. Let me check the numbers again.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to write each number's digits in order. Let's list each digit from right to left (units to higher places):.\n\nFirst number: 737315520.\n\nLet's split into digits:.\n\nStarting from the right (units digit):.\n\nDigit 1 (units): 0.\n\nDigit 2 (tens): 2.\n\nDigit 3 (hundreds):5.\n\nDigit4 (thousands):1.\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):5.\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):3.\n\nDigit7 (millions):7.\n\nDigit8 (ten millions):3.\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):7.\n\nWait, maybe I should count the digits properly. Let's see:.\n\nThe number is 737,315,520. Let's count the digits:.\n\n7 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 0 (units). Wait, that's 9 digits. So positions from right to left:.\n\nPosition 1: 0 (units).\nPosition 2: 2 (tens).\nPosition 3:5 (hundreds).\nPosition4:5 (thousands).\nPosition5:1 (ten thousands).\nPosition6:3 (hundred thousands).\nPosition7:7 (millions).\nPosition8:3 (ten millions).\nPosition9:7 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number:234,356,261.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nDigits from right to left:.\n\nPosition1:1 (units).\nPosition2:6 (tens).\nPosition3:2 (hundreds).\nPosition4:6 (thousands).\nPosition5:5 (ten thousands).\nPosition6:3 (hundred thousands).\nPosition7:4 (millions).\nPosition8:3 (ten millions).\nPosition9:2 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, but the second number is 234,356,261, which is also 9 digits:.\n\n2 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 1 (units). Wait, no. Let's see:.\n\nWait 234,356,261:.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nStarting from left: 2 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 1 (units). Yes, so when written right to left (units first):.\n\nPositions:.\n\n1:1 (units).\n\n2:6 (tens).\n\n3:2 (hundreds).\n\n4:6 (thousands).\n\n5:5 (ten thousands).\n\n6:3 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:4 (millions).\n\n8:3 (ten millions).\n\n9:2 (hundred millions).\n\nSo now, adding digit by digit from position1 to position9, carrying over as needed.\n\nLet me create a table for each position:.\n\nPosition 1 (units):.\n\n0 (first number) + 1 (second number) = 1. No carryover. So digit1 is 1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):.\n\n2 +6 =8. No carryover. Digit2 is8.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):.\n\n5 +2 =7. Digit3 is7.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\n5 (first) +6 (second) =11. So we write down 1 and carryover 1 to the next position.\n\nSo digit4 is 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\nFirst number's digit5 is1, second's is5. Adding those plus the carryover: 1+5 +1 (carryover)=7. So digit5 is7, no carryover.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\nFirst number has 3, second has3. 3+3=6. Plus no carryover. So digit6 is6.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\nFirst number has7, second has4. 7+4=11. So write 1, carryover 1.\n\nDigit7 is1, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\nFirst number's digit8 is3, second's digit8 is3. 3+3=6, plus the carryover 1 makes 7. So digit8 is7, no carryover.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\nFirst has7, second has2. 7+2=9. So digit9 is9.\n\nPutting all together, the digits from position1 to position9 are:.\n\nPosition1:1.\n\nPosition2:8.\n\nPosition3:7.\n\nPosition4:1.\n\nPosition5:7.\n\nPosition6:6.\n\nPosition7:1.\n\nPosition8:7.\n\nPosition9:9.\n\nSo writing the number from highest to lowest (position9 to 1):.\n\n9 (hundred millions),7 (ten millions),1 (millions),6 (hundred thousands),7 (ten thousands),1 (thousands),7 (hundreds),8 (tens),1 (units).\n\nWait, let me arrange the digits properly. The digits are:.\n\nPosition9:9 → hundred millions place.\n\nPosition8:7 → ten millions.\n\nPosition7:1 → millions.\n\nPosition6:6 → hundred thousands.\n\nPositio