sands place) +1 (from second's thousands place) =8, plus carryover 1 \u21929. So digit 9, carryover 0.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):8 (first) +9 (second) =17. Since no carryover, that's 17. Wait, but we need to add the previous carryover? Wait no, the carryover from the thousands place was 0. So 8+9=17. So write 7, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):9 (first) +3 (second)=12 +1 (carryover)=13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):8 (first) +8 (second)=16 +1=17. Write7, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):5 (first) +0 (second)=5 +1=6. Carryover0.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):4 +2=6.\n\nPutting all digits together from right to left:\n\nStarting from the first digit (hundred millions):6, then ten millions 6, millions 7, hundred thousands3, ten thousands7, thousands9, hundreds4, tens4, ones7.\n\nWait let me list them in order:\n\nPositions (from highest to lowest):\n\n10^8:6\n\n10^7:6 (from ten millions place:5+0+1 carried over?)\n\nWait let me retrace:\n\nWait, positions:\n\nPosition9 (10^8): 4 +2 =6, no carryover.\n\nPosition8 (10^7):5 +0 =5, plus any carryover from position7?\n\nWait let's list each step properly:\n\nAfter position4 (thousands place), we had a carryover 0.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\nFirst number's ten thousands digit:8\n\nSecond's ten thousands digit:9\n\nTotal:8+9=17. So digit7, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\nFirst:9, second:3 \u21929+3=12 + carryover1=13 \u21923 with carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\nFirst:8, second:8 (since second number's millions digit is 8 in the 10^6 place?), wait let me check again.\n\nWait second number's digits:\n\nSecond number: 2 (10^8),0 (10^7),8 (10^6),3 (10^5),9 (10^4),1 (10^3),6 (10^2),8 (10^1),0 (10^0).\n\nSo millions place (10^6) is 8 for second number.\n\nFirst number's millions place (10^6) is 8 (since first number is 4 5 8 9 8 7 7 6 7).\n\nSo 8 (first) +8 (second) =16, plus carryover1 from previous (position6's carryover1) \u219217. So digit7, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions place):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is5, second's is0. 5+0 + carryover1=6. So digit6, carryover0.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n4 +2=6, no carryover.\n\nSo compiling all digits from highest to lowest (left to right):\n\nHundred millions:6,\n\nTen millions:6,\n\nMillions:7,\n\nHundred thousands:3,\n\nTen thousands:7,\n\nThousands:9,\n\nHundreds:4,\n\nTens:4,\n\nOnes:7.\n\nSo the number is 6 6 7 3 7 9 4 4 7 \u2192 667,379,447.\n\nWait, but let me check with another method to confirm.\n\nAlternatively, adding the numbers:\n\n458,987,767\n\n+208,391,680\n\n= ?\n\nLet's add them:\n\n458,987,767\n\n+208,391,680\n\nStart adding:\n\nUnits:7+0=7\n\nTens:6+8=14 (4, carry 1)\n\nHundreds:7+6=13 +1=14 (4, carry1)\n\nThousands:7+1=8 +1=9 (9, no carry)\n\nTen thousands:8+9=17 (7, carry1)\n\nHundred thousands:9+3=12 +1=13 (3, carry1)\n\nMillions:8+8=16 +1=17 (7, carry1)\n\nTen millions:5+0=5 +1=6 (6, no carry)\n\nHundred millions:4+2=6.\n\nSo yes, same result:667,379,447.\n\nHmm, so the final answer should be 667,379,447. I think that's correct. Let me just do a quick check with approximate values:\n\n458 million plus 208 million is about 666 million. The exact numbers are a bit more, so 667 million makes sense. The rest of the digits add up accordingly. Okay, confident now.\n</think>\n\n",
