mbers have up to 9 digits, adding them might result in a 10-digit number. So the total positions would be 10 now. So after Position9 (hundred millions):.\n\nPosition9:9 +2 =11, so write1 and carryover1. Then we have to add the carryover to the next position (billions place), which is Position10 (the 10th digit from the right). Since both original numbers have 9 digits, their billions place is 0. So adding 0+0+1 (carryover) =1.\n\nSo the final digits from Position1 to Position10:.\n\nPosition10:1  .\nPosition9:1  .\nPosition8:9  .\nPosition7:4  .\nPosition6:0  .\nPosition5:4  .\nPosition4:0  .\nPosition3:0  .\nPosition2:2  .\nPosition1:9  .\n\nWait, let's list them all properly in order:.\n\nStarting from Position1 (ones) to Position10 (billions):.\n\nPosition1:9  .\nPosition2:2  .\nPosition3:0  .\nPosition4:0  .\nPosition5:4  .\nPosition6:0  .\nPosition7:4  .\nPosition8:9  .\nPosition9:1  .\nPosition10:1  .\n\nWait, but when we write the number, it's from the highest position to the lowest. So the digits would be:.\n\nBillions place:1 (Position10)  .\nHundred millions:1 (Position9)  .\nTen millions:9 (Position8)  .\nMillions:4 (Position7)  .\nHundred thousands:0 (Position6)  .\nTen thousands:4 (Position5)  .\nThousands:0 (Position4)  .\nHundreds:0 (Position3)  .\nTens:2 (Position2)  .\nOnes:9 (Position1)  .\n\nSo putting it all together: 1 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 9 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
