 4 6 0 0 0.\nSecond number:8 9 8 6 9 4 3 9 4.\n\nWait, actually, maybe it's better to count each digit from right to left as positions 1 to 9.\n\nLet me list each digit:.\n\nFirst number (419,846,000):.\n\nStarting from the right (position 1 to 9):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 0.\nPosition 2 (tens): 0.\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 0.\nPosition 4 (thousands): 6.\nPosition 5 (ten thousands): 4.\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands): 8.\nPosition 7 (millions): 9.\nPosition 8 (ten millions): 1.\nPosition 9 (hundred millions): 4.\n\nWait, actually, maybe that's a confusion. Let me think again. Let's write the number 419,846,000 as digits:.\n\nDigits from left to right: 4 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 0 (ones). Wait, that might be the breakdown.\n\nWait, 419,846,000: breaking down:.\n\n- The first three digits (419) are in the millions: 419,000,000.\n- Then the next three (846) are thousands: 846,000.\n- Then the last three (000). So the full breakdown is:.\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 1):.\n\nPosition 1: 0 (ones).\nPosition 2: 0 (tens).\nPosition 3: 0 (hundreds).\nPosition 4: 6 (thousands).\nPosition 5: 4 (ten thousands).\nPosition 6: 8 (hundred thousands).\nPosition 7: 9 (millions).\nPosition 8: 1 (ten millions).\nPosition 9: 4 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number: 898,694,394.\n\nBreaking it down similarly:.\n\n898,694,394:.\n\n- 898 million, 694 thousand, 394.\n\nDigits from left to right: 8 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 4 (ones).\n\nSo digit positions (from right, 1 to 9):.\n\nPosition 1: 4 (ones).\nPosition 2: 9 (tens).\nPosition 3: 3 (hundreds).\nPosition 4: 4 (thousands).\nPosition 5: 9 (ten thousands).\nPosition 6: 6 (hundred thousands).\nPosition 7: 8 (millions).\nPosition 8: 9 (ten millions).\nPosition 9: 8 (hundred millions).\n\nNow, adding each position:.\n\nStarting from position 1 (ones):.\n\nFirst number: 0, second number:4 → 0+4=4. Write down 4. No carryover.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):.\n\n0 +9=9. Write 9. No carryover.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):.\n\n0 +3=3. Write 3. Still no carryover.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):.\n\n6 (from first number) +4 (second number) =10. Wait, 6 +4 is 10. So here we get 0 and carryover 1 to the next higher place.\n\nWait, but the thousands place is position 4. So the sum here is 6 +4 =10. So we write 0 and carryover 1 to the ten thousands place (position 5).\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):.\n\nFirst number has 4, second has 9. So 4+9=13, plus the carryover 1 makes 14. So here, we write 4 and carryover 1 again.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):.\n\nFirst number has 8, second has6. 8+6=14, plus carryover 1 is 15. So write 5 and carryover 1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\nFirst number:9, second:8. 9+8=17, plus carryover 1=18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\nFirst number:1, second:9. 1+9=10, plus carryover 1=11. So write 1 and carryover 1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\nFirst number:4, second:8. 4+8=12, plus carryover 1=13. So here we have 13. Since this is the highest place, we just write 13.\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nStarting from position1 (ones) to position9 (hundred millions):.\n\nPositions:.\n\n1:4.\n\n2:9.\n\n3:3.\n\n4:0 (with carryover 1).\n\n5:4 (with carryover 1).\n\n6:5 (with carryover1).\n\n7:8 (carryover1).\n\n8:1 (carryover1).\n\n9:3 (since 13, but the 1 is carried over? Wait, hold on. Wait, the hundred millions place was 4+8+1=13, so actually, the digit in the hundred millions place is 3, and we have a carryover to a new billion place. Wait, but since both numbers are 9 digits, adding them could result in a 10-digit number. Hmm, I didn't consider that. Let me check again.\n\nWait, when adding the hundred millions place (position9):.\n\n4 (from first) +8 (second) +1 (carryover from position8) =13. So the digit here is 3, and we carryover 1 to the billions place (which is a new digit beyond the original numbers). So the total would have a 1 in the billions place, followed by the 3 in hundred millions, etc.\n\nTherefore, the total sum would be 1,3.. so the digits would be:.\n\nPosition10 (billions):1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):3.\n\nThen the rest as per before.\n\nWait, so putting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the highest digit (billions place):.\n\n1 (from the carryover).\n\nThen the hundred millions place is 3,.\n\nten millions:1,.\n\nmillions:8,.\n\nhundred thousands:5,.\n\nten thousands:4,.\n\nthousands:0,.\n\nhundreds:3,.\n\ntens:9,.\n\nones:4.\n\nWait, let me list all the digits from position10 down to position1:.\n\nPosition10:1.\n\nPosition9:3.\n\nPosition8:1.\n\nPosition7:8.\n\nPosition6:5.\n\nPosition5:4.\n\nPosition4:0.\n\nPosition3:3.\n\nPosition2:9.\n\nPosition1:4</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
