er).\n\nPosition3:7.\n\nPosition4:7.\n\nPosition5:7.\n\nPosition6:3.\n\nPosition7:9.\n\nPosition8:8.\n\nPosition9:0.\n\nPosition10:1.\n\nSo when constructing the number, starting from the highest digit (position10):\n\nPosition10:1 (billions),\n\nPosition9:0 (hundred millions),\n\nPosition8:8 (ten millions),\n\nPosition7:9 (millions),\n\nPosition6:3 (hundred thousands),\n\nPosition5:7 (ten thousands),\n\nPosition4:7 (thousands),\n\nPosition3:7 (hundreds),\n\nPosition2:2 (tens),\n\nPosition1:4 (ones).\n\nSo that's 1,089,377,724. But that contradicts the correct answer of 1,080,377,724. So there must be an error in the digit-by-digit calculation.\n\nWait, let's recalculate the digit-by-digit steps carefully:\n\nStarting over:\n\nFirst number:345,631,564.\n\nDigits (from right):\n\n1:4\n\n2:6\n\n3:5\n\n4:1\n\n5:3\n\n6:6\n\n7:5 (millions)\n\n8:4 (ten millions)\n\n9:3 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number:734,746,160.\n\nDigits:\n\n1:0\n\n2:6\n\n3:1\n\n4:6\n\n5:4\n\n6:7\n\n7:3 (millions)\n\n8:4 (ten millions)\n\n9:7 (hundred millions)\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nPosition1:4+0=4 →4, carry0.\n\nPosition2:6+6=12 →2, carry1.\n\nPosition3:5+1=6 +1=7, carry0.\n\nPosition4:1+6=7, carry0.\n\nPosition5:3+4=7, carry0.\n\nPosition6:6+7=13 →3, carry1.\n\nPosition7:5 (first's millions) +3 (second's millions) +1 (carry)=9 →9, carry0.\n\nPosition8:4 (first's ten millions) +4 (second's ten millions)=8 →8, carry0.\n\nPosition9:3 (first's hundred millions) +7 (second's hundred millions)=10 →0, carry1.\n\nPosition10: carry1 →1.\n\nThus, the digits are:\n\nPosition10:1,\n\nPosition9:0,\n\nPosition8:8,\n\nPosition7:9,\n\nPosition6:3,\n\nPosition5:7,\n\nPosition4:7,\n\nPosition3:7,\n\nPosition2:2,\n\nPosition1:4.\n\nSo the number is indeed 1,089,377,724. But when I did the other method, I got 1,080,377,724. There's a discrepancy here. Which is correct?\n\nWait let me compute using another method:\n\n345,631,564 + 734,746,160.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n345,631,564\n\n+734,746,160\n\nAdd the millions:\n\n345,631,564 is 345 million + 631,564.\n\n734,746,160 is 734 million +746,160.\n\nAdding millions: 345 +734 = 1,079 million.\n\nAdding the remaining parts:631,564 +746,160.\n\n631,564 +746,160:\n\n600,000 +700,000 =1,300,000.\n\n31,564 +46,160 =77,724.\n\nTotal:1,300,000 +77,724=1,377,724.\n\nThus total sum is 1,079,000,000 +1,377,724 =1,080,377,724.\n\nAh! So the error is in the digit-by-digit approach. Where did that go wrong?\n\nLooking back at the digit-by-digit:\n\nPosition7 (millions place) was calculated as 9, which would mean that the millions digit is 9, contributing 9,000,000.\n\nBut according to the correct total, the millions digit should be 0, since the total is 1,080,377,724. Breaking this down:\n\n1,080,377,724 is 1,000,000,000 +80,377,724.\n\n80,377,724 has:\n\n80,000,000 (ten millions place) + 377,724.\n\nWait, let's parse the correct answer:\n\n1,080,377,724:\n\n- Billions place:1.\n\n- Hundred millions:0.\n\n- Ten millions:8.\n\n- Millions:0? Wait no:\n\nWait 1,080,377,724:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n1,000,000,000 (1 billion),\n\n80,000,000 (8 ten millions),\n\n300,000 (3 hundred thousands),\n\n70,000 (7 ten thousands),\n\n7,000 (7 thousands),\n\n700 (7 hundreds),\n\n70 (7 tens),\n\n24 (ones).\n\nWait actually, let's write each digit:\n\n1,080,377,724:\n\nDigits from left to right (billions to ones):\n\n1 (billion),\n\n0 (hundred millions),\n\n8 (ten millions),\n\n0 (millions),\n\n3 (hundred thousands),\n\n7 (ten thousands),\n\n7 (thousands),\n\n7 (hundreds),\n\n2 (tens),\n\n4 (ones).\n\nAh! So the millions place is 0, but in the digit-by-digit calculation, it was 9. That's the mistake.\n\nSo where did the error occur in the digit-by-digit steps?\n\nLooking back at position7 (millions place):\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 5 (from 345,631,564: the millions digit is the 7th digit from the right, which is 5).\n\nSecond number's millions digit is 3 (from 734,746,160: the millions digit is the 7th from right, which is 3).\n\nAdding those gives 5