      "text": "1,133,073,157. Wait, but let me see: 378,659,453 +755,413,704.\n\nAlternatively, 378,659,453 +755,413,704 = (300,000,000 +75,659,453) + (700,000,000 +55,413,704) = 1,000,000,000 + (75,659,453 +55,413,704).\n\n75,659,453 +55,413,704 = 131,073,157.\n\nSo total is 1,000,000,000 +131,073,157 =1,131,073,157? Wait, that's conflicting with previous result. Hmm, now I'm confused. Wait, perhaps my breakdown is wrong.\n\nWait let's recalculate:\n\n378,659,453 +755,413,704.\n\nLet me do this step by step:\n\n378,659,453.\n\n+755,413,704.\n\nAdding the millions:\n\nFirst, 378,659,453 is 3 hundred millions, 7 ten millions, 8 millions, 6 hundred thousands, 5 ten thousands, 9 thousands, 4 hundreds, 5 tens, 3 ones.\n\n755,413,704 is 7 hundred millions, 5 ten millions, 5 millions, 4 hundred thousands, 1 ten thousands, 3 thousands, 7 hundreds, 0 tens,4 ones.\n\nAdding each column:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:3+4=7.\n\nTens:5+0=5.\n\nHundreds:4+7=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nThousands:9+3=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:5+1=6 +1=7 \u21927, no carryover.\n\nHundred thousands:6+4=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nMillions:8+5=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nTen millions:7+5=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:3+7=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nBillions: carryover1 \u21921.\n\nSo the total is 1,133,073,157. But when I broke it down earlier, I had an error in the breakdown. The 378,659,453 +755,413,704.\n\nWait, 378,659,453 + 700,000,000 is 1,078,659,453. Then add 55,413,704 to that.\n\n1,078,659,453 +55,413,704:\n\nAdding 50,000,000 gives 1,128,659,453. Then add 5,413,704: 1,128,659,453 +5,413,704 = 1,134,073,157? Wait, maybe I made a miscalculation here. Wait 1,078,659,453 plus 55,413,704:\n\nLet's add 1,078,659,453 + 55,413,704:\n\nAdd 1,078,659,453 + 50,000,000 = 1,128,659,453.\n\nThen add 5,413,704 to that:\n\n1,128,659,453 + 5,413,704 = ?\n\n1,128,659,453 +5,000,000 =1,133,659,453.\n\nThen add 413,704: 1,133,659,453 +413,704 = 1,134,073,157.\n\nWait, but this contradicts the previous digit-by-digit result. Hmm. There must be a mistake here. Let me check again.\n\nWait, original numbers:\n\n378,659,453.\n\n+755,413,704.\n\nLet me add them as numbers:\n\n378,659,453.\n\n+755,413,704.\n\nStart adding:\n\n3 +4 =7 (units).\n\n5 +0=5 (tens).\n\n4 +7=11 \u21921 (hundreds), carryover1.\n\n9 +3 +1=13 \u21923 (thousands), carryover1.\n\n5 +1 +1=7 (ten thousands).\n\n6 +4=10 \u21920 (hundred thousands), carryover1.\n\n8 +5 +1=14 \u21924 (millions), carryover1.\n\n7 +5 +1=13 \u21923 (ten millions), carryover1.\n\n3 +7 +1=11 \u21921 (hundred millions), carryover1.\n\nThen the carryover1 for billions.\n\nSo the total is 1,133,073,157. But when I broke into parts, I had 1,134,073,157. There's a discrepancy. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let me do the part breakdown again carefully:\n\n378,659,453 +755,413,704.\n\nLet me split both numbers into their components.\n\n378,659,453 is 300,000,000 + 70,000,000 + 8,000,000 + 600,000 + 50,000 + 9,000 + 400 + 50 +3.\n\n755,413,704 is 700,000,000 +50,000,000 +5,000,000 +400,000 +10,000 +3,000 +700 +0 +4.\n\nAdding each part:\n\nHundred millions: 300M +700M =1,000M.\n\nTen millions:70M +50M=120M.\n\nMillions:8M +5M=13M.\n\nHundred thousands:600k +400k=1,000k =1M.\n\nTen thousands:50k +10k=60k.\n\nThousands:9k +3k=12k.\n\nHundreds:400 +700=1,100.\n\nTens:50 +0=50.\n\nOnes:3 +4=7.\n\nNow adding all these:\n\nStart with 1,000,000,000.\n\n+120,000,000 \u21921,120,000,000.\n\n+13,000,000 \u21921,133,000,000.\n\n+1,000,000 \u21921,134,000,000.\n\n+60,000 \u21921,134,060,000.\n\n+12,000 \u21921,134,072,000.\n\n+1,100 \u21921,134,073,100.\n\n+50 \u21921,134,073,150.\n\n+7 \u21921,134,073,157.\n\nWait, so this gives 1,134,073,157. But the digit-by-digit addition gave 1,133,073,157. There's a difference in the millions place. So I must have messed up in one of the methods.\n\nLet me check digit-by-digit again:\n\nLet me write both numbers aligned:\n\n      3 7 8 6 5 9 4 5 3\n\n    +7 5 5 4 1 3 7 0 4\n\nLet me write them properly with commas:\n\nFirst number: 378,659,453 \u2192 digits from right (position 1 to 9):\n\nPositions:\n\n1 (units):3\n\n2 (tens):5\n\n3 (hundreds):4\n\n4 (thousands):9\n\n5 (ten thousands):5\n\n6 (hundred thousands):6\n\n7 (millions):8\n\n8 (ten millions):7\n\n9 (hundred millions):3\n\nSecond number:755,413,704 \u2192 digits:\n\n1 (units):4\n\n2 (tens):0\n\n3 (hundreds):7\n\n4 (thousands):3\n\n5 (ten thousands):1\n\n6 (hundred thousands):4\n\n7 (millions):5\n\n8 (ten millions):5\n\n9 (hundred millions):7\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nPosition 1: 3 +4=7.\n\nPosition2:5+0=5.\n\nPosition3:4+7=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nPosition4:9+3 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nPosition5:5+1 +1=7 \u21927, no carryover.\n\nPosition6:6+4=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nPosition7:8+5 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nPosition8:7+5 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nPosition9:3+7 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nThen the carryover1 for the next digit (billions place):1.\n\nSo the digits from position1 to 10:\n\nPosition10 (billions):1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3.\n\nPosition7 (millions):4.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):5.\n\nPosition1 (units):7.\n\nSo the number is:\n\n1 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 1 (hundreds),5 (tens),7 (units).\n\nWait, so that would be 1,134,073,157? Wait, let me write it as:\n\n1 (billion) \u2192 1,000,000,000.\n\nThen 1 (hundred millions) \u2192 100,000,000 \u2192 total so far 1,100,000,000.\n\n3 (ten millions) \u219230,000,000 \u2192 1,130,000,000.\n\n4 (millions) \u21924,000,000 \u21921,134,000,000.\n\n0 (hundred thousands) \u21920 \u2192 stays.\n\n7 (ten thousands) \u219270,000 \u21921,134,070,000.\n\n3 (thousands) \u21923,000 \u21921,134,073,000.\n\n1 (hundreds) \u2192100 \u21921,134,073,100.\n\n5 (tens) \u219250 \u21921,134,073,150.\n\n7 (units) \u21927 \u21921,134,073,157.\n\nAh! So the digit-by-digit was actually correct, but when I first did the digit breakdown, I must have miscounted the places. So the correct total is 1,134,073,157. Wait, but in my first digit-by-digit addition I thought I got 1,133,073,157. Where did I go wrong there?\n\nLooking back at the first digit-by-digit step-by-step:\n\nAfter position7 (millions place):\n\n8 (from first number) +5 (from second) +1 (carryover) =14 \u21924 and carryover1. That's correct.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):7 +5 +1 (carryover) =13 \u21923 and carryover1. Correct.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):3 +7 +1 (carryover) =11 \u21921 and carryover1. So that's 1 in the hundred millions place, and carryover to billions. So the hundred millions digit is 1, but the ten millions digit is 3, so putting it all together:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:1\n\nTen millions:3\n\nMillions:4 (from position7)\n\nHundred thousands:0 (position6)\n\nTen thousands:7 (position5)\n\nThousands:3 (position4)\n\nHundreds:1 (position3)\n\nTens:5 (position2)\n\nOnes:7 (position1)\n\nTherefore, the number is 1,134,073,157. So earlier when I thought I had 1,133,073,157, that was a mistake in the hundreds place? Wait no, let's see:\n\nWait when I first wrote the digits from right to left, I might have misordered the places. Let me list all digits from highest to lowest:\n\nBillions place:1\n\nHundred millions:1\n\nTen millions:3\n\nMillions:4\n\nHundred thousands:0\n\nTen thousands:7\n\nThousands:3\n\nHundreds:1\n\nTens:5\n\nOnes:7\n\nSo written as 1,134,073,157. Yes. So my initial digit-by-digit was correct but I made an error when I first thought the answer was 1,133,073,157. Probably a typo. So the correct answer is 1,134,073,157.\n\nWait, but when I first broke it down as 378,659,453 +755,413,704, adding 378,659,453 +700,000,000 gives 1,078,659,453. Then adding the remaining 55,413,704:\n\n1,078,659,453 +55,413,704 = ?\n\nLet me compute this step carefully:\n\n1,078,659,453\n\n+ 55,413,704\n\n-------------\n\nAdding the numbers:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n3 +4=7.\n\n5+0=5.\n\n4+7=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\n9+3+1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\n5+1+1=7.\n\n6+4=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\n8+5 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\n7+5 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\n0 (since first number has no digit beyond hundred millions) +5 (from second's hundred millions place?) Wait, perhaps better:\n\nWait 1,078,659,453 is 1,078,659,453\n\n+55,413,704 is 55,413,704.\n\nAlign them:\n\n1,078,659,453\n\n+ \u00a0\u00a055,413,704\n\n= 1,078,659,453 + 55,413,704.\n\nAdding digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:3 +4=7.\n\nTens:5+0=5.\n\nHundreds:4+7=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nThousands:9+3 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:5+1 +1=7.\n\nHundred thousands:6+4=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nMillions:8+5 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nTen millions:7 (from first number's ten millions place) +5 (from second's ten millions place) +1 (carryover)=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:0 (since first number's hundred millions is part of the 1,078 million?) Wait, perhaps I'm getting confused here.\n\nWait, 1,078,659,453 is structured as:\n\n1 (billion), 0 (hundred millions?), 7 (ten millions?), 8 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands),5 (ten thousands),9 (thousands),4 (hundreds),5 (tens),3 (ones).\n\nWait no, actually 1,078,659,453 is 1,078,659,453.\n\nBreaking down:\n\nDigits:\n\n1 (billions place),\n\n0 (hundred millions),\n\n7 (ten millions),\n\n8 (millions),\n\n6 (hundred thousands),\n\n5 (ten thousands),\n\n9 (thousands),\n\n4 (hundreds),\n\n5 (tens),\n\n3 (ones).\n\nAdding 55,413,704 which is:\n\n0 (billions),\n\n5 (hundred millions),\n\n5 (ten millions),\n\n4 (millions),\n\n1 (hundred thousands),\n\n3 (ten thousands),\n\n7 (thousands),\n\n0 (hundreds),\n\n4 (tens),\n\n?\n\nWait actually:\n\n55,413,704 is 5 (crore?), but in the US numbering:\n\n55,413,704 is 50,000,000 +5,000,000 +400,000 +10,000 +3,000 +700 +0