osition5:1 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:4 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:5 (millions)\n\nPosition8:5 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:0 (hundred millions?) Wait, 55 million has no hundred millions. Wait, 55,413,704 is less than 100 million, so the hundred millions digit is 0.\n\nWait, actually, 55,413,704 is a 8-digit number? No, it's 8 digits: 55,413,704 \u2192 8 digits? No, 55,413,704 has 8 digits? Let's count:\n\n5 (1),5 (2),4 (3),1 (4),3 (5),7 (6),0 (7),4 (8). Yes, 8 digits. So it's up to ten millions place. The hundred millions digit is 0.\n\nTherefore, when adding to 1,078,659,453 (which is 1,078,659,453 \u2192 10 digits: 1,0,7,8,6,5,9,4,5,3):\n\nAdding 55,413,704 (8 digits) to 1,078,659,453:\n\nThe sum will be:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition1:3+4=7.\n\nPosition2:5+0=5.\n\nPosition3:4+7=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nPosition4:9+3+1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nPosition5:5+1+1=7.\n\nPosition6:6+4=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nPosition7:8+5 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nPosition8:7 (from first number's ten millions) +5 (from second's ten millions) +1 (carryover) \u21927+5=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nPosition9:0 (hundred millions of first number? Wait first number is 1,078,659,453 \u2192 its hundred millions digit is 0 (since it's 1,078 million). The second number's hundred millions digit is 0 (since it's 55 million). So adding 0+0 +1 (carryover from position8) \u21921.\n\nPosition10: The first number's billions digit is 1, and the second has 0. So 1+0=1.\n\nThus the total is:\n\nPosition10:1\n\nPosition9:1\n\nPosition8:3\n\nPosition7:4\n\nPosition6:0\n\nPosition5:7\n\nPosition4:3\n\nPosition3:1\n\nPosition2:5\n\nPosition1:7.\n\nWhich gives 1,134,073,157. So that's consistent. Therefore the correct answer is 1,134,073,157.\n\nBut in my first digit-by-digit calculation, I thought I had 1,133,073,157 but that was a mistake in transcribing the digits. So the correct sum is 1,134,073,157.\n\nTherefore, the final answer should be 1,134,073,157.\n</think>\n\n",
