sands).\n6: 3 (hundred thousands).\n7: 9 (millions).\n8: 6 (ten millions).\n9: 3 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number: 993734297.\nPositions (right to left):.\n1: 7 (ones).\n2: 9 (tens).\n3: 2 (hundreds).\n4: 4 (thousands).\n5: 3 (ten thousands).\n6: 7 (hundred thousands).\n7: 3 (millions).\n8: 9 (ten millions).\n9: 9 (hundred millions).\n\nSo adding digit by digit:.\n\nStarting at position 1 (ones): 6 +7 =13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 3 +9 =12 +1=13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):7 +2=9 +1=10. Write 0, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):2 +4=6 +1=7. Write7, carryover0.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):8 +3=11. So write 1, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):3 +7=10 +1=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):9 +3=12 +1=13. Write3, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):6 +9=15 +1=16. Write6, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):3 +9=12 +1=13. Write3, carryover1. Since there are no more digits, the carryover 1 becomes a new digit at the next place (billions).\n\nSo putting all together, starting from the right:.\n\nThe digits are:.\n\nAfter each position:.\n\n1:3, 2:3,3:0,4:7,5:1,6:1,7:3,8:6,9:3, then the final carryover 1. So the total digits from right to left would be 1 (carryover) followed by 3,6,3,1,1,7,0,3,3. Wait, maybe I need to reconstruct the number properly.\n\nWait, let's list all the digits with their positions:.\n\nStarting from the first digit (ones place):.\n\nPosition1:3 (with a carryover 1 to next).\n\nPosition2:3 (carryover1).\n\nPosition3:0 (carryover1).\n\nPosition4:7 (no carryover).\n\nPosition5:1 (carryover1).\n\nPosition6:1 (carryover1).\n\nPosition7:3 (carryover1).\n\nPosition8:6 (carryover1).\n\nPosition9:3 (carryover1).\n\nAnd then the final carryover 1.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:.\n\nPosition1:3.\n\nPosition2:3.\n\nPosition3:0.\n\nPosition4:7.\n\nPosition5:1.\n\nPosition6:1.\n\nPosition7:3.\n\nPosition8:6.\n\nPosition9:3.\n\nPlus the final 1 at the billion place.\n\nSo writing the number from left to right (starting with the highest place):.\n\nThe carryover 1 is the billions place, then the next digits are 3 (hundred millions) + .. Wait, let me think again.\n\nWait, the positions:.\n\nThe first digit (rightmost) is ones (position1). The next is tens (position2), up to position9 which is hundred millions, and then the carryover is billions (position10).\n\nSo the digits in order from right to left are:.\n\nPosition1:3 (ones).\n\nPosition2:3 (tens).\n\nPosition3:0 (hundreds).\n\nPosition4:7 (thousands).\n\nPosition5:1 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition6:1 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition7:3 (millions).\n\nPosition8:6 (ten millions).\n\nPosition9:3 (hundred millions).\n\nPlus the carryover 1 (billions).\n\nSo the final number is:.\n\nBillions place:1.\n\nHundred millions:3 (from position9 plus carryover?).\n\nWait, no. Wait the position9 was the original hundred millions digits (3 +9) plus carryover, which was 3 +9 =12 plus carryover1 from position8? Wait, let me check again step by step:.\n\nWait let me retrace each step carefully.\n\nStarting over:.\n\nAdding digit by digit from right to left (starting at ones place):.\n\n1. Ones place: 6 +7 =13. Record 3, carryover 1.\n\n2. Tens place: 3 +9 =12 +1=13. Record 3, carryover1.\n\n3. Hundreds place:7 +2 =9 +1=10. Record0, carryover1.\n\n4. Thousands place:2 +4 =6 +1=7. Record7, carryover0.\n\n5. Ten thousands place:8 +3 =11 +0=11. Record1, carryover1.\n\n6. Hundred thousands place:3 +7 =10 +1=11. Record1, carryover1.\n\n7. Millions place:9 +3 =12 +1=13. Record3, carryover1.\n\n8. Ten millions place:6 +9 =15 +1=16. Record6, carryover1.\n\n9. Hundred millions place:3 +9 =12 +1=13. Record3, carryover1.\n\nNow, after the hundred millions place, we have a carryover of 1. Since there are no more digits, this carryover becomes the next digit (billions place), so we add a 1 there.\n\nSo the digits from right to left (starting with ones) are:.\n\n3 (ones), 3 (tens), 0 (hundreds), 7 (thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 1 (hundred thousands), 3 (millions), 6 (ten millions), 3 (hundred millions), and 1 (billions).\n\nSo arranging them from left to right (billions down to ones):.\n\n1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 3 (ones).\n\nWait let me list them properly:.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:3.\n\nTen millions:6.\n\nMillions:3.\n\nHundred thousands:1.\n\nTen thousands:1.\n\nThousands:7.\n\nHundreds:0.\n\nTens:3.\n\nOnes:3.\n\nSo putting it all together:</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
