ce:** Now, first number has 6, second has 3. 6 + 3 = 9. Easy, so 9 here.\n\n8. **Ten-millions place:** First number has 6, second has 2. 6 + 2 = 8. That's straightforward.\n\n9. **Hundred-millions place:** Finally, the first digit. The first number has 4, and the second has 2. 4 + 2 = 6. So that's the last digit.\n\nWait a second, let me check each step again to make sure I didn't mix up any digits. Let me go through again step by step with the actual numbers written out.\n\nBreaking down each digit position:.\n\nLet me write the numbers with their place values:.\n\nFirst number: 4 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 2 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 1 (ones).\n\nWait, hold on, maybe I misaligned the digits earlier? Let me confirm:.\n\nWait, actually, let me write each digit position properly.\n\nLet me write both numbers with commas to see:.\n\n466,035,865 is:.\n\nBreaking down each digit from right to left (since numbers are written left to right but addition starts from the right):.\n\nPositions (from right, starting at 1):.\n\n1 (ones): 5.\n2 (tens): 6.\n3 (hundreds): 8.\n4 (thousands): 5.\n5 (ten thousands): 3.\n6 (hundred thousands): 0.\n7 (millions): 6.\n8 (ten millions): 6.\n9 (hundred millions):4.\n\nSecond number 223,146,761:.\n\n1 (ones):1.\n2 (tens):6.\n3 (hundreds):7.\n4 (thousands):6.\n5 (ten thousands):4.\n6 (hundred thousands):1.\n7 (millions):3.\n8 (ten millions):2.\n9 (hundred millions):2.\n\nWait, so when adding digit by digit, starting from the ones:.\n\nPosition 1: 5 + 1 = 6 (no carryover).\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 6 +6 =12 → 2, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):8 +7 =15, plus 1 carried →16 →6, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):5 +6=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3 +4=7 +1=8 → no carryover.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0 +1=1 → no carryover.\n\nPosition7 (millions):6 +3=9.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):6 +2=8.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):4 +2=6.\n\nSo putting all together the digits from right to left:.\n\nDigits are:.\n\nPosition1:6.\n\nPosition2:2.\n\nPosition3:6.\n\nPosition4:2.\n\nPosition5:8.\n\nPosition6:1.\n\nPosition7:9.\n\nPosition8:8.\n\nPosition9:6.\n\nSo combining these digits from highest to lowest (left to right):.\n\n6 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 6 (ones). Wait, hold on, maybe I need to list them in order.\n\nWait, the digits we obtained are:.\n\nStarting from the first digit (hundred millions place):.\n\nPosition9:6.\n\nPosition8:8.\n\nPosition7:9.\n\nPosition6:1.\n\nPosition5:8.\n\nPosition4:2.\n\nPosition3:6.\n\nPosition2:2.\n\nPosition1:6.\n\nSo the number is 6 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 6 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 96%|#########5| 479/500 [11:53:16<34:22, 98.21s/it] 
