messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 211964427 and 444376874?\n# Reasoning:", "<think>\nOkay, let's tackle this addition problem. The numbers are 211,964,427 and 444,376,874. Hmm, I need to add them digit by digit starting from the right, right?.\n\nFirst, I'll line them up to make sure the places are correct. Let me write them down:.\n\n211964427.\n+444376874.\n----------.\n\nStarting with the ones place: the last digit of both numbers. 7 + 4 equals 11. So I write down 1 and carry over 1. Wait, actually, the ones digits are 7 and 4. 7+4=11. So ones place is 1, carryover 1.\n\nNext, the tens place: the next digits are 2 and 7. 2+7=9, plus the carried 1 makes 10. So tens place is 0, carryover 1 again.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 4 and 8. 4+8=12, plus the 1 carried over is 13. So hundreds digit is 3, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place: 6 and 6. 6+6=12, plus 1 is 13. So thousands digit is 3, carryover 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: 4 and 7. 4+7=11, plus 1 is 12. So ten-thousands is 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 9 and 3. 9+3=12, plus 1 is 13. So hundred-thousands digit is 3, carryover 1.\n\nMillions place: 1 and 4. 1+4=5, plus 1 is 6. So millions digit is 6, no carryover.\n\nTen-millions place: 1 and 4. 1+4=5. So ten-millions is 5.\n\nHundred-millions place: 2 and 4. 2+4=6. So hundred-millions is 6.\n\nPutting all together, the digits from right to left (since we started at ones) would be 1, 0, 3, 3, 2, 3, 6, 5, 6. Wait, let me check that again.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the order. Let me list each step properly.\n\nWait, maybe it's better to do each digit step by step with their places:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):.\n\n1. Ones place: 7 + 4 = 11 → 1, carryover 1.\n2. Tens place: 2 + 7 = 9 + 1 (carryover) = 10 → 0, carryover 1.\n3. Hundreds place: 4 + 8 = 12 + 1 =13 → 3, carryover 1.\n4. Thousands place: 6 + 6 =12 +1 =13 →3, carryover1.\n5. Ten-thousands:4 +7=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n6. Hundred-thousands:9 +3=12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n7. Millions:1 (from first number) +4 (from second) =5 +1=6 →6, no carryover.\n8. Ten-millions:1 +4 =5.\n9. Hundred-millions:2 +4=6.\n\nWait, but when I list them in order from ones to hundred-millions, the digits would be:.\n\nOnes:1.\n\nTens:0.\n\nHundreds:3.\n\nThousands:3.\n\nTen-thousands:2.\n\nHundred-thousands:3.\n\nMillions:6.\n\nTen-millions:5.\n\nHundred-millions:6.\n\nSo writing the number from highest to lowest place, it's 6 (hundred-millions), 5 (ten-millions), 6 (millions), 3 (hundred-thousands), 2 (ten-thousands), 3 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 1 (ones). Wait, but that would be 656,323,301?.\n\nWait, let me check with actual addition:.\n\n211,964,427.\n\n+444,376,874.\n\nLet me add them step by step:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nFirst digit (units):.\n\n7 +4 =11 → 1, carryover 1.\n\nSecond digit (tens):.\n\n2 +7 =9 +1 =10 →0, carryover1.\n\nThird (hundreds):.\n\n4 +8 =12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nFourth (thousands):.\n\n6 +6 =12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nFifth (ten thousands):.\n\n4 +7 =11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nSixth (hundred thousands):.\n\n9 +3 =12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nSeventh (millions):.\n\n1 +4 =5 +1=6 →6.\n\nEighth (ten millions):.\n\n1 +4 =5.\n\nNinth (hundred millions):.\n\n2 +4 =6.\n\nSo putting it all together:.\n\nThe digits from right to left are 1 (units), 0 (tens), 3 (hundreds), 3 (thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 3 (hundred thousands), 6 (millions), 5 (ten millions), 6 (hundred millions). So when written left to right, it's 656,323,301.\n\nWait, let me confirm with another method. Maybe adding the numbers as whole:.\n\n211,964,427 + 444,376,874.\n\nLet me break them