\n\nFirst number: 395,520,983.\nBreaking down:.\n- Billions: 3.\n- Hundred millions: 9.\n- Ten millions: 5.\n- Millions: 5.\n- Hundred thousands: 2.\n- Ten thousands: 0.\n- Thousands: 9.\n- Hundreds: 8.\n- Tens: 8.\n- Ones: 3.\n\nWait, no, actually, let's parse it properly. 395,520,983 is 3 (hundred millions?), no wait, commas separate into 3,95,520,983? Wait, maybe I should use the standard place values. Let me clarify the digits properly. .\n\nWait, maybe it's better to write both numbers digit by digit from right to left to ensure alignment.\n\nNumber1: 395520983.\n\nLet's count the digits: 9 digits. Starting from the right (ones place):.\n\nPosition: 1 (ones): 3.\n\n2 (tens): 8.\n\n3 (hundreds):9.\n\n4 (thousands):0.\n\n5 (ten thousands):2.\n\n6 (hundred thousands):5.\n\n7 (millions):5.\n\n8 (ten millions):9.\n\n9 (hundred millions):3.\n\nWait, that's 395,520,983. So the digits from right to left are:.\n\n3 (1), 8 (2), 9 (3), 0 (4), 2 (5),5 (6),5 (7),9 (8),3 (9).\n\nNumber2: 794,083,008. Let's break that down similarly. 9 digits.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nPosition 1:8 (ones).\n\n2:0 (tens).\n\n3:3 (hundreds).\n\n4:0 (thousands).\n\n5:8 (ten thousands).\n\n6:0 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:4 (millions).\n\n8:9 (ten millions).\n\n9:7 (hundred millions).\n\nSo digits from right to left:.\n\n8 (1), 0 (2),3 (3),0 (4),8 (5),0 (6),4 (7),9 (8),7 (9).\n\nNow, let's add each column starting from position 1 (ones):.\n\nPosition 1: 3 +8 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 2:8 +0 =8 plus carryover 1 →9. No carryover.\n\nPosition3:9 +3=12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nPosition4:0 +0=0 plus 1 →1. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition5:2 +8=10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition6:5 +0=5 plus 1 →6. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition7:5 +4=9. No carryover.\n\nPosition8:9 +9=18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nPosition9:3 +7=10 plus carryover 1 →11. So write 1 and carryover 1. But since this is the highest digit, we add that 1 as a new digit to the left.\n\nWait, so the total digits now would be 10. Let me se