ons).\n- 4 (millions).\n- 8 (hundred thousands).\n- 6 (ten thousands).\n- 9 (thousands).\n- 4 (hundreds).\n- 9 (tens).\n- 1 (ones).\n\nSimilarly, 993,616,165:.\n\n- 9 (hundred millions).\n- 9 (ten millions).\n- 3 (millions).\n- 6 (hundred thousands).\n- 1 (ten thousands).\n- 6 (thousands).\n- 1 (hundreds).\n- 6 (tens).\n- 5 (ones).\n\nWait a second, when adding, the highest place here is the hundred millions. But when adding them, the sum might go into the billions. Let me start over step by step.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes place: 1 + 5 = 6. Write 6.\n\nTens: 9 + 6 = 15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 4 + 1 = 5 + 1 (carryover) = 6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\nThousands: 9 + 6 = 15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands: 6 + 1 = 7 + 1 (carryover) = 8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\nHundred thousands: 8 + 6 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nMillions: 4 (from first number) + 3 (from second number) = 7 + 1 (carryover) = 8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\nTen millions: 8 (first) + 9 (second) = 17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\nHundred millions: 6 (first) + 9 (second) = 15 + 1 (carryover) = 16. So write 6 here, and carryover 1. But since there are no more digits, that 1 becomes a new digit in the billions place</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
