      "text": "1,000,032,803? Wait, no, my step-by-step gives 1,060,032,803. Hmm, there must be a miscalculation in the alternative method.\n\nWait, let me do the alternative method again:\n\nStart with 789,658,324.\n\nAdd 207,374,479.\n\nBreakdown:\n\n789,658,324 + 200,000,000 = 989,658,324.\n\nThen add 7,000,000 \u2192 989,658,324 +7,000,000 = 996,658,324.\n\nAdd 300,000 \u2192 996,658,324 +300,000 = 996,958,324.\n\nAdd 74,479 \u2192 996,958,324 +74,479.\n\nLet's compute 996,958,324 +70,000 =997,028,324.\n\nThen +4,479: 997,028,324 +4,479 =997,032,803.\n\nSo total is 997,032,803? Wait, but this contradicts the previous result. Wait, where is the mistake here?\n\nWait, the second number is 207,374,479. Breaking down into 200,000,000 +7,000,000 +300,000 +74,479. That's correct. So adding step by step:\n\n789,658,324 +200,000,000 = 989,658,324.\n\nThen adding 7,000,000 gives 996,658,324.\n\nAdding 300,000: 996,658,324 + 300,000 = 996,958,324.\n\nAdding 74,479:.\n\nFirst add 70,000 \u2192 996,958,324 +70,000 =997,028,324.\n\nThen add 4,479 \u2192 997,028,324 +4,479 = 997,032,803.\n\nWait, so this gives 997,032,803. But previously, when adding digit by digit, I got 1,060,032,803. There's a discrepancy here. That means I made a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let me check the digit-by-digit addition again carefully.\n\nStarting over:\n\nFirst number: 789,658,324.\n\nSecond number: 207,374,479.\n\nAligned:\n\n        7 8 9 6 5 8 3 2 4\n\n      + 2 0 7 3 7 4 4 7 9\n\n      ---------------------\n\nLet me write them vertically with commas for clarity:\n\n789,658,324\n\n+207,374,479\n\n------------\n\nStarting from the right (units):\n\n4 +9 =13 \u2192 3, carry 1.\n\nTens place: 2 +7 =9 +1=10 \u21920, carry1.\n\nHundreds:3 +4=7 +1=8 \u21928, no carry.\n\nThousands:8 +4=12 \u21922, carry1.\n\nTen thousands:5 +7=12 +1=13 \u21923, carry1.\n\nHundred thousands:6 +3=9 +1=10 \u21920, carry1.\n\nMillions:9 +0=9 +1=10 \u21920, carry1.\n\nTen millions:8 +7=15 +1=16 \u21926, carry1.\n\nHundred millions:7 +2=9 +1=10 \u21920, carry1.\n\nBillions: carryover1 \u21921.\n\nThus the total is 1,060,032,803.\n\nWait, but in the alternative method, adding step by step I got 997,032,803. Which is different. So where is the error?\n\nAh! Wait, in the alternative method, I think I miscalculated the breakdown of the second number. Wait the second number is 207,374,479.\n\nBreaking into parts:\n\n200,000,000 +7,000,000 +300,000 +74,479.\n\nBut 200,000,000 +7,000,000 is 207,000,000. Then adding 374,479? Wait no. Wait 207,374,479 is 200,000,000 +7,000,000 + 374,479? No, that's not correct. Wait, 207,374,479 is:\n\n200,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n+7,000,000 (millions place? Wait, 207,374,479: the millions digit is the 7 in the ten millions place? Wait, let's parse the number again.\n\nWait 207,374,479 is:\n\n2 (hundred millions)\n\n0 (ten millions)\n\n7 (millions)\n\n3 (hundred thousands)\n\n7 (ten thousands)\n\n4 (thousands)\n\n4 (hundreds)\n\n7 (tens)\n\n9 (units).\n\nSo the breakdown is:\n\n200,000,000 +\n\n0 (ten millions)\n\n7,000,000 (millions)\n\n+ 300,000 (hundred thousands)\n\n+ 70,000 (ten thousands)\n\n+4,000 (thousands)\n\n+400 (hundreds)\n\n+70 (tens)\n\n+9 (units).\n\nSo adding 207,374,479 to 789,658,324:\n\nLet me do it step by step properly:\n\nStart with 789,658,324.\n\nAdd 200,000,000 \u2192 989,658,324.\n\nAdd 7,000,000 \u2192 989,658,324 +7,000,000 =996,658,324.\n\nAdd 300,000 \u2192996,658,324 +300,000 =996,958,324.\n\nAdd 70,000 \u2192996,958,324 +70,000 =997,028,324.\n\nAdd 4,000 \u2192997,028,324 +4,000 =997,032,324.\n\nAdd 400 \u2192997,032,324 +400 =997,032,724.\n\nAdd 70 \u2192997,032,724 +70 =997,032,794.\n\nAdd 9 \u2192997,032,794 +9 =997,032,803.\n\nSo this gives 997,032,803. But the digit-by-digit addition gave 1,060,032,803. These two results are conflicting. That means I made a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the digit positions in the first method. Let me check again the digit-by-digit addition.\n\nFirst number: 789,658,324.\n\nBreaking down each digit from right (units) to left (billions):\n\nPosition1 (units):4.\n\nPosition2 (tens):2.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):3.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):8.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):5.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):6.\n\nPosition7 (millions):9.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):8.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):7.\n\nSecond number:207,374,479.\n\nDigits from right:\n\nPosition1:9.\n\nPosition2:7.\n\nPosition3:4.\n\nPosition4:4.\n\nPosition5:7.\n\nPosition6:3.\n\nPosition7:0.\n\nPosition8:7.\n\nPosition9:2.\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nPosition1:4+9=13 \u21923, carry1.\n\nPosition2:2+7=9 +1=10 \u21920, carry1.\n\nPosition3:3+4=7 +0 \u21927? Wait, no, wait. Wait, after position2, the carryover is 1, so position3 is 3+4 +1? Wait no, the carryover from position2 is 1, so when we process position3, we add the digits plus any carryover from the previous step.\n\nWait, let's redo the digit-by-digit step-by-step with correct carryover:\n\nStarting over:\n\nPosition1 (units):\n\n4 (first) +9 (second) =13 \u2192 digit3, carryover1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):\n\n2 +7 =9 + carryover1=10 \u2192 digit0, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n3 +4 =7 + carryover1=8 \u2192 digit8, carryover0.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n8 +4 =12 + carryover0=12 \u2192 digit2, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n5 +7 =12 + carryover1=13 \u2192 digit3, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n6 +3 =9 + carryover1=10 \u2192 digit0, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n9 +0 =9 + carryover1=10 \u2192 digit0, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n8 +7 =15 + carryover1=16 \u2192 digit6, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n7 +2 =9 + carryover1=10 \u2192 digit0, carryover1.\n\nPosition10 (billions):\n\ncarryover1 \u2192 digit1.\n\nThus, the digits from position1 to position10 are:\n\nPosition10 (billions):1,\n\nPosition9:0,\n\nPosition8:6,\n\nPosition7:0,\n\nPosition6:0,\n\nPosition5:3,\n\nPosition4:2,\n\nPosition3:8,\n\nPosition2:0,\n\nPosition1:3.\n\nSo arranging them from billions down:\n\n1 (billions),\n\n0 (hundred millions),\n\n6 (ten millions),\n\n0 (millions),\n\n0 (hundred thousands),\n\n3 (ten thousands),\n\n2 (thousands),\n\n8 (hundreds),\n\n0 (tens),\n\n3 (units).\n\nThis forms the number: 1,060,032,803.\n\nBut the alternative method gave 997,032,803. The discrepancy is because in the alternative method, I added only 200 million instead of the correct 207 million? Wait, no, wait, the second number is 207,374,479, so when breaking down, the hundred millions digit is 2, so the first part should be 200,000,000, then the remaining is 7,374,479. So adding 200,000,000 to the first number (789,658,324) gives 989,658,324. Then adding the remaining 7,374,479.\n\nWait, 7,374,479 is 7 million plus 374,479. So adding 7,000,000 to 989,658,324 gives 996,658,324, then adding 374,479.\n\n374,479 is 300,000 +74,479.\n\nSo 996,658,324 +300,000 =996,958,324.\n\nThen adding 74,479:\n\n996,958,324 +70,000 =997,028,324.\n\nThen +4,479 \u2192997,028,324 +4,479 =997,032,803.\n\nWait, so why the difference between the two methods?\n\nAh! I see the mistake now. The first number is 789,658,324.\n\nAdding 200,000,000 gives 789,658,324 +200,000,000 = 989,658,324. Correct.\n\nThen adding the remaining 7,374,479 (which is the total of 207,374,479 minus 200,000,000).\n\nWait, 207,374,479 -200,000,000 =7,374,479. So that part is correct.\n\nBut when adding 7,374,479 to 989,658,324:\n\n989,658,324 +7,000,000 = 996,658,324. Then adding 374,479.\n\nWait, 374,479 is 300,000 +74,479.\n\nAdding 300,000 \u2192996,658,324 +300,000 =996,958,324.\n\nAdding 74,479 \u2192996,958,324 +74,479 = ?\n\nWait, 996,958,324 +70,000 =997,028,324.\n\nThen adding 4,479 gives 997,028,324 +4,479 = 997,032,803.\n\nBut according to digit-by-digit, the correct answer should be 1,060,032,803.\n\nWait, this discrepancy is because in the alternative method I added only 207,000,000 plus the rest, but the second number is 207,374,479. Wait, no. Wait, the first number is 789,658,324. Adding the second number 207,374,479:\n\nTotal should be 789,658,324 +207,374,479 = ?\n\nLet me compute using another method:.\n\n789,658,324 + 200,000,000 = 989,658,324.\n\nThen adding 7,000,000 \u2192996,658,324.\n\nAdding 300,000 \u2192996,958,324.\n\nAdding 74,479 \u2192996,958,324 +74,479.\n\nWait, 996,958,324 +74,479:\n\nBreakdown:\n\n996,958,324 +70,000 = 997,028,324.\n\nThen +4,479 \u2192997,028,324 +4,479 = 997,032,803.\n\nBut according to digit-by-digit, it's 1,060,032,803.\n\nWait a second, there's a huge difference here. 1,060 million vs 997 million. That suggests a miscalculation in one of the methods. Let me do a direct addition with smaller numbers to check.\n\nWait, perhaps I misread the original numbers.\n\nWait the first number is 789,658,324.\n\nThe second number is 207,374,479.\n\nLet me add them digit by digit again carefully:\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n          7 8 9 6 5 8 3 2 4\n\n        + 2 0 7 3 7 4 4 7 9\n\n        -------------------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n4 +9 =13 \u2192 3, carry 1.\n\nNext digit (tens):\n\n2 +7 =9 +1=10 \u21920, carry1.\n\nNext (hundreds):\n\n3 +4 =7 +1=8 \u21928, no carry.\n\nNext (thousands):\n\n8 +4 =12 \u21922, carry1.\n\nNext (ten thousands):\n\n5 +7 =12 +1=13 \u21923, carry1.\n\nNext (hundred thousands):\n\n6 +3 =9 +1=10 \u21920, carry1.\n\nNext (millions):\n\n9 +0 =9 +1=10 \u21920, carry1.\n\nNext (ten millions):\n\n8 +7 =15 +1=16 \u21926, carry1.\n\nNext (hundred millions):\n\n7 +2 =9 +1=10 \u21920, carry1.\n\nFinally, the carryover 1 becomes the billions digit.\n\nSo the number is 1,060,032,803.\n\nBut in the alternative method I got 997,032,803. Wait, why is there a difference?\n\nAh! I see now! In the alternative method, I added the first number (789,658,324) and the second number (207,374,479) by breaking the second into 200,000,000 plus 7,374,479. That's correct, but when adding 200,000,000 to the first number, I did:\n\n789,658,324 +200,00